T1  - Deep Clinical Phenotyping Enhances Robustness of Prognostic Prediction to Next Level in Sepsis
A1  - Yang H.
A1  - Zhang C.
A1  - Zong H.
A1  - Wu R.
A1  - Zhou Y.
A1  - Li J.
A1  - Wu E.
A1  - Sierra A.P.
A1  - Shen B.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background: Accurate prognostic prediction for sepsis remains a challenge in critical care due to high heterogeneity and the limited robustness of existing models. Current statistical approaches often fail to deliver reliable predictions, while artificial intelligence (AI) models struggle with interpretability, hindering their widespread adoption.<br><br>Objective: Our study seeks to develop a robust, interpretable sepsis prognostic model through explainable AI (XAI)-driven deep phenotyping from multi-center real-world data.<br><br>Methods: We retrospectively collected data from sepsis patients at West China Hospital, Sichuan University from November 2020 to December 2023. Explainable Boosting Machine (EBM) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) were used for prognostic prediction, with predictive accuracy and classification performance reported. Shape functions in EBM and Integrated Gradients (IG) for LSTM were applied to assess feature importance and model interpretability. Deep clinical phenotyping was conducted to identify comprehensive prognostic signature, and external validation was performed with the MIMIC-IV dataset and data from Chengdu Shangjin Nanfu Hospital to assess model robustness.<br><br>Results: The West China cohort included 3,461 sepsis patients, with 863 non-survivors and 2,598 survivors. LSTM achieved 81.10% accuracy and AUC of 0.78, while EBM achieved 76.20% accuracy and AUC of 0.73, outperforming other prognostic models for sepsis by approximately 3.40% in terms of accuracy. Through deep phenotyping, 42 features were identified as a systemic prognostic signature of sepsis including epidemiological characteristics, infection and inflammation markers, immune markers, organ function markers, metabolic markers, coagulation markers, level of consciousness, comorbidities, and combined markers. External validation confirmed robustness of XAI models across multi-setting data environments.<br><br>Conclusions: Our study developed an innovative sepsis prognostic model with robustness, accuracy, and interpretability by applying XAI-based deep clinical phenotyping approaches. Meanwhile, we uncovered a novel systemic prognostic signature for sepsis, addressing the challenge of single prognostic features amidst high heterogeneity. This study introduced a groundbreaking "Data-Model-Application" research paradigm for sepsis, offering valuable insights for the widespread application of precise mortality prediction and advancing personalized sepsis prognosis.Copyright © 2025, The Authors. All rights reserved.
