T1  - Development and Validation of Interpretable Machine Learning Models To Predict Intensive Care Unit Outcomes in Patients on Hemodialysis: A Multicenter Study
A1  - Chen M.
A1  - Li P.
A1  - Xu Y.
A1  - Li Z.
A1  - Xiong Y.
A1  - Wu J.
A1  - Pandya C.
A1  - Wang Y.
A1  - Huang G.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background: Patients on hemodialysis are at high-risk of intensive care unit (ICU) admission owing to elevated risks of mortality, cardiovascular disease, and infection. Existing ICU prognostic models lack accuracy and utility for this population. We aimed to identify key risk factors and develop interpretable machine learning (ML) models to predict ICU outcomes in these patients. <div> <br> </div> <div> Methods: This multicenter study included 226, 769, and 1878 patients from The First Affiliated Hospital of Sun Yat-sen University, Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care IV (MIMIC-IV), and electronic ICU Collaborative Research Database (eICU-CRD) cohorts, respectively. The primary outcome was all-cause mortality, with cardiovascular and infection-related mortality as secondary outcomes. Thirteen ML algorithms and ensemble models were applied to 113 clinical variables collected within 24 hours of ICU admission. Model performance was evaluated using the area under the curve (AUC) and compared to existing scoring systems. SHapley Additive exPlanation was used for interpretability. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Findings: Six, 11, and 25 features predicted cardiovascular, infection-related, and all-cause mortalities, respectively. NeuralNetTorch performed best for all-cause mortality (AUCs: 0.847 and 0.816 in MIMIC-IV and eICU-CRD, respectively), outperforming existing scoring models. ExtraTrees excelled for infection-related mortality (AUCs: 0.88 and 0.897), whereas CatBoost was most effective for cardiovascular mortality (AUCs: 0.772 and 0.82). An online platform based on these models was developed for clinical use. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Interpretation: ML models accurately predict ICU outcomes in patients on hemodialysis, enabling early identification of high-risk individuals and timely targeted interventions to improve outcomes. The online platform has clinical applicability. </div>Copyright © 2025, The Authors. All rights reserved.
