T1  - A Machine Learning Approach to Clinical Utilization Analysis and Health Risk Assessment Using Medicaid Data
A1  - Rahman Y.
A1  - Dua P.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Medicaid data, with its vast scale and heterogeneity, presents significant challenges in predictive modeling and healthcare analytics. This study leverages a dataset of over 6.3 million records from the Louisiana Department of Health (LDH), aiming to determine which machine learning models best predict three crucial outcomes: (i) multinomial clinical service utilization, (ii) binary COVID-19 infections, and (iii) binary tobacco use behavior.We extracted 20 key features from raw Medicaid records and implemented a rigorous preprocessing pipeline, ensuring optimal model performance. A thorough exploratory data analysis (EDA) guided feature understanding. But ultimately, all 20 features were considered to capture nuanced interactions. Seven supervised models were evaluated-logistic regression, XGBoost, AdaBoost, random forest, decision tree, artificial neural networks (ANNs), and naive bayes under rigorous hyperparameter tuning. Models were compared via accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (ROC-AUC), and computational resource usage (training time, CPU, memory).While ensemble methods such as XGBoost and random forest achieved superior predictive accuracy, their high computational demands underscore the trade-off between accuracy and efficiency in large-scale Medicaid analytics. Simpler models, such as naive bayes and decision tree, were computationally efficient but less accurate. Feature importance analyses revealed 'total length of hospital stay' as pivotal for clinical services, 'tobacco' as a key driver for COVID-19 risk, and 'COPD' as the top predictor of tobacco use. These findings underscore the critical role of health status (e.g., comorbidities) and demographic factors in shaping healthcare utilization and outcomes.By illuminating the interplay between model performance and resource requirements, this study provides actionable insights for healthcare practitioners and policymakers dealing with large, heterogeneous Medicaid datasets.Copyright © 2025, The Authors. All rights reserved.
