T1  - Comparing Epidemics Controls Through Simulations and Explainable Graph Convolution Networks
A1  - Branda F.
A1  - Ceccarelli G.
A1  - Ciccozzi M.
A1  - Scarpa F.
A1  - Lomoio U.
A1  - Chiodo F.
A1  - Veltri P.
A1  - Guzzi P.H.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - BackgroundMpox, a zoonotic disease of growing global concern, presents significant public health challenges because of the complexity of its transmission dynamics. The need for innovative tools to model and control outbreaks has become increasingly evident in the context of recent global health crises.AimsThis study aims to evaluate the utility of a Graph Convolutional Networks (k-GCN) model in the analysis and control of mpox epidemics, with a focus on transmission dynamics in the Republic of Congo and Nigeria.MethodsFive independent simulations were conducted using the k-GCN model to predict node classes in the following year, based on simulation data from a selected year. The simulations integrate graph-based contact models and epidemiological parameters to evaluate the effectiveness of control strategies, such as targeted vaccination, quarantine, and culling of infected rodents.ResultsThe results highlight the effectiveness of the k-GCN model in capturing the temporal and structural dependencies of graph-organized data. Combined strategies, including targeted vaccination, quarantine, and culling, have been shown to be the most effective in reducing human infections and the overall epidemic burden.ConclusionThe study highlights the transformative potential of combining contact-based models with explainable graphical neural networks (XAIs) for epidemic management. The proposed approach offers valuable tools for optimizing health responses and informing evidence-based decisions.Copyright © 2025, The Authors. All rights reserved.
