T1  - Evaluation of a Multi-Epitope Vaccine for Nocardia Seriolae in Micropterus Salmoides
A1  - Qin X.
A1  - Zhu L.
A1  - Liu F.
A1  - Xu J.
A1  - Su Y.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Nocardia seriolae can cause chronic granulomatous bacterial disease in fish, leading to tissue necrosis. Currently, there is no effective method to control N. seriolae disease, which has caused economic losses to the aquaculture industry. In this study, immunoinformatics tools were used to predict antigenic epitopes of seven antigenic proteins of N. seriolae, namely DnaK, GroEL, RpsA, PspA, TerD, RpIL, and Ag8L. 21 B cell epitopes and 21 T cell epitopes were predicted and combined in tandem to form a multi-epitope protein NSEP. In order to evaluate its immunogenicity, the prokaryotic expressed NSEP protein was used to immunize Miropterus salmoides. Then, the activities of lysozyme (LYZ), acid phosphatase (ACP), alkaline phosphatase (AKP), and superoxide dismutase (SOD) in the serum were significantly increased, and the transcription levels of immune related genes IL-10, IL-1 beta, TNF-alpha, IFN-gamma, IgM, and MHCI-alpha were significantly upregulated in the liver and spleen. After being challenged with N. seriolae NS01 in the NSEP group, the protective rate (RPS) was 61.29%, the tissue bacterial load was significantly decreased, and the tissue lesions were obviously alleviated. These results indicated that the multi-epitope protein NSEP can serve as a candidate subunit vaccine against N. seriolae infection in M. salmoides.Copyright © 2025, The Authors. All rights reserved.
