T1  - The Global, Regional, and National Patterns of Change in Total Burden Related to Hepatitis B in Women of Child-Bearing Age, 1990-2021: An Analysis of the Global Burden of Disease Study 2021 and Forecast to 2035
A1  - Hu Y.
A1  - Qi X.
A1  - Jiang Y.
A1  - Jiang W.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: Hepatitis B virus (HBV)-related diseases present significant challenges to global healthcare systems, with a particularly severe impact on women of childbearing age (WCBA, 15-49 years old). These conditions, including acute hepatitis B, chronic hepatitis B with cirrhosis, and liver cancer due to hepatitis B, not only severely affect patients' health but also underscore broader issues related to socioeconomic development, healthcare accessibility, and the efficiency of healthcare systems. Previous studies have been geographically limited and lacked a comprehensive global analysis. This study provides global, regional, and national data on disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) and incidence rates associated with HBV-related diseases from 1990 to 2021, with a focus on WCBA and health disparities. The objective is to offer scientific evidence to optimize resource allocation, prioritize high-burden populations, and reduce the global burden of HBV-related diseases. <div> <br> </div> <div> Methods:This study systematically evaluated the global, regional, and national burden of hepatitis B virus (HBV)-related diseases and their changes from 1990 to 2021 using the Global Burden of Diseases, Injuries, and Risk Factors Study (GBD) 2021. To conduct a more focused analysis, three specific HBV-related diseases were selected: acute hepatitis B, chronic hepatitis B with cirrhosis, and liver cancer due to hepatitis B. Disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) and incidence rates were used as the primary metrics, combined with the sociodemographic index (SDI). Analyses included the slope index of inequality and concentration index to measure health inequalities, decomposition analysis to identify drivers of disease burden changes, Joinpoint regression analysis to assess temporal trends, and the Bayesian age-period-cohort (BAPC) model to predict future disease burden trends. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Findings: The global burden of hepatitis B-related diseases in WCBA exhibited a consistent decline from 1990 to 2021, with age-standardized incidence and DALY rates showing annual percentage decreases of -1.26% and -1.52%, respectively. Acute hepatitis B accounted for the majority of new cases, while chronic liver diseases and liver cancer contributed significantly to DALYs, with pronounced disparities across SDI regions. The highest burdens were observed in low-SDI regions, such as SubSaharan Africa, whereas high-SDI regions experienced the greatest relative reductions. </div> <div> <br> <div> Decomposition analysis identified epidemiological changes as the primary driver for reductions in DALYs, counterbalancing the effects of population aging and growth. Frontier analysis revealed substantial improvement potential in low-SDI countries, while high-SDI countries demonstrated relatively effective disease management. Temporal trends assessed through Joinpoint regression and BAPC models indicated continued reductions in disease burden, with projections suggesting a global decline in age-standardized incidence rates to 494.4 per 100,000 by 2035. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Despite overall progress, inequalities persist, with low-SDI regions disproportionately burdened due to limited healthcare resources and vaccination coverage. Strategies to mitigate these disparities include targeted public health interventions, enhanced healthcare infrastructure, and comprehensive disease management programs. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Interpretation: HBV-related diseases pose a major challenge to global public health. Despite a general decline in disease burden, health inequalities remain significant, particularly in countries and territories with lower levels of development. Future public health interventions should prioritize these regions, focusing on optimizing healthcare resource allocation, improving essential health infrastructure, enhancing health education, and addressing disease burden disparities. Global collaboration and data sharing are crucial to advancing the research, prevention, and treatment of HBV-related diseases, promoting a comprehensive approach to managing these diseases and improving global health outcomes. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Funding: This research was supported by grants from the National Natural Science Foundation of China (No.82370724) , the Qingdao Key Health Discipline Development Fund and the Qingdao Key Clinical Specialty Elite Discipline project. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Declaration of Interest: All authors declare no competing interests. </div> <div> <br> </div> <div> Ethical Approval: For GBD studies, the Institutional Review Board of the University of Washington reviewed and approved a waiver of informed consent (https://www.healthdata.org/research-analysis/gbd). </div> </div>Copyright © 2024, The Authors. All rights reserved.
