T1  - Multiclass Classification of Hepatitis C Using Convolutional Neural Networks to Optimize Clinical Decisions
A1  - Saddiqa A.
A1  - Masih A.
A1  - Asghar K.
A1  - Khan M.Q.
A1  - Hussain M.
A1  - Agbozo E.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Hepatitis C virus (HCV) is one of the major causes of liver cancer, cirrhosis and if it's left undiagnosed, it can cause serious life and death problems. Early detection is crucial to prevent disease progression and long-term complications. However, the scarcity of large, labeled datasets has long constrained the development of accurate machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) models in HCV, limiting the scope of predictive applications and hindering insights into complex patterns. With the availability of a such HCV dataset on Kaggle, opens new avenues for enhancing predictive accuracy and expanding the potential of ML in this domain. Therefore, this study aims to leverage an expanded HCV multiclass dataset to develop a more reliable predictive model capable of addressing key challenges in the domain, such as data scarcity, data labeling, and implementation of DL models. By integrating sophisticated data preprocessing techniques, including handling missing values, data labeling, and feature transformation, and exploring both traditional ML algorithms such as Decision Tree (DT), Logistic Regression (LR), DL methods (Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) and Artificial Neural Network (ANN)) and ensemble techniques namely Random Forest (RF) and XGBoost, this study seeks to optimize model performance. The model performances were evaluated on significantly large dataset containing 30,692 records having 3 HCV classes (normal, mild and chronic). The results obtained indicate that the proposed approach of CNN has achieved the best accuracy of 99% followed by tree-based algorithms i.e., DT and XGBoost which are generally less prone to imbalanced dataset.Copyright © 2024, The Authors. All rights reserved.
