T1  - Measuring Interactions between SARS-CoV-2, Influenza, and Respiratory Syncytial Virus In Co-Epidemics: A Modelling Study
A1  - Liu Y.
A1  - Wang X.
A1  - Li M.
A1  - Cleary E.
A1  - Cheng Z.
A1  - Zhang W.
A1  - Shen Y.
A1  - Han J.
A1  - Ruktanonchai N.W.
A1  - Tatem A.J.
A1  - Lai S.
A1  - Wang Q.
A1  - Yang P.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: The interactions between SARS-CoV-2, influenza virus, and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) in populations are not well understood. This lack of understanding poses significant challenges in accurately predicting and being prepared for potential co-epidemic outbreaks. This study aimed to quantify the interactions among these viruses at population-level and their impact on disease transmission dynamics. Method(s): We collected weekly surveillance data on SARS-CoV-2, influenza A and B viruses (IAV and IBV), and RSV from seven different locations and countries in the northern hemisphere from October 2021 to May 2024. Using distributed lag nonlinear models with a spatiotemporal Bayesian hierarchical framework, we assessed the exposure-lag-response associations between these respiratory infections. We also perform external validation of the model using data from Beijing. A two-pathogen, meta-population mechanistic transmission model was then formulated to simulate the impact of viral interactions and effective reproduction numbers (Re) on co-epidemic dynamics. Finding(s): IAV and SARS-CoV-2 epidemics exhibited a significant negative interaction, with the risk of SARS-CoV-2 infection decreasing as IAV positivity increased. The minimum relative risk (RR) for SARS-CoV-2 was 0.13 (95% CI: 0.02-0.83) at a lag of 5 weeks when the influenza positivity reached the 72th percentile. No significant effect was observed for prior SARS-CoV-2 infection on IAV risk. In contrast, IAV and RSV showed a positive mutual interaction, with the highest risk of IAV infection occurring when RSV positivity was at the 68th percentile (RR 6.65, 95% CI: 2.25-19.67), and RSV infection risk peaking at an IAV positivity of the 74th percentile (RR 3.63, 95% CI: 1.50-8.77). No significant interaction was found between RSV and SARS-CoV-2. The mechanistic model showed that IAV could delay and reduce the peak of SARS-CoV-2 outbreaks. However, this effect weakened as the Reof SARS-CoV-2 increased, while RSV and IAV mutually amplified their epidemic peaks. Interpretation(s): Our results reveal complex, nonlinear interactions between SARS-CoV-2, IAV, and RSV. These interactions should be considered in predicting transmission dynamics and designing interventions for respiratory viruses during co-epidemic scenarios. Funding(s): Capital's Funds for Health Improvement and Research, high Level Public Health Technical Talent Training Plan, the National Institutes of Health, the U.S. National Science Foundation, Key Research Projects of Beijing Natural Science Foundation28 Haidian District Joint Fund, and Special Research Project for Capital Health Development. Declaration of Interest: We declare no competing interests. Ethical Approval: Ethical clearance for collecting and using secondary data in this study was granted by the institutional review board of the University of Southampton (No. 87924). The disease data collected by the routine public health surveillance were obtained from publicly available data sources or relevant data providers. All data were supplied and analysed in an anonymous, aggregated format, without access to personal identifying information.Copyright © 2024, The Authors. All rights reserved.
