T1  - Leveraging multidimensional prognostic models to optimize surgical outcomes in HCC
T3  - Abstracts of the AHPBA 2025 Annual Meeting. Loews Miami Beach, United States.
A1  - Gupta P.
A1  - Kaltenmeier C.
A1  - Moris D.
A1  - Larruso K.
A1  - Guerra J.F.
A1  - Nguyen B.
A1  - Radkani P.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, with surgical resection being the primary curative option. However, the heterogeneous nature of HCC complicates the prediction of post-surgical outcomes. This study aimed to develop a multidimensional prognostic model integrating clinical, genomic, and transcriptomic data to better stratify surgical candidates and optimize post-surgical outcomes in HCC. Method(s): Data from 494 HCC patients who underwent surgical resection were obtained from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) and the International Cancer Genome Consortium (ICGC). A comprehensive statistical framework was employed: Principal Component Analysis (PCA) of transcriptomic data to capture variance in gene expression. Elastic Net Regularization to select key clinical variables, mutated genes, and transcriptomic features predictive of surgical outcomes. Multivariate Cox Proportional Hazards Modeling to evaluate overall survival (OS) and recurrence-free survival (RFS) post-surgery. Machine Learning Ensemble Modeling, combining Random Forest and XGBoost, for enhanced prediction of surgical outcomes. Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) for pathway analysis of patients with poor post-surgical outcomes. Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05 with 95% confidence intervals. Result(s): The study cohort (Nf494) had a median age of 59 years, with 74.3% male patients. HBV infection was prevalent (77.5%), and 60.3% had cirrhosis. BCLC stages were distributed as A (22.1%), B (35.2%), and C (37.7%), with 49.4% showing microvascular invasion. At a median follow-up of 450 days, overall survival was 75.9%, and 66.8% remained disease-free. A prognostic model was created by integrating clinical, genomic, and transcriptomic data which improved surgical outcome prediction: Model Parameters: C-index for OS: 0.843 (95% CI: 0.821 - 0.865). C-index for RFS: 0.812 (95% CI: 0.789 - 0.835). 3-year AUC: 0.891 (95% CI: 0.867 - 0.915) for OS, and 0.863 (95% CI: 0.836 - 0.890) for RFS. Risk Stratification for Surgical Candidates: Three distinct risk groups were identified based on the model: High-risk (27.2%): Median OS of 1.9 years (95% CI: 1.3 - 2.4), with high rates of recurrence following surgery. These patients exhibited significant Wnt/beta-catenin pathway alterations. Intermediate-risk (43.5%): Median OS of 3.8 years (95% CI: 3.2 - 4.5), with moderate post-surgical outcomes and frequent TP53 mutations. Low-risk (29.3%): Median OS of 6.5 years (95% CI: 5.8 - 7.3). These patients had favorable outcomes with JAK-STAT signaling upregulation, indicating lower recurrence rates. Patients in the high-risk group demonstrated poor post-surgical outcomes, often requiring additional adjuvant therapies targeting Wnt/beta-catenin pathway alterations (OR: 3.27, p < 0.001). Conversely, patients in the low-risk group, characterized by immune modulation pathways, were candidates for curative resection alone, with minimal recurrence risk. Conclusion(s): This multidimensional prognostic model provides critical insights into optimizing surgical outcomes in HCC patients. By identifying high-risk patients who may benefit from adjunct therapies and low-risk patients suitable for surgery alone, this model enhances surgical decision-making and personalizes post-surgical management strategies. Future studies should explore integrating this model into clinical practice to improve survival and reduce recurrence in HCC. [Formula presented]Copyright © 2025
