T1  - A Scoping Review of Photo-Narrative Experiences Among Children With Medical Complexity
T3  - The Annual Assembly of Hospice and Palliative Care Oral Presentations. Denver United States.
A1  - Baumgarten N.
A1  - Faye Jonas D.
A1  - Oslin E.
A1  - Ayala E.
A1  - Pollack A.
A1  - Mendelson A.
A1  - Bogetz J.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Outcomes: 1. Participants will self-report a greater recognition of the lived experience of children with medical complexity and their caregivers, family, and clinicians. 2. Participants will self-report a greater awareness of the use of photovoice methods in understanding the lived experiences of marginalized populations. Key Message: Recent studies have highlighted a need to better understand the lived experience of children with medical complexity (CMC) and their family. Our scoping review of studies involving CMC and their family and clinician caregivers using photo-based methodologies provides insight into their lived experience. Children with medical complexity (CMC) comprise a growing inpatient population in US hospitals with chronic conditions, often involving technology dependence, and care needs that affect their family and necessitate significant healthcare system engagement.(1,2) Recent studies have highlighted a need to better understand the lived experience of CMC and their families.(3,4) Photo-based methodologies, including photo narrative, photovoice, and photo-elicitation, humanize patients and have illuminated the lived experience of children with chronic conditions and their family caregivers.(5-7) Objectives: To synthesize findings from studies involving CMC and their family and clinician caregivers through use of photo-based methodologies. Method(s): The search was conducted with a research librarian and followed the Joanna Briggs Institute guidelines for a scoping review. Databases searched include PsycInfo, PubMed, and Web of Science Core Collection. Covidence software was used for organization, screening, and full-text reviews. Included studies: 1) involved CMC through age 25 and/or their caregivers; 2) used photo-based methodologies; 3) were published 2004 or later; and 4) were written in English. Qualitative analysis was performed by a team of physicians, researchers, and social workers with photo-based methodology expertise to identify themes. Result(s): The initial search returned 82 studies, for a final of 122 when combined with researcher-identified articles. 26 papers, published between 2012 and 2024, met the study criteria. 9 themes were found and referred to as "dimensional tensions" describing contrasting elements of the lived experience of CMC and their caregivers, including "independence vs. dependency," "inclusion vs. stigmatization," and "predictability vs. uncertainty." These tensions reflected the relational, navigational, and emotional transitions and challenges experienced by children with medical complexity and their caregivers. Conclusion(s): This review of studies of CMC and their caregivers using photo-based methodology provides valuable insight into their lived experience and the tensions that exist within. References: 1. Berry JG, Hall M, Hall DE, et al. Inpatient Growth and Resource Use in 28 Children's Hospitals: A Longitudinal, Multi-institutional Study. JAMA Pediatr. 2013;167(2):170-177. doi:10.1001/jamapediatrics.2013.432 2. Cohen E, Kuo DZ, Agrawal R, et al. Children With Medical Complexity: An Emerging Population for Clinical and Research Initiatives. Pediatrics. 2011;127(3):529-538. doi:10.1542/peds.2010-0910 3. Teicher J, Moore C, Esser K, et al. The Experience of Parental Caregiving for Children With Medical Complexity. Clin Pediatr (Phila). 2023;62(6):633-644. doi:10.1177/00099228221142102 4. Houlihan BV, Coleman C, Kuo DZ, Plant B, Comeau M. What Families of Children With Medical Complexity Say They Need: Humanism in Care Delivery Change. Pediatrics. 2024;153(Supplement 1):e2023063424F. doi:10.1542/peds.2023-063424F 5. Mendelson A, Bandfield B, Hevezi J, et al. Using Photographs to Bring Dignity to Patients and Help Clinicians Find Meaning and Joy in Work. J Palliat Med. 2023;26(2):165-174. doi:10.1089/jpm.2022.0161 6. Israelsson-Skogsberg A, Heden L, Lindahl B, Laakso K. 'I'm almost never sick': Everyday life experiences of children and young people with home mechanical ventilation. J Child Health Care. 2018;22(1):6-18. doi:10.1177/1367493517749328 7. Levy K, Grant PC, Tenzek KE, et al. The Experience of Pediatric Palliative Caregiving: A Qualitative Analysis From the Photographs of Meaning Program. Am J Hosp Palliat Med. 2020;37(5):364-370. doi:10.1177/1049909119879413Copyright © 2025
