T1  - The one-stage vertical scar lejour pattern augmentation-mastopexy: indications, results and pitfalls
T3  - 53rd Congress of the European Society for Surgical Research. Madrid Spain.
A1  - Kumar N.
A1  - Oni G.
A1  - Malata C.M.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Background: One-stage augmentation-mastopexy remains as one of the most challenging aesthetic breast surgical procedures to perform. The procedure involves two opposing concepts: increasing the breast volume, which produces a downward pull on the tissues versus the upward/tightening vector of a breast lift. Lejour popularized the vertical scar mastopexy but there is a paucity of literature on this technique in combination with augmentation and we therefore reviewed the senior author's experience with the above technique, the outcomes and surgical considerations. Material(s) and Method(s): A retrospective review of all vertical scar augmentation-mastopexy carried out by the senior author over a 15 year period. Operative indications, patient demographics, and post-operative complications were recorded. Result(s): 87 patients were identified over a 15-year period. The patients were almost equally distributed between primary augmentation (n=45) and secondary implant exchange (n=42). The most common indication was aesthetic followed by developmental asymmetry. Implant sizes ranged from 100cc to 850cc. There was one implant loss in a reconstructive patient who had had previous radiotherapy. The complication rate was low (12%) with surgical intervention required for implant infection, wound dehiscence, scar revision/dog ears, and recurrent ptosis. Risk factors for complications included subglandular implant placement, weight loss and smoking. Conclusion(s): While challenging, we have found the vertical scar augmentation-mastopexy to be a relatively predictable procedure with an acceptable complication/revision rate in a technically challenging patient group. Notable surgical pitfalls include over-resection skin and large volume augmentations. We have identified patient factors that influence complication rate and these patients should be approached with a high degree of caution.
