A1  - Kim H.-R.
A1  - Ko K.S.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: Deep learning offers a promising solution for complex biological classifications, especially when well-curated datasets are available. This study focuses on the challenge of analyzing three-dimensional protein structures. Method(s): Our approach involves a novel pipeline using opensource tools to transform protein structures into a format suitable for computational analysis. We applied a two-dimensional Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to a dataset of 12,143 avian influenza virus genomes from 64 countries, covering 119HN types. Additionally, we incorporated Principal Component Analysis (PCA) for dimensionality reduction and one-class SVM for anomaly detection, with model robustness enhanced through bootstrapping. The K-Nearest Neighbor (K-NN) algorithm was also fine-tuned using hyper-parameter optimization. Result(s): The CNN model, with configurations ranging from zero to six mid-layers, revealed that a four-layer model was most effective in identifying highly pathogenic strains, achieving accuracies over 0.9. the OCA facilitated the identification of distinct clustering for pathogenic HA, resulting in an AUC of up to 0.85. The optimized K-NN model showed an accuracy between 0.96 and 0.97. Conclusion(s): The combined methodologies of CNN, PCA, and K-NN in our deep learning framework demonstrate a significant capacity for rapid and precise identification of pathogenic avian influenza strains. This provides a critical tool for managing global avian influenza threats, highlighting the effectiveness of deep learning in biological classification tasks.
