A1  - Shaikh S.
A1  - Harrop J.S.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - BACKGROUND CONTEXT: The management of surgical site infections (SSIs) following spinal surgery, particularly with instrumented spinal fusion, presents significant clinical challenges. Research in this area has explored various approaches, including the use of antibiotics without removing surgical instrumentation. In 2017, Hey et al conducted a retrospective study on 84 patients, demonstrating that spinal instrumentation in infected spines does not lead to higher reoperation or relapse rates, and is associated with lower mortality. In 2010, Rayes et al reviewed 47 patients treated with instrumented surgery for spinal infection, indicating that instrumentation after radical debridement does not increase the risk of recurrent infection. In a 2020 study involving 29 pediatric patients, Moyano et al found that 96.6% of infections were resolved with a combination of IV and oral antibiotics, suggesting the effectiveness of retaining implants to treat acute SSIs. PURPOSE: The primary aim of this study is to assess the effectiveness of IV followed by oral antibiotic therapy in preventing reinfections after spinal surgery, using a larger patient cohort of 303 cases at Jefferson. This study is critical as it explores the hypothesis with a significantly larger sample size than previous studies, potentially providing more robust evidence regarding the safety and efficacy of nonsurgical management in postoperative spinal infections with retained instrumentation. STUDY DESIGN/SETTING: This investigation is a retrospective cohort study conducted at Jefferson. It involves a comprehensive review of medical records and postoperative outcomes of patients. Jefferson provides a substantial patient base and diverse demographic, ensuring a comprehensive analysis. The retrospective nature of the study allows for a thorough examination of real-world outcomes and the practical effectiveness of the antibiotic regimen in a clinical setting. PATIENT SAMPLE: A total of 303 patients who underwent spinal fusion with instrumentation and returned to Jefferson with surgical site infection. OUTCOME MEASURES: The primary outcome measure is the rate of reinfection within one year following spinal surgery. Secondary outcomes include the identification of risk factors for reinfection and the evaluation of the effectiveness of IV followed by oral antibiotics in preventing reinfections. METHOD(S): This study employs a retrospective analysis of patient data. Variables collected include demographic information (patient ID, age, gender, BMI, ethnicity, zip code), health status and history (diabetes, obesity, hypertension, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, scleroderma, osteopenia/osteoporosis, smoking, medical marijuana, alcohol use), surgical details (previous revision, type of surgery, surgeon, duration of procedure), infection-related data (date of surgical site infection, type of bacteria, severity of SSI, debridement, antibiotics, vacuum-assisted closure), and postoperative follow-up details (hardware removal, antibiotic treatment duration, side effects/complications, reinfection at one year). Descriptive statistics summarize patient characteristics. Logistic regression models further identify predictors of reinfection. Kaplan-Meier survival analysis estimate time-to-event (reinfection) data, and Cox proportional hazards model adjust for potential confounders. RESULT(S): None of the patients in our study demonstrated reinfection or needed hardware removal within one year of follow-up of their infection, after being treated with the combined regimen of IV and oral antibiotics. This outcome would suggest that the regimen is highly effective in managing postoperative spinal surgical site infections without the need for removing surgical instrumentation. Additionally, as we continue to conduct secondary analyses, the study may reveal significant insights into patient-specific factors patient's age, gender, BMI, comorbid conditions like diabetes or obesity, and the specific types of bacteria involved in the initial surgical site infection, that contribute to the success of the antibiotic therapy in preventing complications. CONCLUSION(S): The study aims to contribute evidence on the effectiveness of IV followed by oral antibiotic therapy in preventing reinfection in spinal surgery patients. This could support a shift in postoperative care protocols towards avoiding instrumentation removal, based on the outcomes observed in this and referenced studies. FDA Device/Drug Status: This abstract does not discuss or include any applicable devices or drugs.Copyright © 2024
