T1  - Cytokine mediated modulation of drug metabolizing enzymes observed at physiologically relevant levels in truvivo hepatocyte model
T3  - 25th North American ISSX Meeting. Boston United States.
A1  - Aluri K.
A1  - Hariparsad N.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Proinflammatory cytokines including interleukin 6 (IL-6) and interleukin 1beta (IL-1 beta) are elevated during infection, or due to diseases including autoimmune disorders and cancer. It is understood that the increased levels of these cytokines reduces the expression of drug metabolizing enzymes with more than 2-fold increase in the exposure of drugs reported (1). Modulation of cytokines levels can also be of therapeutic value thus, regulatory agencies require clinical characterization of the DDI potential in these cases. The effect of IL-6 and IL-1 beta on various cytochrome P450 (CYP) isoforms has been evaluated with limited success using cryopreserved primary human hepatocytes and supratherapeutic cytokine levels. These studies highlighted the need for more complex models to account for indirect interactions and the interplay between multiple cell types (2,3). Cryopreserved primary human hepatocytes have a narrow window of use making them an inefficient model to understand the impact of long-term exposure of cytokines on drug metabolizing enzymes and transporters. To circumvent the limitations observed with standard hepatocyte models a novel triculture model (TruVivoTM) containing human hepatocytes cocultured with human fibroblasts and endothelial cells was used and the IC50 values for IL-6 and IL-1 beta against multiple CYP and UGT enzymes was derived. The degradation and synthesis rates could be described by monitoring the loss and recovery of CYP1A2, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP2D6 and UGT2B7. The data was then applied using static and dynamic models to evaluate the physiological relevance of the model. Comparison with observed clinical data under conditions with varying levels of these cytokines demonstrated good concordance with predictions highlighting the potential value of this system to predict cytokine mediated DDIs. References: 1. Coutant, D.E., Boulton, D., Dahal, U.P., Deslandes, A., Grimaldi, C., Pereira, J.N., Sall, C., Sarvaiya, H., Schiller, H., Tai, G.J.C.P. et al. (2022) Therapeutic Protein Drug Interactions: A White Paper from the International Consortium for Innovation and Quality in Pharmaceutical Development. 2. Dickmann, L.J., Patel, S.K., Rock, D.A., Wienkers, L.C., Slatter, J.G.J.D.M. and Disposition. (2011) Effects of interleukin-6 (IL-6) and an anti-IL-6 monoclonal antibody on drug-metabolizing enzymes in human hepatocyte culture. 39, 1415-1422. 3. J Dickmann, L., K Patel, S., C Wienkers, L. and Greg Slatter, J.J.C.D.M. (2012) Effects of Interleukin 1beta (IL-1beta) and IL-1beta/Interleukin 6 (IL-6) Combinations on Drug Metabolizing Enzymes in Human Hepatocyte Culture. 13, 930-937.Copyright © 2023
