T1  - Deep Learning-Guided and Structure-Informed Multifunctional AAV Capsid Design Pipeline for Manufacturability and Redirected Tropisms
T3  - 27th Annual Meeting of the American Society of Gene & Cell Therapy. Baltimore United States.
A1  - Zhang C.
A1  - Lv Z.
A1  - Lu Z.
A1  - Zhang Y.
A1  - Yuan P.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - The recombinant adeno-associated virus (AAV) is a highly promising vector that is transforming the field of gene therapies for human diseases. Capsid engineering, which involves modifying the interactions between the capsid and cell surface receptors, has been achieved through a combination of knowledge-based design and iterative directed evolution. This approach has successfully produced AAV variants with notably altered biodistribution and other beneficial properties, such as improved manufacturability. Despite the capability of screening millions of capsid variants in a single library, conventional methods fall short in exploring the vastly larger combination pool and efficiently identifying the small fraction of vectors with the desired characteristics for clinical use. The process is also marred by the tedious and costly nature of multiple selection and testing rounds across different tissues. In response to these challenges, we introduce a comprehensive method that integrates deep learning-based models for sequence-to-characteristic predictions and a structural approach for capsid classification and clustering. This strategy simplifies the screening process and expedites the discovery of new capsids. Our method utilizes random 7-mer NNK insertional capsid libraries, with packaging and enrichment screening conducted across various tissues including the liver, muscle, and central nervous system (CNS). A deep-learning framework has been developed, capable of predicting manufacturability and tissue-specific targeting based on peptide sequences. From an initial experimental library of approximately 360,000 variants, representing a fraction of the 1.28 billion possible sequences, each peptide sequence was transformed into a one-hot encoded 7x20 two-dimensional array. A deep learning model was then trained for binary classification on production efficiency, supplemented by regression models to assess the enrichment scores for each target tissue. The integration of an attention mechanism to capture global sequence relationships and 1-D convolution for local context analysis has resulted in a classification model with an impressive 98% accuracy. Our regression model also consistently achieved over 70% accuracy in its predictions. By combining these models, we constructed a condensed, multi-trait capsid library of 300 manufacturable candidates targeting the CNS and muscle. This library was experimentally validated, with over 50% of the variants showing targeted tissue enrichment-a success rate significantly higher than that achieved through traditional screening methods. Moreover, we utilized AlphaFold2 for predicting oligopeptide structures, facilitating the clustering of variants into distinct clades based on structural similarities. This approach aided in the deduplication and efficient classification of top candidates showing robust tissue enrichment in mouse models. Preliminary data from a non-PHP.eB-clustered variant demonstrated high and consistent CNS transduction in both C57BL/6 and BALB/c mice, underscoring the effectiveness of structure-based capsid classification. In summary, our proposed pipeline represents a significant advancement in AAV capsid engineering. It drastically narrows the search space for potential variants, reduces the number of screening and testing cycles needed, and demonstrates the powerful synergy of deep learning and structural analysis in expanding therapeutic possibilities.Copyright © 2024
