T1  - Navigating Airway Challenges After Lung Transplantation
T3  - American Thoracic Society 2024 International Conference. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Dhanani Z.
A1  - Criner G.J.
A1  - Anjum F.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Introduction:Lung transplantation remains the primary treatment for end-stage lung diseases with a global increase in its prevalence. However, the effectiveness of lung transplantation can be significantly hindered by complications such as airway complications (AC), notably airway stenosis and dehiscence. Despite advancements in surgical and bronchoscopic techniques, post lung transplant AC occurs in 1.4% to 44% of cases primarily attributed to donor bronchial ischemia, Several potential factors, including ischemic time, mechanical ventilation, and microbiologic contamination, have been proposed, yet confirmed predictors for AC are lacking. To address this knowledge gap, we conducted a retrospective analysis of lung transplant recipients at Temple University Hospital (TUH) to investigate the incidence of AC and identify potential risk factors. Method(s): We retrospectively reviewed 200 lung transplant patients who underwent the Lung Transplantation at TUH between November 2016 and July 2022. AC was classified into stenosis, dehiscence, ischemia, and necrosis. Using independent t-tests we then compared baseline characteristics, post-transplant outcomes, and complications in patients with and without AC. Multivariate Logistic regression analysis was utilized to adjust for various factors, including telescoping, pseudomonas colonization, CMV pneumonia, fungal colonization, gastroesophageal reflux disorder (GERD), primary graft dysfunction (PGD), cold ischemic time, post-transplant use of extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO), acute kidney injury (AKI) during the initial admission and intensive care unit (ICU) length of stay. Result(s): Airway complications were identified in 17.5% of patients in the overall cohort. Stenosis was the most common complication seen in 13% of the patients followed by malacia in 2.5% and 2% of the patients had a combination of two or more complications. Patient characteristics and risk factors are highlighted in Figure 1a. Patients with pseudomonas colonization with or without infection (n=68) post transplantation were more likely to develop AC in comparison to patients without pseudomonas colonization (n=132) [adjusted odds ratio (aOR) 2.59, P 0.018; 95% Confidence interval [CI] 1.17-5.73] (Figure 1b). Conclusion(s):Our paper demonstrated that the presence of pseudomonas colonization in lung transplant recipients was associated with airway complications. Prospective studies are needed to assess whether pseudomonas contributes to the development of airway complications or is a consequence of impaired mucus clearance in abnormal airways.
