T1  - How to Resolve Vector-Based Therapeutics Major Manufacturing Pain Points by Innovation-Driven Collaborations
T3  - 27th Annual Meeting of the American Society of Gene & Cell Therapy. Baltimore United States.
A1  - Legmann R.
A1  - Gantier R.
A1  - Chung K.
A1  - Mihlbachler K.
A1  - Krishnamoorthy G.
A1  - Agbogbo F.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - With increasing approvals of viral vector-based gene therapies, the industry is shifting its focus to the expansion of viral vector manufacturing to meet the demands of the growing number of therapies on the market. The higher complexity of viral vectors compared to monoclonal antibodies or recombinant proteins creates additional production and purification challenges to overcome. Therefore, collaboration, better fit innovative platforms and expertise are crucial for rapid continuous improvements in the efficiency of viral vector production to meet demand and get lifesaving therapies to patients quickly. This must be carefully balanced with consistency and quality of the final product. Multiple applications and technologies to address current manufacturing challenges and solutions to increase overall viral vector yield throughout development will be explored. Collaborative case studies will be presented to demonstrate the progress associated with entire process intensification and optimization from benchtop development to large-scale commercial manufacturing. Upstream process intensification strategies using the KrosFlo tangential flow depth Filtration (TFDF) technology, a perfusion platform for lentivirus and AAV high production and clarification will be presented. Two experimental upstream studies illustrate the next generation manufacturing process for AAV and LV viral vector intensification using HEK293 cells in suspension. In both experimental studies, the TFDF, a perfusion platform, was integrated into the bioreactor to intensify AAV8 viral vectors and in the second study lentivirus (LV) was intensified. The TFDF, a perfusion integrated platform, increased the HEK293 cell density by 3 X fold at time of transfection led to increase up to 10 X fold of total titer of rAAV8 and rAAV9. The cell retention filters enabled the continuous harvest clarification of secreted AAV8 particles into the media during the virus production, demonstrating the potential for continuous upstream-downstream processing of secreted AAV vectors. The TFDF platform increased lentivirus (LV) yield up to 86 X through synergism between high cell density, higher specific productivity, and continuous harvest approach. Overall, the TFDF platform enables manufacturers to meet high vector demand and potentially reduce the cost per dose. We will also present the collaborative effort with a gene therapy CDMO that involved a detailed evaluation of scaling up the affinity chromatograph step for AAV9 from a 1-L benchtop chromatography scale to a 167-L industrial scale using the innovative single-use advanced KRMTM Chromatography System. The large-scale tests were sequentially executed with the benchtop-scale tests in parallel. All process fractions were analyzed by Droplet Digital PCR. It also demonstrated high reproducibility between the scale-up runs without compromising quality product attributes such as empty and full ratio, endotoxin, residual host cell protein and aggregation. This successful AAV scaleup demonstrated a 240-fold increase in the overall viral recovery that is 1.4 X fold higher than predicted, as well as good reproducibility of the scale-up runs. The innovative KRMTM Chromatography Systems enabled high productivity and cost-effective manufacturing of viral vectors, including AAV and lentivirus, through novel design features like low hold-up volumes, gentle internal flow paths, and accurate pump performance. The results of this case study will further demonstrate the capabilities to scale-up form 500-L to 5000-L manufacturing scale bioreactors.Copyright © 2024
