T1  - Paresis of the Right Hemidiaphragm and Anhidrosis of the Right Hemithorax: A Case Report
T3  - American Thoracic Society 2024 International Conference. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Acar Silva J.
A1  - Reis K.
A1  - Sales R.
A1  - Lucas S.
A1  - Barranco L.
A1  - Vigario P.
A1  - Costa C.H.
A1  - Lopes A.
A1  - Mafort T.T.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Introduction: The diaphragm is the main respiratory muscle and plays a crucial role in ventilation. It is a domeshaped musculotendinous structure, bilaterally innervated by the phrenic nerve, which originates from the C3 to C5 nerve roots. Diaphragmatic paralysis or paresis may be an idiopathic clinical condition or associated with conditions resulting from trauma, surgery, infections and neuromuscular disorders. Description of Case: A 54-year-old man, paraplegic, wheelchair user, with a previous traumatic fracture of T3-T4, due to a car accident in 2008, when he was admitted to a general hospital for 2 months, undergoing T3-T4 arthrodesis and right thoracic drainage due to pneumothorax due to chest contusion resulting from the trauma. During his hospital stay, he continuously used supplemental oxygen therapy and did not need mechanical ventilation. After discharge, he remained asymptomatic, despite reporting anhidrosis of the right hemithorax, neck and head ipsilateral to right hemidiaphragm paresis. The patient continues to undergo weekly monitoring with a physiotherapy team for rehabilitation, and in the last year has been evaluated by the pulmonology team and was submitted to additional tests. Spirometry using Brazilian reference values without bronchodilator testing showed: FEV1/FVC ratio 79.8 (98.5%), FEV1 4.47 (105.4%), FVC 5.60 (106.7%). It was not possible to perform plethysmography due to limited accommodation of the paraplegic patient in the box. The muscle strength was tested and a value of -90 cmH2O (80% predicted) was obtained for maximum inspiratory pressure and + 50 cmH2O (40% predicted) for maximum expiratory pressure. Diaphragmatic ultrasound revealed the presence of diaphragmatic paresis. The diaphragmatic thickness at the end of deep inspiration was 0.37 cm and the thickness at the end of maximum expiration was 0.31 cm, generating a reduced thickening fraction of 19%. The diaphragmatic excursion values in tidal volume were 3.32 cm, in deep breathing of 6.45 cm; despite having normal values a fractional inspiratory movement was visible in B-mode and represented as notches in the curve formed in M-mode (Figure 1). Discussion(s): The patient in question presents a rare case of anhidrosis of the right hemithorax combined to a right hemidiaphragmatic paresis without dyspnea, orthopnea or nocturnal dyspnea. Diaphragmatic paralysis is a disorder that can lead to dyspnea, with orthopnea being common, as well as pulmonary atelectasis of the adjacent base, hypoventilation and even lead to hypercapnic respiratory failure when bilateral.
