T1  - Study on the Applicability of Digital Twins for Home Remote Motor Rehabilitation
A1  - Falkowski P.
A1  - Osiak T.
A1  - Wilk J.
A1  - Prokopiuk N.
A1  - Leczkowski B.
A1  - Pilat Z.
A1  - Rzymkowski C.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - The COVID-19 pandemic created the need for telerehabilitation development, while Industry 4.0 brought the key technology. As motor therapy often requires the physical support of a patient's motion, combining robot-aided workouts with remote control is a promising solution. This may be realised with the use of the device's digital twin, so as to give it an immersive operation. This paper presents an extensive overview of this technology's applications within the fields of industry and health. It is followed by the in-depth analysis of needs in rehabilitation based on questionnaire research and bibliography review. As a result of these sections, the original concept of controlling a rehabilitation exoskeleton via its digital twin in the virtual reality is presented. The idea is assessed in terms of benefits and significant challenges regarding its application in real life. The presented aspects prove that it may be potentially used for manual remote kinesiotherapy, combined with the safety systems predicting potentially harmful situations. The concept is universally applicable to rehabilitation robots.
