T1  - Comparison of Padua Prediction Score and IMPROVE VTE Risk Score for Venous Thromboembolism at Admission to Assess Hypercoagulation in COVID-19 Patients
T3  - ISTH 2023 Congress. Montreal Canada.
A1  - Lasut P.
A1  - Hukom A.
A1  - Irawan V.
A1  - Arifputra J.
A1  - Rotty L.
A1  - Haroen H.
A1  - Hendratta C.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background: The inflammatory process, cytokine storm, and lung injury associated with the Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) can increase the risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE). Once VTE is complicated with COVID-19, the treatment will be challenging, and the patients may have a worse clinical outcome. Padua Prediction Score (PPS) and IMPROVE VTE Risk Score (IVRS) are validated tools for VTE risk assessment. Aim(s): We aimed to compare these two validated tools to assess hypercoagulation in COVID-19 patients. Method(s): A cross-sectional retrospective study was conducted on COVID- 19 patients. We enrolled adult patients who were hospitalized in Prof. dr. R. D. Kandou General Hospital from August 2022 to October 2022. Ddimer levels and data of VTE risk assessment using PPS and IVRS were collected at admission and analyzed to determine the hypercoagulation state. A D-dimer level above two is considered a hypercoagulation state. Data analyses were done using SPSS version 22. Result(s): A total of 42 COVID-19 patients were included in this study. There were 23 patients (54.76%) having elevation of D-dimer (> 2 mug/mL). Mean of PPS and IVRS, consecutively, was 1.48 +/- 1.93 and 1.07 +/- 1.20. Therefore, PPS was not superior than IVRS to assess hypercoagulation in COVID-19 patients [AUC 0.754 (0.078); p = 0.005 vs. AUC 0.759 (0.077); p = 0.004]. Combination the values of PPS and IVRS was more superior to assesses hypercoagulation [AUC 0.815 (0.072); p = 0.001]. Conclusion(s): Padua Prediction Score and IMPROVE VTE Risk Score can be used to assess hypercoagulation in COVID-19 patients. In addition, the combination of these two validated tools is superior to assess hypercoagulation in COVID-19 patients.Copyright © 2023 International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis
