Y1  - 2023//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Chest Infections Case Report Posters 4 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Pleural infection always being a great burden globally with significant morbidity. The incidence of pleural infection is about 80,000 in US and UK combined whereas mortality from pleural infection is roughly about 5% in the United States. The drainage of the pleural fluid collection with the use of intra-pleural alteplase and DNase decreases the morbidity, frequency of surgical referral, and duration of the hospital stay. Many studies and trials performed reflect the cost effectives of the use of these medications. CASE PRESENTATION: A 44-year-old female smoker presented with a one-week history of productive cough associated with pleuritic chest pain to the emergency department. Labs revealed a leukocytosis with predominant granulocytes. The initial chest X-ray revealed left upper lobe consolidation and left lower lobe airspace opacities. Patient underwent CT angiography of the chest revealing a large left upper lobe cavitary lesion as well as a large lobulated hydropneumothorax. Patient initially started on ceftriaxone and doxycycline which was later changed to piperacillin/tazobactam. Additional lab results were negative for acid-fast bacillus, HIV, urine antigens for legionella and streptococcus pneumonia. The patient underwent left sided pleural thoracentesis and subsequent pleural fluid analysis was consistent with empyema. The blood cultures and bronchial washing cultures came back negative, but sputum culture was positive for streptococcus pneumonia. A pigtail catheter was placed and the patient was started on intra-pleural medications alteplase 10mg and DNase 5mg and completed 6 doses 1-2 hours apart as per the MIST-2 protocol. Repeat imaging of the chest showed persistent left right upper lower consolidation. Patient was evaluated by the surgical team and refused surgical intervention. As she had declined surgery, she received 6 more doses of intra-pleural medications without any complications. After one week, her chest tube was removed having minimal output. She was discharged home with IV antibiotics for 2 weeks and regular follow up with the infectious disease physician with repeat weekly laboratory studies. Patient underwent repeat CT scan of chest 4 weeks after discharge with resolving consolidation. DISCUSSION(S): The Multicenter Intrapleural Sepsis Trial-2 guidelines recommended the use of intrapleural medications as an alternative to surgical interventions but do not recommend the use of high doses which may lead to pulmonary complications. Many studies implicated the deescalating the doses based on the improvements of the findings on imaging.The prior case reports and trials do not mention the use of higher doses which is highlighted in this case report. Some of the trials reported side effects like noncardiac chest pain and minor bleeding but no major bleeding or mortality and in comparison with this study no reported side effects even with high doses. CONCLUSION(S): There are ongoing dose de-escalation studies to establish the lowest effective dose of intrapleural medications. There is no literature review available so far about the highest and safest possible dose of these medications which can be used in the pleural infections without any complication. There should be more supporting data available about the safety of higher doses and their complications. REFERENCE #1: 1.Corcoran, John P., John M. Wrightson, Elizabeth Belcher, Malcolm M. DeCamp, David Feller-Kopman, and Najib M. Rahman. "Pleural infection: past, present, and future directions." The Lancet Respiratory Medicine 3, no. 7 (2015): 563-577 REFERENCE #2: 2. Hassan, Maged, Shefaly Patel, Ahmed S. Sadaka, Eihab O. Bedawi, John P. Corcoran, and Jose M. Porcel. "Recent insights into the management of pleural infection." International Journal of General Medicine (2021): 3415-3429. REFERENCE #3: 3. Davies, Christopher WH, Susan E. Kearney, Fergus V. Gleeson, and Robert JO Davies. "Predictors of outcome and long-term survival in patients with pleural infection." American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine 160, no. 5 (1999): 1682-1687 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Noreena Aslam No relevant relationships by Karen Avgush No relevant relationships by Aamir HayatCopyright © 2023 American College of Chest Physicians
