Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Rationale: PUL-042 is lipopeptide Pam2CSK4 acetate combined with phosphorothioate oligodeoxynucleotide M362, acting as Toll-like receptor 2/6 and 9 agonists, respectively. In animal models, they act synergistically to prevent infections. Method(s): We assessed the safety and efficacy of inhaled PUL-042 on experimental rhinovirus (RV-A16)-induced lower respiratory symptoms (LRS) in actively smoking GOLD stage 0 pre-COPD subjects. The effects of PUL-042 on lung function, blood biomarkers, AEs, rhinovirus load, serum, nasal and sputum pro-inflammatory cytokines (CXCL10 and IL-6), and sputum cell counts were examined. Result(s): 143 subjects were screened, 24 were enrolled, 20 received two doses of PUL-042 or placebo (1:1, dosed on day -1 and day 2) and were infected with RV-A16 on day 0, 2/20 were excluded due to positive other virus detection at baseline, leaving n=9/group for analysis. RV-A16 infection induced colds, but there was no significant change from baseline in the placebo group, or difference between placebo and PUL- 042 groups in the LRS score. PUL-042 was well tolerated, and AEs were not-significantly different between groups. There were no SAEs. PUL-042 caused significant, yet temporary reductions in % predicted FEV1 and % predicted FVC on both dosing days, but not in PEF or FEV1/FVC. Lung function returned to baseline by 8h and there was no accumulation of effect between dosing days. PUL-042 caused a ~doubling of blood neutrophils at 4-8h after dosing on both dosing days (P<0.001) which returned to baseline within 24h. PUL-042 also decreased blood monocytes ~30% at 2h (P<0.001, both dosing days) yet caused a ~25% increase at 6h on day -1 (P<0.05). PUL-042 also showed non-significant trends for decreased blood lymphocytes on both dosing days. PUL- 042 increased serum CRP ~100% at 8h on day -1 (P=0.011) and ~1-300% at 24h on day -1 & day 2 (P<0.0001 & 0.001), subsequently returning to baseline. There was no significant difference between placebo and PUL-042 in sputum virus load, however nasal lavage virus load was lower in the PUL-042 group on day 6 (P=0.031). PUL-042 showed non-significant trends for transient increased sputum and serum IL-6 and sputum total cells, neutrophils, lymphocytes and monocytes post-treatment. The lack of RV-induced LRS in the control group precluded measurement of difference in LRS in the treatment group. Conclusion(s): PUL-042 treatment was well tolerated in actively smoking GOLD 0 COPD subjects. The data highlight the immunomodulatory effects of PUL-042, causing temporary changes in systemic and local immune biomarkers following inhalation.
