N2  - Rationale: Influenza-associated pulmonary aspergillosis (IAPA) is a feared complication in patients with severe influenza, especially those receiving corticosteroids. However, validated murine models of IAPA in a background of corticosteroid immunosuppression are lacking, as are robust in-vivo data for immunotherapeutic interventions to improve the outcomes of IAPA. Method(s): 8-10-week-old female BALB/c mice were infected with 7.5% of the 90% lethal dose of a mouse-adapted influenza A/H3N2/Hong Kong/1968 strain, delivered by aerosolization. Mice then received two intraperitoneal injections of 10 mg cortisone acetate (CA) or mock injections on days (d) 5 and 8 after influenza infection. On d9, mice were intranasally challenged with 50,000 Aspergillus fumigatus conidia (Figure 1A). Infection severity was scored using the viral pneumonia score (VPS, 0=healthy to 12=moribund) and the murine sepsis score (MSS, 0=healthy to 3=moribund, 4=death). Fungal burden was determined in lung tissue homogenates on day 16 or upon death using an 18S qPCR assay. For therapeutic studies, mice received either a 30-minute nebulization of PBS (mock treatment), a single (d8) or dual-dose (d8+12) of the synergistic Toll-like receptors 2/6/9 agonists, Pam2+ODN (4 muM/1 muM), before or before and after Aspergillus respectively. Therapeutic studies utilized a combined morbidity and mortality endpoint, with an event defined as either death or reaching a VPS >=7. Result(s): Corticosteroid-immunosuppressed mice with IAPA had 64% mortality and severe morbidity was seen in surviving mice (median MSS, 2.7). In contrast, non-immunosuppressed mice with IAPA showed consistently low morbidity (median d16 MSS, 0.5). Point biserial correlation analysis confirmed CA as an important predictor of influenza severity (d9 VPS, rho=0.76, p<0.001) and AF-associated morbidity/mortality (d16 MSS, rho=0.54, p<0.001). All mock-treated CA-immunosuppressed mice with IAPA reached the combined morbidity/mortality endpoint by d13. Single-dose Pam2+ODN intervention led to universal event-free survival until d13 but all mice reached the morbidity/mortality endpoint by d16 (Figure 1B). Therapeutic dual-dose Pam2+ODN interventions led to 80% event-free survival until d21, with all survivors fully recovering (VPS=0, p<0.001 versus all other groups) (Figure 1C). Conclusion(s): We have established a novel CA-immunosuppressed murine model of IAPA that underscores the detrimental effect of corticosteroid therapy on the outcomes of IAPA. Immunomodulatory treatment with Pam2+ODN significantly improved influenza- and IAPA-associated morbidity and mortality. Detailed studies of the mechanistic underpinnings of this effect are in progress. In the future, we will study the impact of synergistic anti-infective and immunotherapeutic interventions on the course and immunopathogenesis of IAPA.
