T1  - Clinical profile and short-term outcome of Acute on Chronic Liver Failure (ACLF)
T3  - 31st Annual Meeting of Indian National Association for the Study of the Liver (INASL) - 2023. Bhubaneswar India.
A1  - Debbarma M.
A1  - Chetri K.
A1  - kelkar A.
A1  - Agarwala M.
A1  - Agarwala R.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background and Aim: Acute on Chronic Liver Failure (ACLF) carries a high mortality. There are few studies from north-east part of India. Study aims to assess the clinical profile, severity, and 28 days outcome of ACLF patients at a tertiary care hospital. Method(s): In this prospective study (April 2021-March 2022), patients satisfying Asian Pacific Association for the Study of the Liver (APASL) definition of ACLF were included and followed up for 28 days. Etiology of acute precipitating insult and underlying chronic liver disease (CLD) were assessed. Mortality recorded at 28 days. The severity of ACLF was assessed using Child-Turcotte-Pugh (CTP), Model for End stage Liver Disease (MELD), APASL ACLF Research Consortium (AARC) and Chronic Liver Failure Consortium (CLIF-C) ACLF scores at admission to hospital and compared. Result(s): The mean (+/-SD) age of 71 consecutive ACLF patients was 46.24+/-10.7 years; 81.7 % were men. The etiology of CLD include alcohol (74.6%), followed by cryptogenic (7%), hepatitis B viruses (7%) and autoimmune hepatitis (4.2%). Precipitating factors included hepatic (47.9%), extrahepatic (11.3%), combined hepatic and extrahepatic (35.2%) and unknown (5.6%). Among the hepatic precipitants, alcoholic hepatitis (59.1%) was commonest, followed by viral hepatitis (Hepatitis E 11.6%, Hepatitis B 4.2%, Hepatitis A 1.4%). Among the extrahepatic precipitants, sepsis (38%) was commonest, followed by gastrointestinal bleed (19.7%). Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (44.7%) was the most common cause of sepsis, followed by cellulitis (14.8%), urinary tract infection (14.8%) and pneumonia (14.8%). Short-term mortality was 38%. AARC score (>10) has the highest Area Under Curve (AUC) of 0.82, followed by CLIF-C ACLF score (>45), MELD score (>30) and CTP score (>12) with AUCs of 0.76, 0.73 and 0.49 respectively. Conclusion(s): Alcohol was the commonest etiology of CLD and precipitant for ACLF. The AARC score was found as best predictor of outcome.Copyright © 2023
