T1  - The Application of Artificial Intelligence-based Positron Emission Tomography in the Analysis of Epilepsy
T3  - Society of Nuclear Medicine and Molecular Imaging Annual Meeting, SNMMI 2023. Chicago, IL United States.
A1  - Teichner E.
A1  - Su Y.
A1  - Subtirelu R.
A1  - Khan T.
A1  - Ciro A.
A1  - Raynor W.
A1  - Revheim M.-E.
A1  - Hoilund-Carlsen P.F.
A1  - Werner T.
A1  - Alavi A.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Introduction: 1. Review the role of FDG-PET in neurosurgical procedures for the treatment of epilepsy. 2. Assess the added benefit of artificial intelligence (AI) in FDG-PET imaging of epilepsy patients. Method(s): PubMed and Google Scholar search engines were utilized to accrue relevant studies regarding FDG PET and epilepsy. Review articles and scientific studies were compiled from the databases using the COVIDENCE platform as a means of accumulating a comprehensive body of literature. Studies were then assessed for quality, relevance, and potential bias to derive broader conclusions about the standing knowledge of the aforementioned objectives. Result(s): Epilepsy is a central nervous system disorder characterized by recurring seizures caused by disrupted nervous system activity. In order to select patients eligible for epilepsy surgery and to obtain an optimal treatment response, precise localization of the epileptogenic region is necessary. For this purpose, PET imaging has multiple advantages over other imaging modalities. PET can detect epileptogenic foci in morphologically inconspicuous areas non-invasively; it does not depend on the electrical activity of the brain and is more precise than visual inspection. The greatest hindrance that limits the potential of PET imaging in epilepsy localization is the low signalto-noise ratio and the limited spatial resolution that compromises the image quality. However, new research advancements in artificial intelligence (AI) and deep learning techniques have shown great promise in resolving this issue. Studies have demonstrated that artificial neural networks (ANNs) such as ResNet can improve image quality with the utilization of measurements from objective and quantitative metrics. More research is needed to determine the generalizability of ANNs. Current research on smaller samples indicates significant and promising results; future studies should incorporate clinical data from large cohorts. Furthermore, deep learning techniques such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and generative adversarial networks (GANs) have been utilized to reconstruct and generate standard-quality images from noisy, low-quality images, allowing for lower doses of radiation. The use of AI in attenuation correction and image synthesis to supplement missing data is growing rapidly. The use of AI in the synthesis of standard-quality images and noise reduction can allow for improved detection of epileptic lesions, resulting in optimal and individualized treatment strategies (and outcomes). Machine learning predictive models like gradient-boosting decision trees and logistic regression analyses on FDG-PET and functional MRI data have been used in prior studies to assess the recurrence of seizures after temporal lobe epilepsy surgery. In addition to improved image quality, quantitative analysis of FDGPET imaging data has the ability to predict the long-term recurrence of seizures in refractory epilepsy. Recent research indicates that AI-based models that utilize quantification outperform qualitative clinical assessment when tasked with identifying patients with seizure recurrence. The retrospective data indicate that machine learning models can assist with long-term prognosis prediction and the clinical decision-making process in epilepsy treatment. Conclusion(s): One of the most substantial challenges in epilepsy management is the difficulty clinicians face attempting to precisely locate epileptic regions. Literature indicates that FDG-PET imaging combined with AI could effectively tackle this hurdle by improving image quality via noise reduction and better attenuation correction. In addition, quantitative analysis using FDG-PET/AI models demonstrates the possibility of predicting long-term prognosis, which warrants additional investigation in future research.
