T1  - POS-182 SDF4 IS A PROGNOSTIC FACTOR FOR 28-DAYS MORTALITY IN PATIENTS WITH SEPSIS VIA NEGATIVELY REGULATING ER STRESS
T3  - ISN World Congress of Nephrology (WCN). Kuala Lumpur Malaysia.
A1  - Zhu T.
A1  - Su Q.
A1  - Wang C.
A1  - Jiang H.
A1  - Chen J.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Introduction: Sepsis is a heterogeneous syndrome induced by infection and results in high mortality. Even though more than 100 biomarkers for sepsis prognosis were evaluated, prediction of patient outcomes in sepsis continues to be driven by clinical signs because of unsatisfactory specificity and sensitivity of these biomarkers. This study aimed to elucidate the key candidate genes involved in sepsis response and explore their downstream effects based on weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA). Downstream effects of the hub gene were then explored, which might provide potential targets for sepsis treatment in the future. Method(s): The dataset GSE63042 from the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) database was utilized for WGCNA. After construction of the gene co-expression network and identification of trait-related modules, hub gene was identified with the following criteria:1) DEGs; 2) GS > 0.2, MM > 0.8; 3) highest 20 Maximal Clique Centrality(MCC)value calculated by the Cytohubba package in Cytoscape. Subsequently, two datasets (GSE54514 and E-MTAB-4421) and a cohort (n=89) validation were performed. Logistic regression analysis was used to build a prediction model and the combined score was further tested in another sepsis cohort (n= 70). Then, patients and cecal ligation and puncture (CLP) mice was assayed to explore underlying mechanism of the hub gene. Result(s): In WGCNA analysis, we identified the hub gene SDF4 (stromal cell derived factor 4) from the M6 module, which was significantly associated with mortality. SDF4 was also down-regulated in patients who would later die compared to sepsis survivors in two datasets (GSE54514 and E-MTAB-4421) and cohort (n=89). Logistic regression analysis was used to build a prediction model and the combined score resulting in a satisfactory prognosis value (area under the ROC curve=0.908). The model was subsequently tested by another sepsis cohort (n=70, area under the ROC curve= 0.925). We next demonstrated that endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress tended to be more severe in patients PBMCs with negative outcomes compared to those with positive outcomes and SDF4 was related to this phenomenon. In addition, our results indicated that adenovirus-mediated Sdf4 overexpression attenuated ER stress in CLP mice. [Formula presented] Conclusion(s): In summary, our study indicates that incorporation of SDF4 can improve clinical parameters predictive value for the prognosis of sepsis,has great predictive value for the prognosis of sepsis, and decreased expression levels of SDF4 may contributes to excessive ER stress, which is associated with worsened outcomes, whereas overexpression of SDF4 attenuated such activation. No conflict of interestCopyright © 2022
