T1  - 29.2 Crisis Afar: An International Collaboration to Examine the Heterogeneous Outcomes of the COVID-19 Pandemic in Youth With Neurodevelopmental Conditions
T3  - The Scientific Proceedings of the 69th Annual Meeting of the American Academy of Child & Adolescent Psychiatry. Toronto Canada.
A1  - Di Martino A.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Objectives: The objectives are to assess the heterogeneity of COVID-19 pandemic outcomes and identify their predictors in youth with ASD and other neurodevelopmental disabilities (NDDs). Method(s): Parent responses to the CoRonavIruS health and Impact Survey (CRISIS) initiative adapted for Autism and Related Neurodevelopmental Conditions (AFAR) were cross-sectionally collected (April to October 2020) in 1275 children with ASD/NDD in 14 North American and European institutions. AFAR measures symptoms relevant to ASD/NDD before and during the pandemic, life and treatment service changes, and COVID-19 worries. To determine homogenous subgroups of outcomes, we ran hierarchical clustering across empirically derived factor scores of the change in daily living skills, restrictive-repetitive behaviors, problem behaviors, as well as the number of services lost and modified inside and outside school. Then, random forest (RF) assessed the predictive value of 20 variables including indices of sociodemographics, prepandemic clinical presentation and services received, restrictions in response to COVID-19 infections, and COVID-19 worries. Result(s): The 4-factor clustering solution was most optimal. Subtype 1 (20% of the aggregate sample) was characterized by behavioral worsening and marginal differences in service changes. The 3 other subtypes (collectively 80%) showed marginal behavioral changes but differed for service patterns. In Subtype 2 (51%), the number of services lost and modified were similar to the aggregate average. Subtype 3 (23%) had the highest number of services modified. Subtype 4 (6%) had the highest number of services lost. RF revealed that the number of services received before the pandemic, along with the stringency index in the child's living area, child's lifestyle stress, COVID-19 worries, and age had the highest predictive value. Conclusion(s): Consistent with prior research in the general population, heterogeneity of the impact in ASD/NDD is the norm. A relatively small group appears most vulnerable to the adverse impact of the pandemic on behavioral symptoms, while another is relatively more resilient, at least at the early stage of the pandemic. Prepandemic services and pandemic-related factors (eg, COVID-19 rates, local restrictions) differentially predict the impact on subgroups. LONG, ASD, TREATCopyright © 2022
