N2  - Background: Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) patients with pregnancy have high maternal mortality. This study aimed to provide clinical evidence with multidisciplinary team (MDT) management and to evaluate the risk factors for outcomes in PAH patients during the perinatal period. Method(s): We conducted a retrospective evaluation of pregnant PAH patients between May 2015 and May 2021. Result(s): Twenty-two patients (24 pregnancies) were included in this study and received MDT management, and 21 pregnancies chose to continue pregnancy with cesarean section. Twenty-one (87.5%) pregnancies received single or combined pulmonary vasodilators. The maternal survival rate of PAH patients reached 91.7%. Fifteen (62.5%) pregnancies were complicated with severe adverse events (SAEs, including death, heart failure, respiratory failure, and postoperative infections). Compared with the subgroups without SAE, patients complicated with one of adverse events had a lower admitted percutaneous oxygen saturation (SpO2), lower albumin, lower fibrinogen, longer activated partial thromboplastin time, longer coagulation time, higher pulmonary artery systolic pressure (PASP), and higher blood pressure (P < 0.05). At the moment of fetal removal retrieval, lower SpO2 was related to death (P = 0.00) and RF (P = 0.049), and higher BP was related to HF (P < 0.05) compared to subgroups without SAE. Conclusion(s): The survival rate of parturients with PAH was improved in relation to MDT and pulmonary vasodilator therapy during the perinatal period compared with previous studies. SpO2, albumin, PASP, blood pressure, and coagulation function should be considered important indicators for predicting the risk of SAE in PAH patients during pregnancy.Copyright © 2022 American College of Cardiology Foundation
