Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Introduction: An anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) Registry was established at the Queensland Children's Hospital (QCH) to investigate paediatric ACL injuries, supporting the International Olympic Committee's recommendations for research priorities. This Registry collects comprehensive domains including medical imaging, concomitant injuries, instrumented laxometry, physical/functional measurements, surgical techniques, complications, and patient reported outcome measures (PROMs). The primary aim of this study was to report baseline measures, focusing on demographics, injury characteristics, and PROMs. Method(s): Patients with ACL tears confirmed by MRI were prospectively recruited from QCH between September 2018 and January 2022. Baseline data was collected from electronic medical records, patient interview, PROMs (KOOS, IKDC, HSS Pedi-FABS), and standardized physical assessment, and entered into a REDCap database. Data for 200 consecutive enrolments was summarized using descriptive statistics with median (IQR) presented for non-normal variables, and percentages for frequencies Results: Median age at injury was 14.3 (IQR 13.0, 15.3) with a range of 6 - 17.3. Males represented 56.5% of the cohort with 50% being skeletally immature. In contrast, most females were skeletally mature (86%) and post-menarchal (93%). There was an over-representation of Maori and Pacific Islander (20.5%), and indigenous (8.5%) patients relative to population prevalence, with both groups having a predominance of females. Across all tears, 82% occurred during sport, and two thirds were non-contact. More females (38.8%) reported a contact mechanism while playing sport compared to males (29.4%). The main causal sport for both females (28%) and males (40%) were the rugby codes. For males, the next most causative sports were soccer, basketball, athletics, touch football, AFL, and netball. For females, they were netball, touch football, soccer, AFL, basketball, and athletics. A peak of injury prevalence (22%) was observed in August. 78% of participants reported a pain level of 7/10 or greater (IKDC q3). Many children reported mechanical knee symptoms with 54% reporting locking (IKDC q7) and 63% reporting catching (IKDC q8). 78% were unable to complete "hard" activities, indicating difficulties playing sport or meeting the national physical health recommendations for moderate to vigorous activity. The average IKDC score for this cohort was 60 which is worse than age matched peers (89 - 94) but better than children undergoing patella stabilisation surgery (39-41). Discussion(s): Analysis of 200 Queensland children with ACL injuries revealed a heterogenous group. Females were more likely to experience a sporting contact mechanism possibly reflecting increased female participation in sports such as rugby codes. These findings are similar to other epidemiology studies with regards to sex and age but have described other parameters in more detail and revealed the impact of symptoms and reduced participation in physical activity. Further research will leverage this baseline information to determine independent and combined predictors of ACL injuries, and knee joint health into adulthood. Impact and applications to the field: Prevention programs have been shown to be effective in reducing ACL injuries when targeting at-risk groups. Ongoing Registry longitudinal data collection will identify risk factors for primary and subsequent ACL injuries and influence decision making in the management of this challenging problem. Conflict of interest statement: My co-authors and I acknowledge that we have no conflict of interest of relevance to the submission of this abstract.Copyright © 2022
