T1  - S-10-03 Current state of developmental immunotoxicity testing & knowledge gaps
T3  - XVIth International Congress of Toxicology (ICT 2022) - UNITING IN TOXICOLOGY. Maastricht Netherlands.
A1  - Nygaard U.C.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Immunotoxic exposures leading to an unbalanced immune system may have a huge economic and societal impact, by promoting adverse effects like immune suppression, -stimulation, hypersensitivity and autoimmunity. A number of these diseases have an early life onset, and developmental immunotoxicity is of particular concern. Although considerable work has been performed since the 1970, progress in regulatory immunotoxicity testing and inclusion of functional immune endpoints in regulatory guidelines have been limited. Presently, the only OECD guideline tests assessing functional immunotoxicity are for skin sensitization and, only when triggered, developmental immunotoxicity (DIT) is assessed in the extended one-generation reproductive toxicity study (EOGRTS). Inclusion of the DIT cohort in the EOGRTS assesses antigen-specific T-cell dependent antibody-responses (TDAR) in offspring after developmental exposure and may reveal effects on immune suppression or stimulation. The current challenge is that this cohort is only included if triggers are presented. While only observational (not functional) immune endpoints, in adult animals, are available from mandatory regulatory testing, this may lead to lack of regulatory testing of putative developmental immunotoxicity since mechanisms and sensitivity may strongly differ between adult and developmental exposure. The increase in early-life prevalences of immune diseases as well as other disease states where inflammatory processes play a central role illustrates that we are not sufficiently protecting the population from detrimental exposures during development. In addition to the need for more knowledge on the ability of the DIT EOGRTS cohort to identify immunotoxic compounds, there are also a knowledge and testing gap for other arms of immunotoxicity as hypersensitivity and autoimmunity. Identification of predictive regulatory tests has probably been hampered by the complexity of the fine-tuned immune system and its adverse outcomes. Recent technological and biostatistical advances allow for high dimensional assessment of immune function and systems immunology approaches. Such approaches hold promise for future advancements in identifying mechanisms and causal relationships in (developmental) immunotoxicology. The combination of experimental and epidemiological studies is expected to be important for future immune toxicity evaluations.Copyright © 2022 Elsevier B.V.
