T1  - Predicting exacerbation in adults with cystic fibrosis using a 1-minute sit-to-stand test
T3  - North American Cystic Fibrosis Conference. Philadelphia United States.
A1  - Fallahi L.
A1  - Carbone A.
A1  - Bochicchio E.
A1  - Koff J.
A1  - Stanley G.
A1  - McGowan B.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Background: Forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) is typically used in people with cystic fibrosis (PwCF) to measure disease severity, progression, and therapeutic efficacy. PwCF typically have exercise intolerance, so exercise testing is imperative to monitor for physical limitations and potential adverse reactions. Other outcome measures such as the cardiopulmonary exercise test and 6-minute walk test have previously used, but they require significant resources. The aim of this quality improvement project was to determine whether there are statistically significant correlations between changes in FEV1 or percentage predicted FEV1 (FEV1pp) and 1-minute sit to stand (1MSTS) test outcomes. Method(s): PwCF or PwCF-like diseases who regularly attend Yale New Haven's Adult CF Clinic were selected to participated in the 1MSTS. Each patient was educated on the appropriate procedure. Information was obtained on age, sex, FEV1, FEV1pp, pre-heart rate, and pre-oxygen saturation. The patients then performed as many sit to stands as possible in 1 minute, with heart rate and oxygen saturation recorded at the 30- second mark and upon completion of the test. The total number of sit to stands was recorded, along with rest breaks, subjective comments, and adverse events. Result(s): Data were collected over 2 years in the CF clinic. Sixty-six PwCF and PwCF-like disease completed the 1MSTS and pulmonary function tests during the clinic visit. Replicate values represent patient data collected at separate clinic visits; final N = 109. One patient experienced desaturation to 89% and recovered at rest without additional interventions. There were no reports of syncope, chest pain, nausea, vomiting, orthopedic injury, or exacerbation of pulmonary symptoms. We found that performance on the 1MSTS predicted FEV1 (r = 0.28, p < 0.01) and FEV1pp (r = 0.22; p < 0.05). Simple liner regression was used to determine whether 1MSTS predicted FEV1. The fitted regression model was FEV1 = 5.82 + 3.173*(STS). The overall regression was statistically significant (R2 = 0.078, F(1107) = 9, p < 0.01). Performance on the 1MSTS significantly predicted FEV1 (beta = 11.71, p < 0.01). Simple liner regression of 1MSTS did not significantly predict FEV1pp. Subgroup analysis for age, sex, and FEV1 greater than 40% are ongoing. Conclusion(s): The 1MSTS test was integrated successfully into CF clinic visits through a combination of spirometry and physical therapy assessment. There was a weak correlation between performance on the 1MSTS test and FEV1 and FEV1pp. Limitations include variations in administration of the 1MSTS test, patient effort, and coordination of pulmonary function tests with clinic visit. Subgroup analysis will investigate the strength of correlation between 1MSTS and FEV1 when age, sex, or baseline FEV1 is controlled. Despite needing to continue to analyze these associations, this quality improvement project has established that the 1MSTS test is time efficient, safe, cost effective, and feasible to administer. This test could assist CF clinic centers in obtaining insight into a patient's tolerance for exercise and the need for further interdisciplinary team evaluation. Also, amidst a global pandemic during which telehealth has become a regular means of patient-provider interactions, the 1MSTS test could be helpful in providing objective data, when pulmonary function tests may not be accessible, because it can be administered through video. Acknowledgements: This work was supported by OUTRun 38, Yale New Haven Hospital Inpatient Rehabilitation Services, Yale University, and the Cystic Fibrosis Foundation.Copyright © 2022, European Cystic Fibrosis Society. All rights reserved
