Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Background and Aim: Predicting clinical outcomes after stopping nucleos(t)ide analogue (NA) therapy is challenging. The amount of circulating HBV RNAs (cirB-RNA) in peripheral blood may reflect hepatitis B virus (HBV) covalently closed circular DNA transcriptional activity within infected hepatocytes and predict for clinical outcomes. We evaluated the role of cirB-RNA levels to predict clinical outcomes in a large cohort of patients enrolled in a prospective NA stop study. Method(s): The Melbourne HBV-STOP study was a prospective multicenter study of NA discontinuation in 110 hepatitis B e-antigen-negative non-cirrhotic patients who had achieved long-term virological suppression on treatment. All patients were followed for 96 weeks. A subset with serum samples stored at -80degreeC were included in this study. cirB-RNA levels were measured at baseline and longitudinally using the Roche cobas 6800/8800 HBV RNA investigational assay. Clinical outcomes of interest included rates of virological relapse (HBV DNA > 2000 IU/mL), biochemical relapse (alanine aminotransferase [ALT] > 2 x upper limit of normal [ULN]), hepatitis flare (ALT > 5 x ULN), and hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) loss. Result(s): There were 65 participants had serum samples available at baseline and longitudinally off-treatment. In total, 969 patient samples were tested. At the time of stopping NA therapy, the patients' median age was 56 years (31-74), 54% were male, and 75% were Asian. Median HBsAg level was 701 IU/mL (1.6-19 912). Following treatment withdrawal, virological relapse was observed in 58/65 participants (89%) by Week 96. Biochemical relapse occurred in 38/65 participants (58%), hepatitis flare in 25/65 (38%), and HBsAg loss in 4/65 (6%). At the time of stopping NA, cirB-RNA was detectable in 16/65 participants (25%). The detection of cirB-RNA at baseline was associated with higher risk of biochemical relapse (88% vs 62%) and hepatitis flare (62% vs 28%). No participant with detectable cirB-RNA at baseline achieved HBsAg loss (0 vs 8%). Participants in whom cirB-RNA was not detectable at baseline were more likely to achieve sustained disease remission (38% vs 12%). The combination of undetectable cirB-RNA and HBsAg < 100 IU/mL at baseline was associated with a higher likelihood of disease remission (67% vs 24%). Among participants who were negative for cirB-RNA at baseline, cirB-RNA became detectable in 96% during follow-up. Off-treatment levels of cirB-RNA correlated with serum HBV DNA levels, and sharply rising levels of cirB-RNA were associated with risk of hepatitis flare (P < 0.05). Conclusion(s): cirB-RNA is a potential biomarker for guiding clinical decision making before stopping long-term NA therapy. Detectable cirB-RNA at baseline predicts for a high risk of clinical relapse and hepatitis flare. The combination of undetectable cirB-RNA and HBsAg level < 100 IU/mL at baseline predicts for sustained disease remission offtreatment.
