A1  - Yip A.
A1  - Dvorak C.
A1  - Cowan M.
A1  - Puck J.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Rationale: Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) describes several profound defects of lymphocyte development that require hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT) for survival. Depending on genotype and conditioning regimen, 10-40% of patients fail to achieve immune recovery post-HCT and remain at risk for life-threatening infections. It is difficult to predict which patients will fail immune reconstitution, but we hypothesize that these patients have lower T-cell diversity as early as 3 months post-HCT, when donor T-cells can first be detected. Method(s): Using samples from SCID patients transplanted at University of California, San Francisco, we performed high throughput sequencing of T-cell receptor beta (TCRbeta) transcripts pre-HCT and at 3, 6, 12 months, and yearly post-HCT. Results were compared with clinical and immunologic outcomes. Result(s): Healthy, term newborns had comparable TCRbeta diversity to healthy adults, whereas SCID patients started with low diversity that normalized 6-12 months post-HCT. TCRbeta transcripts demonstrated significantly decreased diversity, equitability, and information density in SCID patients requiring second HCT (n=5) compared to successful single HCT (n=17). These decreases were present as early as 3 months post-HCT and persisted up to 2 years post-HCT (p <0.01 for all three indices by two-way ANOVA). Conclusion(s): SCID patients with poor post-HCT immune recovery appear to have fundamentally decreased T-cell diversity that does not improve with time. T-cell diversity may be a useful early biomarker for predicting the need for second HCT and for assessing efficacy of newer approaches, such as gene therapy. Future work will include additional patients from the Pediatric Immune Deficiency Treatment Consortium #6901 prospective study (n=345).Copyright © 2021
