A1  - Garbellano D.
A1  - Gesualdo M.
A1  - Caldarola P.
A1  - Massari F.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Background: The estimation of glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) based on serum creatinine values provide prognostic information in patients with heart failure (HF). In addition to evaluation of fluid status, bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA) can assess the body cell mass that combined with serum creatinine levels is able to develop a new GFR-prediction model (Donadio formula). The Aim of this Study: was to evaluate whether Donadio formula predict all-cause mortality and compare they accuracy with mostly creatinine-based formulas used to asses renal function in HF population as Cockroft-Gault, MDRD-4 (Modification of Diet in renal Disease Study) and Chronic Kidney Disease Epidemiology Collaboration (CKD- EPI) formulas. Methods and Results: A total of 436 subjects (52% men; age 75+/-11 years and 42% with acute HF) were studied. Serum creatinine and BIA was performed at admission. Over the study period (median 463 days, IQR 287-669) 92 patients (21%) died. Areas under the receiver operator characteristic curves for Cockroft-Gault formulas (AUC=0.75) was significantly higher than that for the Donadio (AUC=0.72), MDRD4 (AUC=0.68), and CKD-EPI (AUC=0.71) formulas. While the performances were non different among latter eGFR. After multivariate adjustment, all eGFR formulas ere independent predictors of death, and for the decrease of 1 point of ml/min/1.73 m2 the Cockroft-Gault, Donadio, MDRD4 and CKD-EPI increase the mortality of 2.6%, 1.5%, 1.2% and 1.6%, respectively. Conclusion(s): In patients with HF, Donadio formula was independent predictor of mortality with similar accuracy of MDRD4 and CKD-EPI formulas, but less accurate of Cockroft-Gault. These findings confer to BIA an further important function useful in management of HF patients.
