T1  - Vancomycin dosing recommendations for obese patients with varying renal function admitted to the ward or icu
T3  - Annual Meeting American College of Clinical Pharmacology, ACCP 2021. Virtual.
A1  - Zhang T.
A1  - Krekels E.H.
A1  - Smit C.
A1  - Van Dongen E.P.
A1  - Bruggemann R.J.
A1  - Knibbe C.A.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Statement of Purpose, Innovation or Hypothesis: Vancomycin is used to treat severe infections. Its varying pharmacokinetics (PK), depending on renal function and potential nephrotoxicity, make it challenging to dose in patients with obesity, renal insufficiency, critical illness or a combination of these characteristics. How obesity and disease severity impact vancomycin PK remains unquantified, while dosing guidelines aiming at optimal exposure before individual results from Therapeutic DrugMonitoring (TDM) are available, are urgently needed. The objective of this study is to quantify the PK of vancomycin in obese patients with varying degrees of renal function admitted to the hospital ward or ICU and to provide model-derived dosing recommendations. Description of Methods and Materials: From electronic health records, data from patients admitted to the ward or ICU with BMI >=25 kg/m2, who received >=1 doses of vancomycin and had >=1 vancomycin plasma concentrations and >=1 serum creatinine measurements were extracted. Patients receiving renal replacement therapy were excluded. These data were pooled with previously-published prospective data from eight non-obese and 20 (morbidly) obese but otherwise healthy subjects [1]. The population PK model was performed using NONMEM v7.4 and validated by bootstrap and normalized prediction distribution errors (NPDE) analysis. Monte Carlo simulations were performed to design an optimal dosing guideline targeting an AUC0-24 >=400 and <600 mg h/L. Data and Results: A total of 1,499 serum concentrations were available from 217 obese hospitalized patients (total body weight [TBW]: 59-143 kg, renal function estimates with chronic kidney disease epidemiology collaboration equation [CKD-EPI]: 5.99-176.99 ml/min/1.73m2, ICU %: 25.81%), in conjunction with 249 concentrations from 28 non-obese and obese otherwise healthy subjects (TBW: 60-235 kg, CKD-EPI: 70.28-127.96 ml/min/1.73m2). A three-compartment model best described the data. Clearance (CL) was 5.05 L/h in a patient with a TBW of 70 kg and CKD-EPI of 75 ml/min/1.73m2 and was found to have an exponential relationship with TBW and CKD-EPI with exponents of 0.417 and 0.713, respectively. Compared to healthy subjects, CL was 41.2% lower in ward patients and 51.3% lower in ICU patients. The peripheral volume of distribution was 1.33 L and increased with TBW with an exponent of 2.84. Additionally, this volume was 34.8 times higher in patients compared to healthy subjects. This model was validated to be robust and have good predictability by bootstrap and NPDE. Model-based simulations were performed to develop a safe and effective vancomycin dosing guideline based on CKD-EPI, TBW and patient status. See Table 1 &Figure 1. Interpretation, Conclusion or Significance: In this large study, patients admitted to the ward or ICU were found to have substantially-lower clearance values compared to healthy subjects, with renal dysfunction resulting in even lower clearance values, while obese patients were found to have higher clearance values. These results can be used to get to individualized doses across the entire hospital population that may ultimately not only lead to optimized efficacy and safety, but potentially also to a reduced need for (repeated) TDM of this nephrotoxic drug.
