T1  - Emicizumab use in pups and children
T3  - Congress of the European Association for Haemophilia and Allied Disorders. Virtual.
A1  - Kenet G.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Emicizumab (Hemlibra, Roche) is a humanized bi-specific antibody directed against activated factor IX (FIXa) and factor X (FX), functioning as a bridge between them, thereby mimicking the co-factor function of activated FVIII. The drug is administered subcutaneously, which makes it especially suitable for treating pediatric populations. Never the less, real-world data on emicizumab use and monitoring in pediatric severe hemophilia A (HA) patients are scarce. Two multi-center phase III studies that examined emicizumab prophylaxis in HA patients with inhibitors were HAVEN 1, which investigated adults and adolescents, and HAVEN 2, which investigated 2-12 years old. The median age of the pediatric study patients was 7 years, and only two patients were younger than 2 years of age. Following the HAVEN 3-4 trials, emicizumab received FDA and EMA approval for the prevention and reduction of bleeding in patients with severe HA; however, the HAVEN 3-4 trials on emicizumab prophylaxis in non-inhibitor HA patients included mainly adults and adolescents. Real-world data on the safety and efficacy of emicizumab prophylaxis among the pediatric hemophilia population are limited. Recent British guidelines advised caution when considering emicizumab prophylaxis in children younger than 2 years of age due to the limited data available in this age group. Small prospectively followed cohorts of children with FVIII inhibitors demonstrated that emicizumab prophylaxis is safe and reduces patients' annual bleeding rate (ABR) when administered weekly or every other week to pediatric patients, including infants and toddlers. In these studies emicuzumab was administered either as a single therapy or along with immune tolerance induction (ITI). Notably, emicizumab prophylaxis during ITI was well tolerated, yet, co-administration of FVIII and emicizumab may pose a substantial financial burden on healthcare systems and future studies will be necessary to compare treatment outcomes of a combined ITI and emicizumab regimen with a standard ITI or emicizumab prophylaxis only regimen. The only data on non-inhibitor children are currently derived from a cohort of Japanese patients (aged 4 months to 10 years) who had been successfully treated by emicizumab prophylaxis every 2 or 4 weeks, multicenter US cohorts (including adults as well as children) and our group's publications, that evaluated and monitored emicizumab prophylaxis in a cohort of 40 children with severe HA, including 22 non-inhibitor patients and 9 infants younger than 1 year. In the pediatric cohorts reported so far breakthrough bleeding episodes occurred in 30%-50% of treated children and were mostly trauma-related. Bleeding risk positively correlated with the length of emicizumab prophylaxis. Emicizumab treatment may be associated with several risks, such as the development of antidrug antibodies, loss of efficacy, thrombosis and thrombotic microangiopathy. Real world data regarding such complications is currently insufficient, as thrombosis in children is extremely rare, yet caution is advised in children with central venous lines and during potentially hyper-coagulable situations with increased pro-thrombotic risk (e.g. COVID-19 infection, surgery, co-administration of bypass agents, etc.). Longitudinal laboratory monitoring of prophylactically treated children discloses normalization of aPTT values, correlating with increase in emicizumab plasma levels. Improvement in thrombin generation parameters was observed in treated children, however among young infants lower values were noted. Notably, as bleeding episodes are mainly trauma-related, laboratory monitoring has a limited role in predicting bleeding risk. With the approval of emicizumab prophylaxis for pediatric HA patients without FVIII inhibitors, healthcare givers and parents face new choices and challenges, and individual discussions should be held with families, aimed at tailoring the most feasible and favorable prophylaxis regimen. Such discussion should consider parental and patients' preferences, venous access, bleeding history (including presence of target joints), physical activity and issues related to patients' adherence and compliance. Although some very young children have already been treated, initiation of emicizumab prophylaxis in previously untreated patients (PUP), either with FVIII provided in order to induce tolerance, or as a single therapy, deserves further contemplation and should await results of the planned HAVEN-7 study which will evaluate the efficacy, safety, pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of emicizumab in participants from birth to 12 months. Overall, emicizumab prophylaxis reported so far in pediatric HA patients was safe and well-tolerated. Decisions on guidelines for patients' transition to emicizumab and on PUP treatment should be considered together with the families and the multidisciplinary hemophilia treatment center's team. Emicizumab monitoring and its potential clinical implications warrant further evaluation in future larger prospective studies.
