T1  - REFRACTORY NONTUBERCULOUS MYCOBACTERIAL PULMONARY DISEASE: DESCRIPTIVE ANALYSIS FROM THE UNITED STATES BRONCHIECTASIS AND NTM RESEARCH REGISTRY
T3  - CHEST 2021 Annual Meeting. Virtual, Online.
A1  - Aksamit T.
A1  - Choate R.
A1  - Mannino D.
A1  - Wang P.
A1  - Wu J.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - TOPIC: Chest Infections TYPE: Original Investigations PURPOSE: Nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (NTMPD) cases are increasing. The evaluation and management of NTMPD continues to be complex and challenging, especially for those with refractory NTMPD (lack of sputum conversion despite >= 6 months of guideline-based therapy). Little is known regarding the natural history and discriminatory risk profile for those subsequently classified as refractory NTMPD. The objective of these analyses was to investigate the demographics and clinical characteristics of patients at the time of treatment who later develop refractory NTMPD versus those who do not. METHOD(S): Data from the COPD Foundation US Bronchiectasis and NTM Research Registry was used. Patients were included if they received treatment for NTMPD. Registry participants were stratified into a refractory group if they received oral Clofazimine/Bedaquiline or inhaled Amikacin/ALIS or were considered "treatment failure" (i.e. culture positive despite >=6 months of treatment).Descriptive statistics were computed for demographic and clinical characteristics stratified by refractory status and NTM species. Values between the strata were compared using Kruskal-Wallis test for continuous and Chi-square or Fisher's exact tests for categorical variables. RESULT(S): A total of 770 patients receiving treatment for NTMPD were analyzed. Four hundred seventy-four (61.6%) patients were classified as non-refractory and 296 (38.4%) refractory. Demographics and clinical characteristics (overall) that were similar between the two groups at the time of treatment included age (67.1 years), female gender (82.2%), race (93.6% white), smoking history (53.1% never smokers), COPD (17.2%) and history of Pseudomonas (20.8%). Demographics and clinical characteristics that differed at the time of treatment between non-refractory and refractory NTMPD patients included BMI (22.0 versus 21.1, p=0.013), FVC% predicted (83.61 versus 76.95, p<0.0001) and fibrocavitary/cavitary disease (16.1% versus 32.3%, p=0.0009). Subgroup analyses by specific NTM species (M. avium complex (MAC) versus M. abscesses (MAB) versus combined MAC and MAB) did not demonstrate significant difference in demographic or clinical characteristics at the time of treatment for the two groups. CONCLUSION(S): Analysis of demographics and clinical characteristics at the time of treatment suggests that patients who subsequently are classified as refractory NTMPD are more likely to have a lower BMI, reduced lung function, and the presence of fibrocavitary or cavitary disease. These findings do not appear to be influenced by specific NTM species although further investigations are warranted. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Approximatively 1/3 of treated NTMPD became refractory with differences in some clinical characteristics at the time of treatment between the patients who were subsequently classified as non-refractory and refractory NTMPD. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Timothy Aksamit, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Radmila Choate, source=Web Response Employee relationship with GlaxoSmithKline Please note: >$100000 by David Mannino, source=Web Response, value=Salary Employee relationship with GSK Please note: 8/2017-9/2020 Added 04/22/2021 by David Mannino, source=Web Response, value=Salary Consultant relationship with COPD Foundation Please note: 11/2020 - present Added 04/22/2021 by David Mannino, source=Web Response, value=Salary Employee relationship with Insmed Incorporated Please note: Mar 2017 to Present Added 04/22/2021 by Ping Wang, source=Web Response, value=Salary No relevant relationships by Jasmanda Wu, source=Web ResponseCopyright © 2021 American College of Chest Physicians
