A1  - Nguyen S.
A1  - Chan K.H.R.
A1  - Cadena J.
A1  - Soper B.
A1  - Kiszka P.
A1  - Womack L.
A1  - Work M.
A1  - Duggan J.
A1  - Haller S.T.
A1  - Hanrahan J.
A1  - Kennedy D.J.
A1  - Mukundan D.
A1  - Ray P.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Introduction/Background As the number of COVID-19 cases surges in the United States, health care facility resources are strained to capacity. Hence it is critical at the first encounter with a COVID -19 patient as evidenced by a positive nasopharyngeal PCR test for SARS-CoV-2, to determine whether hospitalization is required, or outpatient treatment can be utilized without risk of deterioration or increased morbidity and mortality using readily available demographic information, comorbidities and available biomarkers (vitals and laboratory Results). With the increasing availability of Electronic Health records (EHR) of hospitalized COVID-19 patients, machine learning (ML) based decision support systems for patients with COVID19 triaged at the point of contact with the health care system is a promising solution which has been explored extensively in the recent literature. However, for wide-spread adaptability of such ML based systems, two fundamental challenges remain. First, to gain the confidence of health care providers, clinical interpretability of the ML algorithms is crucial. Second, the cost as well as accessibility of various laboratory tests vary substantially across different facilities as well as across geographic locations. Hence accounting for general test availability and costs in the ML based decision tool is also critical. Objective(s) On this background we sought to 1) develop a machine learning (ML) based approach for predicting adverse outcomes, such as the need for mechanical ventilation or death based on demographic information, co-morbidities and biomarkers collected close to the date of a positive PCR test; 2) gain insight into the decision-making process of the ML algorithm based on interpretable ML tools such as SHAPley values; and 3) identify and account for unequal costs of clinical features in the ML based decision support system by finding the optimal set of biomarkers that Results in the highest utility under a given cost structure. Methods A retrospective study was performed using electronic health records (EHRs) from patients in the largest health care system in northwest Ohio and southeastern Michigan (ProMedica Health System). The patient data used in this study corresponds to patients who 1) had a positive nasopharyngeal PCR test for SARS-CoV-2 between 03/20/2020 and 12/29/ 2020, and 2) were admitted to the hospital shortly before or after the positive result. A total of 1312 patients met these criteria. Demographics (e.g., age, race, co-morbidities, insurance status), vitals and a wide range of lab tests available within 3 days of a first positive PCR test were used to train machine learning models to predict a patient's risk of adverse outcomes, namely, composite ventilation and death. Extensive hyperparameter search was carried out with cross-validation to find best model parameters and avoid overfitting. Model performance was evaluated with Average Precision and AUC ROC. Moreover, game theoretic SHAPley values were used to gain clinical insight and validation of the feature attributions of the ML models. The best set of features under a budget constraint was optimized via exhaustive search across all combinations of features. Naively iterating through the possible permutations of features would result in intractable exponential runtime. Instead, features were grouped into clinically meaningful subsets (e.g., Complete Blood Count, Basic Metabolic Profile, Blood Gases, etc.), thus significantly reducing the possible search space. Results Based on readily available demographic information, co-morbidities and available biomarkers, the performance of various Machine Learning (ML) approaches (e.g., XGBoost, Random Forest, Gaussian Process, etc) for predicting ventilation status or death were tested. Compared to a baseline Linear Logistic Classifier model, more advanced nonlinear or tree-based classifiers provide improved performance both in terms of Average Precision (AP) and AUC ROC (table 1). The importance of individual features is captured via game theoretic SHAPley values and is provided in Figure 1. While recent studies have investigated ML approaches as well as feature attribution in the context of prediction of adverse outcomes for COVID positive patients, our study is unique in terms of incorporating the cost of collecting a clinical feature for feature selection. For a pre-defined cost structure as provided in table 2, the optimal feature sets for a given cost constraint is provided in table 3. It is interesting to note that despite Procalcitonin being a highly relevant feature (as seen in figure 1), the addition of Procalcitonin to the models, in some cases, yielded only marginal increases in utility as measured by AP. For example, adding Procalcitonin to the set of features [DCM, BMP, LDH, Sedrate, BNP, Ferritin] increases AP by 4% (from 0.344 to 0.357) while increasing cost by 27%. Thus, relatively expensive features such as Procalcitonin may not provide meaningful benefit to patient care in a setting of cost-constraints or limited test availability. Of even greater interest is the observation that a feature set consisting of the following clinical variables [BMP, CBC, DDimer, LDH, CRP] provides an AP which is within 6% of the AP provided by the best set of variables. Thus, an increase of the budget/cost constraint of 75% (i.e., from '8' to '14') only provides a 6% increase in the AP of the model (table 2). Conclusion This study presents a fast and accurate method to evaluate risk of clinical deterioration for patients with SARSCoV- 2 infection at the time of medical evaluation and to determine appropriate medical management for newly admitted patients. By identifying the top leading risk factors, health care providers can now determine a patient's outcome in a cost-effective manner. The current method provides a pointof- care model that allows improved resource allocation and evidence-based management that can be applied to improve patient care not only in the setting of COVID-19 but also other clinically challenging diseases. (Table Presented).
