T1  - Frailty and sarcopenia measures in liver transplantation candidates and their association with adverse outcomes after liver transplantation
T3  - GESA AGW 2021. Virtual.
A1  - Kimber J.
A1  - Woodman R.
A1  - Schembri D.
A1  - Ramachandran J.
A1  - Muller K.
A1  - Chen J.
A1  - Wigg A.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Background and Aim: Measures of frailty and sarcopenia can be used for liver transplantation (LT) candidates to help guide transplant teams on their future risk of adverse outcomes. However, the comparative utility of these measures in predicting important clinical outcomes after LT requires further study. The aim of this work was to determine the comparative utility of several frailty and sarcopenia measures in predicting early adverse clinical outcomes after LT. Method(s):We performed a single-center, retrospective, observational cohort study of consecutive patients who underwent LTassessment and subsequent LT between 2015 and 2020. Clinical outcomes measured were hospital length of stay (LOS), intensive care (ICU) LOS, and severe infection or death within 12 months of LT. Frailty and sarcopenia measures examined for association with these outcomes included handgrip strength, mid-arm muscle circumference, and cardiopulmonary exercise testing measures (lactic threshold, peak VO2, and Ve/VCO2 slope). Multivariate analysis was performed using logistic and Cox regression models, with all analyses adjusted for age, sex, Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) and Child-Pugh scores, etiology, and Charlson Comorbidity Index. Result(s): Data were analyzed for 127 transplants on 125 patients. Patients were predominantly male (64%), with a median age of 57 years (IQR, 49-63) and median MELD score of 16 (IQR, 11-21). The most common etiologies were combined hepatitis C virus and hepatocellular carcinoma (18%) and alcoholic liver disease (17%). Sepsis within 12 months was predicted by handgrip strength, with the lowest tertile more likely to have sepsis (28.1%) compared with those in the middle (12.2%) or highest (10.8%) tertiles (Fig. 1). In adjusted logistic regression analysis, the odds of sepsis were significantly lower in patients in the highest versus lowest tertile (odds ratio, 0.004; 95% CI, 0.000-0.125; P = 0.002). ICU LOS was associated with peak VO2 (hazard ratio [HR], 1.83; 95% CI, 1.06-3.16; P = 0.03) and Ve/VCO2 slope (HR, 0.71; 95% CI, 0.58-0.88; P = 0.002). Hospital LOS was associated with handgrip strength on a continuous scale (HR = 103, 1.00-1.06; P = 0.034). There were no associations between sepsis, hospital LOS, or ICU LOS and mid-arm muscle circumference. The 1-year mortality rate after LT was 5%, with no frailty or sarcopenia measures significantly associated with mortality on multivariate analysis. Conclusion(s): Handgrip strength and cardiopulmonary exercise testing measures provided more accurate prediction of severe sepsis, prolongedhospital LOS, and hospital LOS 12 months after LT compared with mid-arm muscle circumference. These data suggest that bedside testing of handgrip strength, in particular, may be a simple and accurate way to identify transplant candidates at higher risk of adverse outcomes during the early post-LT period. Identification of high-risk LT candidates could enable improved risk assessment for transplant teams and opportunities for prehabilitation to improve outcomes after LT.
