T1  - Predictors of deep venous thrombosis in the hospitalized patients with liver cirrhosis in the u.s
T3  - 2020 Annual Scientific Meeting of the American College of Gastroenterology, ACG 2020. Nashville, TN United States.
A1  - Yassine A.A.
A1  - Abureesh M.
A1  - Alkhayyat M.
A1  - Deeb L.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: Patients with liver cirrhosis were traditionally considered to be autoanticoagulated, thus their risk of developing venous thromboembolism was thought to be decreased. Recent studies disclosed conflicting results regarding deep venous thrombosis (DVT) risk in cirrhotic patients. Moreso, the use of anticoagulants in those patients with advanced liver disease and esophageal varices is a challenging and often scary situation. The aim of our study is to evaluate the prevalence and risk association of DVT in hospitalized cirrhotic patients. METHOD(S): We queried a commercial database (Explorys Inc, Cleveland, OH); an aggregate of electronic health record data from 26 US healthcare systems. Excluding patients younger than 20, a cohort of patients with a Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine-Clinical Terms of liver cirrhosis and inpatient care between 2015 and 2019 was identified. Prevalence of DVT was calculated in the study group versus control group. Statistical analysis for multivariable model was performed using Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS version 25, IBM Corp). We adjusted for following factors: gender, race, obesity, hypoalbuminemia, Diabetes Mellitus (DM), viral hepatitis, and liver malignancy. RESULT(S): Among 9,990,290 hospitalized patients, 157,400 had liver cirrhosis. Baseline characteristics of patients with cirrhosis and control group are presented in Table 1. The prevalence of DVT in hospitalized cirrhotic patients was 3.29% compared to 3.18% in controls. Using Multivariate analysis model, DVT was inversely associated with cirrhosis in hospitalized patients [OR: 0.921; 95% CI 0.848-0.999, P < 0.0001] compared to controls. Predictors of developing DVT among patients with cirrhosis were non-Caucasian race, obesity (BMI >30), liver malignancy, hypoalbuminemia, and DM (Table 2).On the other hand, viral hepatitis related cirrhosis was less likely associated with DVT [OR: 0.775; 95% CI: 0.661-0.909 P < 0.001] compared to controls. CONCLUSION(S): Our study revealed that the prevalence of DVT in hospitalized cirrhotic patients was slightly higher than in non-cirrhotic subjects (3.29% versus 3.18%), however cirrhosis as an independent factor was associated with less risk of developing DVT during hospitalization. This poses a question regarding the need for DVT prophylaxis in hospitalized cirrhotic patients taking into consideration their increased risk of bleeding. Further studies are needed to clarify the need, efficacy and safety of DVT prophylaxis in this patient population. (Table Presented).
