T1  - Using prediction polling for infectious disease forecasting
T3  - International Congress on Infectious Diseases 2020. Kuala Lumpur Malaysia.
A1  - Sell T.
A1  - Warmbrod L.
A1  - Trotochaud M.
A1  - Ravi S.
A1  - Martin E.
A1  - Watson C.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Background: Early warning and situational awareness of emerging outbreaks have the potential to mitigate unfolding epidemics. We developed an online crowd-forecasting tool to help predict infectious disease outcomes and understand circumstances in which forecasters are most accurate. Methods and materials: A prediction polling platform was developed in collaboration with Lumenogic, a company with extensive experience with crowdsourcing techniques. Recruitment of participants (N = 288) was conducted through public health networks, including ProMED-mail, and was aimed toward public health experts, physicians, epidemiologists, modelers, risk experts, vector control officials, individuals with on-the-ground understanding of conditions surrounding disease outbreaks, and others who were interested in disease outbreaks. The performance of each participant was measured relative to other participants' performance in terms of timeliness and accuracy using a Brier score to create rankings. Questions were focused on a range of infectious disease outcomes, including intensity, spread, and case counts. Individual forecasts were aggregated to show crowd forecasts using a combination of temporal decay, differential weighting, and extremization that was developed in previous research. Result(s): Twenty-five questions were published and resolved January-June 2019. On average, 92 forecasters participated in each question. Forecasters correctly forecast 21 outcomes. In 17 instances forecasters provided the correct answer at least 1 week in advance of the outcome. Accurate predictions were provided on average 4 weeks ahead of the question resolution with a median of 2 weeks and maximum of 20 weeks. Professional expertise was not an indicator of better accuracy as those forecasters with greater experience provided less accurate forecasts. When available information, including official surveillance, media coverage, details about cases, and time for trends to emerge, is limited, forecasts were less accurate. Conclusion(s): Results indicate the potential utility of prediction polling for forecasting disease outcomes but highlight the need for application in information rich settings. Such crowd-sourced forecasting can predict disease trends and allow public health and policy-makers time to respond to an unfolding disease situation. These efforts will never replace traditional surveillance methods, since surveillance data is needed to verify outcomes, but can supplement traditional methods.Copyright © 2020
