T1  - Transfusion transmitted infectious disease (TTID) working party-surveillance, risk assessment and policy (SRAP) subgroup: Summary of recent activities
T3  - 36th International ISBT Congress. Virtual.
A1  - Steele W.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Background: The SRAP subgroup brings together members of the ISBT TTID Working Party interested in using surveillance (blood donor, recipient and general population) data, research methodologies and modeling to assess transfusion transmitted infectious disease risks and implications for policy. Aim(s): To describe how SRAP brings together surveillance and risk assessment in relation to policy and to report recent and upcoming work. Method(s): SRAP has 22 current affiliated members from the TTID WP. In addition to the annual TTID meeting, the group meets by teleconference quarterly to review the status of projects and collaborations. SRAP members also participate in project-specific team meetings or subgroup calls as necessary for those collaborations. In general, the group uses surveillance data for quantitative risk assessments, aiming to estimate as accurately as possible the probability of an event occurring (such as releasing an infectious unit of blood into inventory). These types of assessments can be used to inform evidence-based policy formulation. Result(s): SRAP has several projects and activities related to the policies surrounding the deferral of men who have sex with men (MSM) as many countries around the world have been evaluating changing or changing this deferral. A SRAP team published a mathematical model predicting HIV risk after reducing the deferral period for MSM to 1 year and comparing the model prediction to postimplementation observational data from England and Canada for validation. Continuing with this work the team was able to use the model to estimate HIV residual risk in Canada should they move to a 3-month deferral. Currently, the team is expanding their work to model other strategies such as alternative risk questioning (e.g., not asking about MSM history), removing the MSM criteria with plasma product quarantine/retest or using pathogen reduction technology, or a combination of these. For the past two years at the AABB Annual meeting, in conjunction with the AABB Donor History Task Force, the group has sponsored an Educational Session on an MSM topic: the first focused on the complexity of assessing compliance and the psychology behind it, and the second on the impact of changes in MSM deferrals in different countries. Both sessions featured speakers from the TTID WP and outside experts. Another team collaborated with the Parasite subgroup on a manuscript to retrospectively compare how decisions around the malaria blood safety policies of five non-endemic countries had been made versus how they would have been made using the ABO Risk-Based Decision-Making Framework. New and ongoing projects include work with the Parasite subgroup on Chagas Disease risk mitigation strategies and a collaboration with the Virus subgroup to develop a Nucleic Acid Testing (NAT) survey that will be looking at the use of NAT in different jurisdictions. Finally, the Transmissible Spongiform Encephalopathy (TSE) subgroup was formally merged into SRAP in 2019 as many countries are now changing their policies surrounding deferral for these diseases and the work of TSE will better fit under SRAP. Summary: The focus of SRAP is mathematical modelling of risk and application of surveillance data and risk assessment to policy formulation. This work has mainly been focused on modeling HIV risk related to changes to the MSM deferral, while continuing to contribute to other TTID projects where surveillance and risk assessment techniques are needed.
