T1  - Sleep duration before an E. coli challenge predicts diarrhea severity during subsequent infection
T3  - 25th Congress of the European Sleep Research Society, ESRS 2020. Virtual.
A1  - Mantua J.
A1  - Gutierrez R.
A1  - Isidean S.
A1  - Alcala A.
A1  - Testa K.
A1  - Talaat K.
A1  - Doty T.J.
A1  - Porter C.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Objectives/Introduction: The bi-directional relationship between sleep and immune function is well-established. Sufficient sleep supports immune health and can increase vaccine efficacy. Conversely, sickness can disturb sleep quality, which can delay recovery and waking functioning. However, the bi-directional relationship between sleep and infectious diarrhea, the leading infectious disease threat to deployed military populations (and a common illness for non-military travelers), has not been studied. We assessed the bi-directional relationship between sleep and enteric disease utilizing data from a recently-completed controlled human infection model (CHIM) for enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC) strain B7A. Method(s): During a CHIM assessing the efficacy of an immunoprophylactic targeting ETEC (NCT03040687), we measured sleep via actigraphy over an 8-day inpatient period. Participants ingested prophylaxis 3 times/day during study days-2 and-1 and were challenged with ETEC on day 0. We hypothesized better sleep pre-challenge would reduce risk of disease following challenge (assessed using linear regression). We also hypothesized total sleep time (TST) and sleep efficiency (SE) following challenge would be lower/poorer than baseline (assessed using paired t-test). Result(s): Among 59 participants (aged 34.4 +/- 8.1 yrs, 64% male), longer TST the night preceding ETEC challenge was associated with lower total diarrhea volume throughout the course of sickness (B =-3.13, p = 0.001) and fewer overall sickness symptoms (B =-0.005, p = 0.03). SE was slightly poorer following challenge (78 vs. 76%; t (55) = 2.2, p = 0.03), but there was no significant change in TST, potentially due to low TST pre-challenge (316 vs. 329 minutes; p = 0.12). Conclusion(s): These results suggest acute sleep loss prior to an enteric immune challenge may lead to more severe symptoms during illness. These results-in aggregation with previous work on sleep and vaccines-suggest travelers (either military or civilian) should increase sleep prior to traveling to an area with a high risk for infectious enteric disease.
