T1  - Phenotyping cutaneous toxicities associated with immune checkpoint blockers used in advanced melanoma
T3  - 100th Annual Meeting of the British Association of Dermatologists, BAD 2020. Virtual.
A1  - Kim M.
A1  - Ye W.
A1  - Payne M.J.
A1  - Coupe N.
A1  - Fairfax B.P.
A1  - Matin R.N.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - A variety of immune-related adverse events (irAEs) have been reported with immune checkpoint blockers (ICBs), with cutaneous toxicities being one of the earliest to present and most prevalent. To date, only small cohorts (< 30 cases) recording cutaneous toxicity have been reported from U.K. centres. We undertook a retrospective case-note review to phenotype cutaneous toxicity in patients with advanced melanoma receiving ICBs with palliative intent at a U.K. tertiary centre between November 2015 and August 2019. Of the 297 patients receiving an ICB, 84 (28.3%) developed cutaneous toxicity. Mean age was 65.1 years (range 28-87); male to female ratio was 1.4:1. Median time to onset of skin reaction was 57 days for anti-cytotoxic T-lymphocyte-associated protein 4 (CTLA-4) (n = 14; mean 89; range 7-347), 42 days for anti-CTLA-4/antiprogrammed death 1 (PD-1) combination (n = 39, mean 1285; range 13-42 644) and 93 days for anti-PD-1 inhibitors (n = 31, mean 188; range 14-903). The most commonly used description for dermatological irAEs reported by oncologists was 'maculopapular rash' (n = 30/84), with pruritus being the most frequently reported symptom in 65% (n = 55). Other skin irAEs reported included vitiligo (n = 9), morbilliform/papular rash (n = 8), acneiform/folliculitis (n = 9), bullous pemphigoid (n = 4), eczematous (n = 3), urticated rash (n = 2), annular erythema (n = 3), granulomatous reaction (n = 2), dermatomyositis (n = 1), cutaneous infections (n = 7), petechial/vasculitis (n = 2), morphoea (n = 1) and lichen sclerosus (n = 2). Overall, 40/84 (48%) skin toxicity cases were reviewed by a dermatologist (mean time to review 2 months, range 0-16 months after the onset of toxicity); 12 cases (14%) were investigated with histopathology and only 14 (17%) cases had medical photography images recorded. The majority of cutaneous irAEs were low grade and five (6%) patients discontinued treatment due to skin toxicity alone. Mean length of follow-up in this cohort was 22 months (range 0.7-77). Mortality in the skin toxicity cohort was 27% (n = 23/84). To the best of our knowledge, we report the largest characterized cohort of skin toxicity in patients with advanced melanoma treated with ICBs at a single U.K. centre. Although the majority of cutaneous irAEs are mild, the clinical manifestation is highly variable and infrequently requires treatment discontinuation. We would advocate detailed documentation of skin toxicity using medical photography images and early referral to dermatology for formal assessment. A low threshold for histopathological investigation could lead to better characterization of skin irAE, reduce morbidity and facilitate more optimized management strategies. Furthermore, we would advocate working collaboratively across multiple centres in the U.K. to better understand predictors of skin toxicities and ultimately personalize management.
