T1  - Different source of PBSC improved the outcome of sibling and unrelated umbilical cord blood stem cell transplantation in TM::84 cases report
T3  - 25th Congress of the European Hematology Association Virtual Edition, EHA 2020. Frankfurt Germany.
A1  - Feng X.
A1  - He Y.
A1  - Xiao Y.
A1  - Liao J.
A1  - Tan W.
A1  - Wu X.
A1  - Peng Z.
A1  - Ruan Y.
A1  - Liu X.
A1  - Li C.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Background: At present, allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation is still the only way to cure thalassemia major (TM) patients. But in fact, less than half of the patients can find matched sibling or unrelated donors.Umbilical cord blood stem cells is a potential source of stem cells because of their weak antigenicity, low HLA matching requirements and low GVHD rate. However, the number of stem cells in umbilical cord blood is fewer,especially many TM children with iron overload before transplantation, which makes the transplantation of umbilical cord blood stem cells in thalassemia faces great challenges. Aim(s): In order to increase the dose of stem cells, peripheral blood stem cells from same newborns and haploid peripheral blood stem cells from relatives were co-transplanted in sibling cord blood transplantation( CBT) and unrelated CBT respectively in TM. This paper was to explore effectiveness of this new strategy and compare the prognosis of sibling and unrelated cord blood stem cell transplantation in TM. Method(s): From Jan. 2008 to Oct. 2019, the clinical data of 84 children with TM who were first underwent cord blood stem cell transplantation was analyzed retrospectively. As of Dec.31, 2019, the median follow-up time was 48months. The NF-08-TM protocol with CY + Bu + flu + TT in conditioning, was used for cord blood transplantation(CBT). Haploid peripheral blood stem cells combined unrelated cord blood transplantation was carried out with NF-14-TM protocol added CY in day +3, day+ 4 days ,followed infusion of unrelated cord blood in + 6 day. The average infusion of fresh cord blood mononuclear cells was 5.90 x 107 (0.77-15.78 x 107), of which CD34 + cells were 1.58x 105 (0.169-3.6 x 105). The number of haploid mononuclear cells was 27.33 x 108 (9.90-45.0 x 108), and the number of CD34 + cells was 19.43 x 106 (0.278-44.68x 106). Unrelated cord blood mononuclear cells were 5.03 x 107 (0.77-15.78 x 107), of which CD34 + cells were 1.52 x 105 (0.02-4.44 x 105). Before transplantation, serum ferritin, liver and heart MRI-T2* were used to evaluate the level of iron load. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS 20.0 statistical software. Recipients were divided into the high-ferritin group (>=4833.5ng / ml) and the low-ferritin group (< 4833.5ng / ml) using the ROC curve method with 100% sensitivity and 97.6% specificity, AUC = 0.982, P = 0.020. Result(s): Total of 45 cases of sibling CBT and 39 cases of unrelated CBT were enrolled. Two of the 84 thalassemia children died, with an OS of 96.5+/-2.5%, TFS 92.5+/-3.0%:TMR was 3.5%. The OS of the sibling CBT group and the unrelated CBT group were 97.8 +/- 2.2% and 91.2+/-4.9%, P = 0.673; Meanwhile TFS were 93.3 +/- 3.7% and 90.9+/-8.7%,P = 0.863. OS and TFS (100% and 95+/-2.5%) in the low-ferritin group were markedly higher than those in the high-ferritin group (50+/-25% and 37.5+/-28.6%,P = 0.00). Serum ferritin level of 4833.5ng / ml was the best predictive value for transplant related mortality. Summary/Conclusion: Our study showed that sibling and unrelated cord blood stem cell transplantation all had a good prognosis without significant differences with the strategy of co-transplantation of PBSC from different source in TM. Serum ferritin more than 4833.5ng / ml significantly predicted the mortality of CBT.
