T1  - High Flow Nasal Oxygenation and TIVA to aid intubation of an obese (BMI 55) patient
T3  - World Airway Management Meeting. Amsterdam Netherlands.
A1  - Mclenachan J.
A1  - Waite A.
A1  - Taylor L.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Obesity presents numerous anaesthetic challenges, including difficult facemask ventilation. This may result in desaturation and awareness, even in patients without clinical predictors of difficult airway. We describe successful use of high-flow nasal oxygenation (HFNO) in combination with bispectral index (BIS) monitored total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA), head-elevating laryngoscopy pillow (HELP) and videolaryngoscopy to provide good intubating conditions during a period of apnoeic ventilation in a patient with a body mass index (BMI) of 55. We report a case of a 50-year-old female patient attending for elective craniotomy and excision of frontal lobe metastasis arising from lung adenocarcinoma. Medical history included obesity1 (height 1.62m; weight 144kg) hypothyroidism, hypertension and epilepsy secondary to brain tumour. Airway examination showed Mallampati score 3, thyromental distance <6.5cm, inter-incisor distance >3cm, ability to sublux jaw, and reasonable neck extension. The patient had her original adult dentition. For induction of anaesthesia, the patient was positioned using a head-elevating laryngoscopy pillow (Oxford HELP, Alma Medical, UK). High-flow nasal oxygen (Optiflow, Fisher & Paykel Healthcare, NZ) was commenced at flow rate of 30L/min and bispectral index monitor (BIS, Covidien, UK) applied. TIVA was commenced using an empirical 80kg patient weight setting to guide pump infusion rates. Remifentanil 80mcg was infused over one minute, followed by background infusion of 8mcg/min. Propofol was given (Marsh model, plasma target 6.0ng/ml initially, reduced to 3.0 ng/ml) until loss of verbal response, BIS level below 50 and appropriate EEG waveform change. Rocuronium (80mg) was administered and one minute allowed to elapse prior to attempted intubation. Intubation with size 7.0 armoured tracheal tube( Unomedical, UK) was successful at first attempt using a McGrath videolaryngoscope (Covidien, UK) and bougie. Oxygen saturations dropped to minimum 92% during the 2-minute apnoeic period. A fibreoptic laryngoscope was prepared and available in case of difficulties, but was not required. The case we describe combines commonly-used techniques and widely-available equipment to reduce the risk of both awareness and desaturation. This is a particular concern when providing to anaesthesia due to the obese. The familiarity of many anaesthetists with high-flow nasal oxygenation and TIVA gives them an advantage when managing this challenging patient group. Knowing of this combination may provide a useful additional technique to consider when management the airway in this patient group. The patient has given written consent for submission of this case report. Caldicott approval has been applied for. References [1] World Health Organisation Website accessed 26.7.19.https://www.who.int/topics/obesity/en/Copyright © 2020
