T1  - The evolution of antibody response during immune tolerance induction in patients with severe hemophilia a predicts outcome
T3  - 63rd Annual Meeting of the Society of Thrombosis and Haemostasis Research. Berlin Germany.
A1  - Wieland I.
A1  - Sykora K.-W.
A1  - Stichel D.
A1  - Orlowski A.
A1  - Konigs C.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - The eradication of inhibitors in patients with severe hemophilia A during immune tolerance induction (ITI) is a great challenge. Treatment success is evaluated by inhibitor titers, FVIII recovery, and half-life. The change of IgG subtypes during ITI has not been evaluated in detail. Method(s): We report the course of two boys with severe hemophilia A who were monitored long-term for coagulation, pharmacokinetic and immunological parameters during ITI. We analyzed FVIII specific antibodies, isotypes, IgG subclasses (IgG 1-4), cytokine levels, inhibitor titers and FVIII affinity during ITI for of up to 3 years. Patient 1, a now 6 years old boy developed a high titer inhibitor of 82 BU. ITI was started with a recombinant rFVIII product according to a modified Bonn Protocol. The inhibitor (maximum >7000 BU) decreased slowly, but increased after each danger signal. After 9 months of ITI the factor VIII product was switched to rFVIIIFc. After 21 months of ITI the inhibitor titer became negative, with normal recovery but still shortened half-life of 3 hours. Patient 2, a now 4 years old boy developed high titer inhibitor (150 BU) after 9 exposure days of low dose prophylaxis. After starting ITI, the inhibitor increased to a maximum titer of 881 BU. After 10 months of ITI the inhibitor was 0.6 BU, increased after CVL infection to 7 BE and decreased very slowly without reaching < 2 BU. Recovery and half-life remained reduced. A change to rFVIIIFc resulted in only temporary improvement. Finding(s): Both boys showed FVIII specific antibodies mainly belonging to subclass IgG4 initially. In patient 1, IgG1 and IgG3 were also increased for a short time, disappeared after start of ITI and appeared again after danger signals. After changing the FVIII concentrate to rFVIIIFc, IgG4 levels dropped sharply, but still remained detectable after BUs were negative. IgG4 levels most closely reflected half-life and recovery. In patient 2, IgG1, IgG3 and IgG4 were detected at high levels during inhibitor development. After start of ITI, all three subclasses decreased with disappearance of the inhibitor, with IgG4 again never becoming completely negative. IgG1, 3 and 4 increased again after vaccination. In contrast to patient 1, IgG1 and 3 did not decrease further but reached a plateau and IgG4 increased slowly. Conclusion(s): The different clinical courses or our patients were reflected in the course of the FVIII specific IgG subclasses. Patient 1 had a predominance of IgG4 and was improved after change of concentrate to rFVIIIFc. In patient 2, with unsuccessful ITI, IgG1, 3 and 4 remained positive. This suggested that not a single subclass alone but a combination of subclasses may indicate unsuccessful ITI. In both cases, the course of IgG subclasses reflected the clinical outcome more precisely than Bethesda Units alone.
