T1  - Bacteriophage therapy in the future: Opportunities and challenges
T3  - 33rd Annual North American Cystic Fibrosis Conference. Nashville, TN United States.
A1  - Pride D.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Bacteriophage therapy has gained significant popularity due to a handful of recent successes in treating a variety of invasive bacterial illnesses. The question that we must ask ourselves is, "Is bacteriophage therapy ready to be used for the masses?" In answering this question, let us review what we know about bacteriophages, why we may need bacteriophage therapy, what lessons we have learned from its recent uses, and then review its potential for the future. Bacteriophages, also called phages, are viruses that target and kill bacteria. They do so by targeting specific bacteria hosts, multiplying within those host cells, and then killing those host bacteria to release high numbers of their progeny. The progeny can then target additional bacteria hosts, continuing the cycle until all the bacteria hosts are killed, or the bacteria develop resistance to their phages. Bacteriophages are present and target their bacteria hosts nearly everywhere on the planet, including in the human body. Phages that are capable of killing bacteria that cause human illness can be found in many environments, including bodies of water, soil, and sewage treatment facilities. Bacteriophages were discovered almost 100 years ago, and soon thereafter were used to treat bacterial infections with limited success. They were largely replaced as therapeutics when antibiotics were developed in the decades to follow. The benefits that antibiotics held over phages were that bacterial susceptibility to antibiotics was often predictable, the dosing regimens for successful use of antibiotics have been established, and their safety has been demonstrated over their many years of use. Unfortunately, due to their overuse in societies across the globe, resistance to them has risen substantially. Some bacteria have become resistant to most if not all available antibiotics. With the emergence of these bacteria has come a reassessment of the use of phages as therapeutics in humans, but there are other reasons phages have gained popularity as well. We all are inhabited by an abundance of different microbes, collectively referred to as the microbiome. There are about 38 trillion bacteria in your microbiome according to recent estimates. They live on most body surfaces, including the skin, mouth, guts, and bladder. It is important to preserve our microbiomes because they play important roles in preventing pathogens from establishing infections, in the development of our immune systems to recognize and kill pathogens, and in metabolic processes such as the digestion of foods. Indiscriminate uses of antibiotics can have profound and long-lasting effects upon our microbiomes by killing many of the bacteria that make up our microbiome; thus limiting their use may aid in keeping us healthy. For these reasons, phages have become more attractive therapeutics to kill pathogens because they may help preserve our microbiomes while killing pathogens. There have been a number of recent cases using phage therapy that have taught us quite a few lessons about its potential utility in combating human infections. We have learned that phage therapy in combination with antibiotics and our own immune responses can be effective under certain circumstances. These cases have taught us that bacteriophages can be delivered intravenously, and those phages likely will find their host bacteria in the body wherever they exist. We have established that careful preparation of phages can be delivered safely and reproducibly with minimal side effects. The pathogenic bacteria can develop resistance when phages are used as therapeutics, so multiple different phages may be necessary to achieve the desired response. We have learned from these cases that bacteria can also pay a cost for the development of resistance to phages, which may render them less successful pathogens. So, a handful of cases have significantly advanced our knowledge about the potential for phage therapy in humans. Phages have a lot of limitations compared to conventional antibiotics that have limited their use. These weaknesses will continue to limit their use until we can design appropriate studies to learn the same information about phages that has already been established for antibiotics for many years. For example, we do not yet know how much phage we need to deliver to achieve appropriate responses? The conventional wisdom has been to just use as much phage as possible. The problem is that not all phages can be grown to such high levels as have been used in most phage therapy cases; thus, addressing the basic question of how much phage to deliver is critical. Another important question is, "How often do we deliver the phages?" For antibiotics, we understand their pharmacokinetics (how phages are absorbed and metabolized) and their pharmacodynamics (how phages affect their bacteria hosts in the body), which informs us on how much to deliver, how often to deliver it, and how long it will remain in the tissues after it has been delivered. For phages, we have just begun to examine these principles. There is a lot of work to do to understand how to properly use phages in humans. We do not even know if different types of phages will have different pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic properties in humans. The host range of phages is also a concern. We can just run a standardized panel of antibiotics against a bacterium and know exactly which drugs will be effective. There is no standardization of phages in the same way. Right now, each institution has their own phages, and determines whether they kill pathogens using their own specific protocols. There is a critical need to develop phage therapy protocols that can be standardized across the many institutions that are using it. Lastly, there now are data that suggest that phage therapy may not be as benign for the microbiome as we once thought. We need more information on the effects of phages on the microbiome to understand their potential long-term health benefits compared to antibiotics. There are reasons to be optimistic and reasons to be pessimistic about the future of phage therapy in humans. One of the primary reasons for optimism is the undeniable need for alternative therapies to antibiotics. Whether the focus is on antibiotic resistance, the need to spare the human microbiome, or diseases where bacteria may not be eradicated by antibiotics, the need for such alternative therapies is ongoing. Those with cystic fibrosis, who become colonized and continue to have Pseudomonas (and other pathogens) throughout their disease is a prime example of the limitation of antibiotics in eradicating pathogens. Other conditions such as bronchiectasis, or individuals who have implantable devices have similar difficulties in eradicating pathogens and are in need of alternative therapies to antibiotics. While we are relatively early in revisiting this nearly 100 year-old therapy, the combination of clinical need and recent scientific advancements provides a strong impetus to perform needed clinical studies to advance phage therapy for use in a broad range of clinical situations.
