T1  - The journey with kidney function assessment: Old is no longer gold
T3  - 56th Annual Congress of the European Renal Association-European Dialysis and Transplant Association, ERA-EDTA 2019. Budapest Hungary.
A1  - Shendi A.M.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) has been considered one of the best physiologic measures of kidney function. Several GFR assessment methods have been developed and refined. METHOD(S): A web-based review of relevant bibliography: articles and textbooks reporting the development milestones of GFR assessment, was conducted. RESULT(S): The first attempts to measure kidney function dated since the early 20th century when the ratio between blood and urine urea was used. The term clearance was then introduced by Moller, McIntoch and Van Slyke in connection with the excretion of urea in their series of articles Studies of urea excretion between 1928-1930. Five years later, the first evidence for Inulin clearance as the most accurate measure of GFR, and for creatinine clearance as an approximate, yet overestimated, GFR measure in human, came in two landmark papers by James Shannon. The use of inulin for GFR measurement is laborious and thus a number of radio-labeled agents were successfully introduced as alternative exogenous filtration markers: 131Ilothalamate in 1965 by Sigman, 51Cr-EDTA in 1967 by Garnett, and 99Tc-DTPA by Kiopper in 1971. The contrast agent Iohexol was used as filtration marker by Krutzen in 1984. These agents have become the gold standard for GFR measurement. The emergence of urine-free estimates of GFR since the mid-70s was a clinically-sound simplification of GFR assessment. The first widely used prediction equation was developed by Cockroft and Gault in 1976 to predict creatinine clearance from serum creatinine in adults. In order to avoid the drawbacks of this equation, Levey and his colleagues published the Modification of Diet in Renal Disease study (MDRD) equation to estimate GFR in 1999, originally based on 6 variables: serum creatinine, demographic factors (age, sex, ethnicity), and serum urea nitrogen and albumin. A year later, simplified version of the equation based on 4 variables: age, sex, ethnicity, and serum creatinine was introduced, and was further re-expressed using standardized serum creatinine in 2006. A more accurate equation was developed by the chronic kidney disease epidemiology collaborators in 2009: the CKD-EPI creatinine equation (eGFRcr). All creatinine-based equations suffered its inherent limitations as a biomarker. Cystatin C was thus proposed as an alternative endogenous biomarker and two equations were developed in 2012 by the CKD-EPI collaborators: one using cystatin C alone (eGFRcys) and the other using cystatin C combined with creatinine (eGFRcr-cys). The focus have then broadened to include additional novel filtration markers to further refine GFR estimation. The CKD-EPI investigators, in 2016, proposed combined Beta-2-microglobulin and beta-trace-protein GFR estimation. Despite this equation had similar performance to both creatinine and cystatin C equations; it did not represent an improvement nor was as accurate as the combined creatinine-cystatin C equation. CONCLUSION(S): GFR assessment has developed over the years to be more accurate and convenient. Current guidelines recommend reporting eGFRcr by the use of CKDEPI 2009 equation as the initial test and reporting eGFRcys and eGFRcr-cys by use of the CKD-EPI 2012 equations as a confirmatory test. The emergence of GFR prediction equations which rely on alternative filtration markers represents a paradigm shift towards potential independence from creatinine.
