T1  - A retrospective review of the "step-by-step" approach in the management of young infants presenting to hospital with a history or presence of fever
T3  - RACP Congress 2019, Impacting Health Along the Life Course. Auckland New Zealand.
A1  - Martinson K.
A1  - Kochar A.
A1  - Smith N.
A1  - Kelly A.
A1  - Sutherland L.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Background: Management of febrile infants less than 3 months of age in the Emergency Department (ED) can be challenging. There is a large variation in testing and treatment of these infants. Several tools like the Rochester1 and Philadelphia2 criteria have been used to assist physicians to manage these infants. The adherence to these tools is low due to changing epidemiology of bacterial infections and the introduction of vaccinations, serum biomarkers such as C-reactive protein (CRP) and, more recently, Procalcitonin (PCT). The Step-by-Step algorithm which is a sequential approach using laboratory and clinical parameters has been recently developed and validated prospectively.3,4 Its primary objective was to identify a low risk group of infants who could be safely managed as outpatients without lumbar puncture and empirical antibiotic treatment. It uses PCT which recently became available as a rapid test in our institution. Aim(s): Retrospectively validate the Step-By-Step approach in infants aged less than 3 months presenting to the Women's and Children's Hospital ED with a history or presence of fever. Method(s): Ethics approval was obtained from the Women's and Children's Hospital Ethics Committee. The study period was 18/10/2017 - 30/06/2018. Inclusion criteria included infants age less than 3 months, presentation to ED with a history of documented fever greater than or equal to 38 degrees at home/GP/Ambulance service or a fever recorded in the department. Exclusion criteria were inter-hospital transfer patients or those where one or more of the ancillary tests were absent (blood culture, urine dipstick or culture, PCT, CRP or White Cell Count (WCC)). The Step-By-Step algorithm was retrospectively applied by study researchers to identify 'low-risk' and 'high risk' infants through case note review. Primary outcome measure was prevalence of Serious Bacterial Infection (SBI) defined as urinary tract infection (UTI), pneumonia or bacterial gastroenteritis, and Invasive Bacterial Infection (IBI), defined as culture of bacteria from a sterile site such as blood or cerebrospinal fluid. Result(s): Eighty three patient encounters fulfilled the inclusion criteria. By applying the Step-By-Step algorithm, thirty five infants were identified as 'low risk' and forty eight infants were considered 'high risk'. The overall prevalence for IBI and SBI was 6% and 8.4% respectively. The vast majority of these infections represented UTI and related complication such as bacteraemic UTI. There were no cases of IBI or SBI in the patients considered 'low risk' by following the Step-By-Step algorithm. Therefore algorithm Sensitivity was 100% for SBI (95% CI 66.37-100.00), IBI (95% CI 47.82-100.00) and SBI/IBI combined (95% CI 73.54-100). Similarly, Prevalence Adjusted Negative Predictive Value was 100% for SBI, IBI and SBI/IBI combined. Conclusion(s): The Step-By-Step algorithm is promising as a rule out test for both IBI and SBI when retrospectively applied to our cohort of infants less than 3 months of age presenting with fever. This study was limited by low patient numbers, however provides support for a multi-centre prospective validation study in Australia.
