T1  - Thyroid Surgery Outcomes in the Obese Patient
T3  - 101st Annual Meeting, Scientific Sessions and Exhibition. Boston Convention Center, Boston United States.
A1  - Bunnell A.
A1  - Turner B.
A1  - Collin J.
A1  - Yekikian M.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Obesity continues to increase in prevalence among the adult population in the US. With obesity comes other comorbidities including cardiovascular disease, coronary artery disease, cerebrovascular disease, and obstructive sleep apnea. The increased body mass index and body habitus may present specific challenges to the surgeon during thyroid surgery itself. These challenges include difficulty palpating anterior neck landmarks, shortened neck, excessive submental and upper thoracic tissue obstructing the surgical field, and thickness of structures overlying the thyroid. While obesity has been associated with increased serum levels of thyroid stimulating hormones, the literature is inconsistent regarding the correlation between obesity and incidence of thyroid cancer.1 Despite these inconsistencies, there remains an increased number of obese patients undergoing thyroid surgery each year. The increased frequency of obese patients undergoing thyroidectomy warrants further evaluation to identify any difficulties or potential complications that may be specific to the obese patient. While there are studies that evaluate outcomes of thyroid surgery in the obese population, very few discuss thyroid specific outcomes of thyroid surgery in the obese patient.2 The aim of this study is to determine if patient demographics and comorbidities have an effect on surgical outcomes, including thyroid specific complications in obese patients undergoing thyroid surgery. A retrospective cohort analysis was completed to evaluate outcomes for patients who underwent thyroid surgery at the University of Florida College of Medicine Jacksonville Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery from January 2012 to December 2018. Patients were stratified based on their body mass index into obese (body mass index [BMI] >= 30 kg/m2) and non-obese cohorts (body mass index [BMI] <= 30 kg/m2). Thyroid surgery performed included thyroid lobectomy, total thyroidectomy, and substernal thyroidectomy (through both cervical approach or sternotomy). This review included the following data variables: patient demographics, presenting signs and symptoms, indication for procedure, comorbidities, ASA classification, total operating room time, procedure time, length of stay, final pathology, and postoperative complication rate. Thyroid specific postoperative complications that were evaluated included: hypocalcemia, recurrent laryngeal nerve injury, hematoma, infection, and surgical site breakdown. Comparisons among cohorts were performed using the Pearson's Chi-Squared Test, Fisher's Exact Test, and Wilcoxon Rank Sum Tests. A total of 402 patients underwent thyroid surgery during the study period, of which 6 were excluded due to insufficient data. Of the 396 patients, 218 patients had a BMI >= 30 kg/m2, while 178 had a BMI <= 30 kg/m2. The majority of subjects were classified as ASA II (95, 43.6%) and ASA III (115, 52.8%). Hypertension (108, 27.2%), and diabetes (75, 18.9%) were the most common comorbidities present for both groups. While compressive symptoms were more common at time of initial presentation for the obese group, the difference was not statistically significantly different. Average total procedure time was greater in the obese cohort (154.37 minutes) compared with non-obese cohort (139.47 minutes) (P =.001). There was also a significant difference in total operating room time between obese (202.31 minutes) and not obese (182.8 minutes). Rates of hypocalcemia (P =.830), recurrent laryngeal nerve injury/hoarseness (P =.384), or hematoma (P =.695), infection (P =.999) were not significantly different individually or combined overall (P =.984) among the 2 groups. While there is an increased procedure and overall operating room time, these data show that thyroidectomy can be safely and predictably performed in the obese patient without an increase in unfavorable surgical outcomes.Copyright © 2019
