T1  - Predicting the cost-effectiveness of cardiovascular interventions across multiple settings: A model-based approach to guide global health policy
T3  - American Heart Association Scientific Sessions, AHA 2019. Philadelphia, PA United States.
A1  - Shakil S.S.
A1  - Rosettie K.L.
A1  - Roth G.A.
A1  - Stafford L.
A1  - Weaver M.R.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Background: Cost-effectiveness analyses (CEA) are a useful tool for policymakers in prioritizing health interventions, but can be challenging to interpret beyond the country in which they are performed. Here we report coronary heart disease (CHD) results for a model that predicts incremental cost-effectiveness ratios (ICERs) for multiple diseases in all regions of the world. Method(s): Using the Tufts registries of published CEA we identified 179 CHD-targeted ICERs from 93 CHD-related CEA. We paired ICERs with the relevant disease, study location and age groups from the 2017 Global Burden of Disease Study, and the most likely delivery platform (health center, first-level hospital, referral/specialty hospital). For each platform, we predicted ICERs in 195 countries for each intervention-disease-platform combination using a Bayesian meta-regression model. Fixed and random effects were included for each intervention, with fixed effects for gross domestic product (GDP) per capita and disease-age-location-specific disability-adjusted life-years (DALYs) averted by the intervention. The table shows country-level predictions, with uncertainty, aggregated to World Bank income groups. All ICERs are relative to placebo or no intervention. Result(s): ICERs for CHD interventions were estimated as significantly lower in lower-income settings. Aspirin, clopidogrel and coronary artery bypass were the most cost-effective interventions for CHD in their respective platforms. Conclusion(s): Results suggest that the relative cost-effectiveness of CHD interventions may persist across diverse settings, and that CEA performed mostly in high-income countries can help inform policy decisions globally. Inexpensive antiplatelet agents and long-lasting surgical revascularization may be "best buys" for countries investing in health services to address an epidemic of cardiovascular disease.
