T1  - PIN82 CHOICE OF INFLUENZA IMMUNIZATION PROVIDER AND IMPACT ON IMMUNIZATION COVERAGE IN ALBERTA, CANADA
T3  - ISPOR Europe 2019. Copenhagen Denmark.
A1  - Kirwin E.
A1  - Rafferty E.
A1  - MacDonald S.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Objectives: Influenza immunization and treatment have a significant impact on health care budgets. Jurisdictions are challenged in identifying the optimal combination of immunization providers (public health, pharmacists, and physicians) to achieve immunization coverage targets and maximize program cost effectiveness. This study assessed influences on choice of immunization provider and impact on immunization coverage under various policy scenarios. Method(s): Using administrative data for the 2016 mid-year Alberta population, we determined immunization status and associated provider type for the 2015-16 and 2016-17 influenza seasons. Age of individuals and any children determined family classification, while postal code determined distance to each provider type. Multinomial logistic regression was used to estimate immunization provider choice (none, physician, pharmacist, public health) from prior year immunization provider, sex, age, family type, and distance to nearest immunization provider of each type. Parameter estimates were used to predict age-group coverage under different scenarios, such as reduced distance to specified immunization providers. Result(s): 4,191,535 individuals were included. Women were more likely to immunize regardless of provider: public health (aOR 1.54, CI 1.53-1.55), physicians' offices (aOR 1.30, CI 1.27-1.32), and at pharmacies (aOR1.19, CI 1.18-1.20). Relative to those without children, individuals aged 20-49 with children below the age of five were most likely to immunize at public health (aOR 1.88, CI 1.85-1.92), and physicians (aOR 1.21, CI 1.15-1.27) and less likely to be immunized at a pharmacy (aOR 0.68, CI 0.66-0.70). Individuals were most likely to return to the same provider type as the prior year, and if switching, most likely to switch to a pharmacy provider. Counterfactual scenarios with a 50% reduction in distance to pharmacy providers increased immunization coverage by 2.5%. Conclusion(s): This study provides estimates for factors impacting influenza immunization provider choice. The study also presents a novel method for predicting immunization coverage under varying policy scenarios.Copyright © 2019
