N2  - Introduction: Neonatal and Pediatric Heart and Renal Outcomes Network (NEPHRON) is a multicenter collaborative created to better understand cardiac surgery induced acute kidney injury (CS-AKI). NEPHRON'S first aim was to describe the multicenter epidemiology & outcomes associated with neonatal CS-AKI, utilizing modified Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) criteria. Hypothesis: CS-AKI is frequent and associated with morbidity and mortality. Method(s): 22-center study of neonates (<30 days) undergoing cardiac surgery utilizing a CS-AKI module combined with the Pediatric Cardiac Critical Care Consortium data set. Consecutive retrospective subjects were screened for enrollment starting 8/2017. Exclusions: preoperative AKI, ECMO, and reoperation. Primary Outcome: hospital mortality. Multinomial regression determined CS-AKI predictors. Associations among CS-AKI stages and mortality and other outcomes were assessed with multivariable logistic or linear regression. Result(s): 2240 neonates were included [Table]. CS-AKI occurred in 53.9%. Center CS-AKI rates varied >3 fold: 27% to 86%; site-specific proportions of KDIGO stages also varied. KDIGO Stage 3 was most commonly defined by oligo-anuria, 65% vs 35% by creatinine criteria. In multivariate analysis, only use of cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB), but not CPB duration, increased the odds of CS-AKI [OR 1.5; 95% CI 1.0-2.3]. Only KDIGO Stage 3 was associated with mortality: [OR 2.4; 95% CI 1.3-4.6]. No stage of CS-AKI impacted duration of mechanical ventilation or hospital length of stay. Conclusion(s): When defined by KDIGO, CS-AKI occurs in >50% of neonatal cardiac surgeries; rates and severity vary widely by center. CS-AKI did not importantly impact most outcomes; only KDIGO stage 3 is independently associated with mortality. Further NEPHRON analyses will explore center variation, CS-AKI risk factors, understanding impact of oligo-anuria, and optimizing the definition of neonatal CS-AKI.
