T1  - Predicting infection in trauma patients
T3  - 41st Annual Conference on Shock. Scottsdale, AZ United States.
A1  - Eguia E.
A1  - Cobb A.
A1  - Afshar M.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Background: Sepsis is a major challenge in hospitals, where it is one of the leading cause of death. In patients with trauma, nearly a quarter develop sepsis during their hospital admission. Therefore, a prediction tool to identify trauma patients at high risk for sepsis can augment decision making for early antibiotics to curtail the high mortality associated with sepsis. We aim to derive a prediction model for the development of acquired infection post-trauma. Method(s): All patients with trauma admitted between 2007 and 2016 at a Level 1 Trauma Center were examined. Inclusion criteria were adult trauma patients who met the Sepsis-3 criteria for suspected infection in the electronic health record (combination of antibiotics and body fluid cultures). Cases of infection were confirmed during chart review by a physician and ranked on a likert scale from not likely to definite. Patients were excluded if they were less than 18 years of age and not admitted for a primary trauma. The outcome was the development of in-hospital infection. Candidate variables for evaluation included the following: immunosuppression, diabetes, cirrhosis, neurovascular disease, age, gender, race abbreviated injury scores (AIS) for all body regions, injury severity score (ISS), and mechanism of injury. Variable selection techniques for prediction of in-hospital infection was performed using the following two methods: (1) best 2-, 3-, 4-, and 5-variable subsets and (2) stepwise selection in logistic regression. The most parsimonious model was chosen by considering discrimination with the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) area under the curve and model fit with the Bayesian information criterion. We analyzed the data using STATA software. Result(s): There was a total of 961 patients that met inclusion criteria, and a random selection of 309 patients were analyzed. The mean age was 46.1 (SD 21) and 24.9% (N = 77) black race. Patients who were identified as having a proven or probable infection accounted for 17.8% (N = 55) of the cohort and comprised the group with in-hospital infection. The 3-variable model with age, severe injury (ISS>15), and serious abdominal injury (AIS > 2) was derived using the best subsets and stepwise logistic regression, and it was among the best models with an ROC area under the curve and BIC of 0.72 (95% CI 0.66 - 0.77) and 283.8. The greatest odds ratio for developing infection was from severe injury (OR 6.2, 95% CI: 2.0-18.7) followed by age (OR 1.03, 95% CI: 1.02-1.05). Serious abdominal injury was noted to have a decreased odds ratio at 0.28 (95% CI: 0.12 - 0.63). A 5-variable model using best subsets had similar discrimination and fit as the 3-variable model but introduced added complexity with the additional variables serious lower extremity injury and burn mechanism. Conclusion(s): A 3-variable model with age, ISS, and AIS abdomen had good discrimination for identifying patients at-risk for developing an infection during hospitalization. Validation of the model is needed prior to clinical application.
