T1  - Successful interdisciplinary treatment of disseminated adenovirus in presumed immunocompetent patient, presenting as severe hypogammaglobulinemia and lymphopenia
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2018. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Ramaniuk A.
A1  - McNeil D.L.
A1  - Kynyk J.A.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Adenovirus is associated with a wide range of clinical syndromes with potential lethal outcomes, even in immunocompetent patients. Limited data is available on treatment, severity assessment, immune system evaluation, and prognostication of disseminated adenovirus in the presumed nonimmunodeficient population. This report presents a previously healthy male with rapidly worsening respiratory status secondary to adenovirus viremia. Patient was treated successfully with cidofovir/probenecid and intravenous immunoglobulins. The goal of this case is to highlight the need for: 1) increased level of suspicion for underlying immunosuppression when suspected causal organism does not correlate with severity of illness, 2) increased awareness and treatment options for disseminated adenovirus, 3) utility of quantitative immunoglobulins and subclasses in decision for initiation of immunotherapy. A 22 year old male with history of exercise-induced asthma and sinusitis, presented with fevers, malaise, vomiting and diarrhea. On hospital days 1-3, patient was stable on room air and febrile. Infectious disease and hematology were consulted for pancytopenia. On days 4-5, oxygen requirement increased- broad spectrum antibiotics, stress dose steroids, and intensive care were initiated. On hospital days 5-6, patient experienced worsening sepsis with ARDS on high flow oxygen, Tmax 104.8, encephalopathy. On day 6: respiratory PCR was positive for adenovirus, leading to immunoglobulin evaluation, immunodeficiency profile and an Immunology consultation. Treatment was started with Cidofovir 5mg/kg once weekly, concomitant probenecid and IVIG. Lab values were indicative of panhypogammaglobuliemia and T cell lymphopenia, suggesting a combined immunodeficiency. He was discharged on hospital day ten, after a rapid resolution of fevers, improvement of pancytopenia, decrease in the viral load >1log - predictive of a successful outcome. Outpatient follow up with immunology confirmed IgA deficiency. Upon literature review, limited studies provide clear insight into successful treatment of severe adenovirus viremia in non-immunocompromised adults. Mortality of disseminated adenovirus has been shown to be up to 83% in immunocompromised and 60% in immunocompetent patients. Quantitative adenovirus PCR as >1x10(6) copies/mL and severe lymphopenia ( <300 cells/mm3) are associated with progression to fatal disease. Preemptive treatment with cidofovir is indicated when the adenoviral load exceeds a critical level. Immunoglobulin evaluation, immunodeficiency panel, and immunology consultation is indicated if primary immunodeficiency is suspected. The use of intravenous immunoglobulin has also been met with success for a small number of patients. This case affirms that timely treatment and interdisciplinary collaboration between Infectious Disease, Pulmonary/CC, and Immunology specialists are instrumental in successful identification and treatment of disseminated adenovirus and immunodeficiency evaluation.
