T1  - A meta-analysis to evaluate the relationship between serum antibody neutralizing titer and protection against SHIV challenge in nonhuman primates
T3  - HIV Research for Prevention Conference, HIVR4P 2018. Madrid Spain.
A1  - Pegu A.
A1  - Huang Y.
A1  - Pauthner M.
A1  - Corey L.
A1  - Gilbert P.
A1  - Burton D.
A1  - Mascola J.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Background: To study serum neutralizing antibody titer of passively infused neutralizing HIV monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) as a correlate of risk/protection against SHIV infection, we conducted a metaanalysis combining information from 17 mAb nonhuman primate (NHP) single high dose SHIV challenge studies: 12 published between 2010-2017 and 5 unpublished. Method(s): Individual-level data were available from 265 animals and include 16 different mAb targeting five envelope epitope regions (CD4 binding site, V2-apex, V3-glycan, high mannose glycan, MPER) and seven SHIV challenge viruses with intrarectal, intravaginal, or oral inoculation. Serum 50% neutralization titer (ID50) against the challenge virus was predicted based on individual animal's mAb concentration at the day of challenge using the measured inhibitory concentration (IC50) of the mAb against the challenge virus. We used logistic regression to study the association of serum ID50 with infection outcome, and to assess possible effect modification by any of several factors. Result(s): There was no evidence for effect modification by mAb epitope category, virus tier (1 or 2), challenge dose, or sex, and borderline evidence for modification by challenge virus (p=0.056). Infection risk is inversely correlated with a linear effect of log10ID50 (p< 0.001), after adjusting for mAb epitope region and challenge virus, with 95.7% reduction in the odds of infection per 10-fold increase in ID50. Averaged across the combinations of epitope targets and viruses studied, the geometric mean of ID50 to achieve 50%, 75%, and 95% protection is 39, 87, and 194 respectively. Conclusion(s): Serum neutralizing ID50 titer strongly correlated with protection from SHIV challenge in NHPs; this correlation was seen consistently across experimental factors including for mAbs targeting different epitopes. The results suggest particular attention should be paid to measurement of serum neutralizing titers against relevant viruses in mAb clinical efficacy trials, most notably in the AMP trial.
