N2  - Background: We aimed to quantify rates and identify predictors of serious adverse events (SAE) with vedolizumab (VDZ) for Crohn's disease (CD) and ulcerative colitis (UC). Method(s): This was a review of a multicenter, US-based consortium of CD and UC patients treated with VDZ. Serious infections (SI) were defined as those requiring antibiotics or hospitalization, or resulting in discontinuation of VDZ or death. SAE were defined as SI or adverse events resulting in discontinuation of VDZ or death. Multivariable logistic regression analyses with odds ratio (OR) and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) are reported for independent predictors of SI and SAE. Result(s): 1,087 patients (n=650 CD; 45% male) with a median follow-up of 302 days (interquartile range 171-434) were included. SI occurred in 66 patients (6%); the most common SI were C. difficile (n=24), abscess (n=11), respiratory tract infections (n=10), zoster (n=7), and bacteremia (n=6). SAE occurred in 73 patients (7%), with no reported malignancies. Transverse myelitis (n=1) and optic neuritis (n=1) were the only 2 reported central nervous system adverse events. SI and SAE were observed in 3.7% and 4.3% of patients on VDZ monotherapy, 6.1% and 6.7% of patients on VDZ in combination with steroids or an immunomodulator (azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine, methotrexate), and 9.8% and 10.7% of patients on VDZ in combination with both steroids and an immunomodulator. On multivariable analyses, after adjusting for inflammatory bowel disease type (CD vs UC), age, and sex, concomitant immunosuppressive (steroid or immunomodulator) therapy was the only independent predictor of SI (OR 1.67 per number of immunosuppressive agents used, 95% CI 1.17-2.38) and SAE (OR 1.65 per number of immunosuppressive agents used, 95% CI 1.17-2.31). Discussion(s): VDZ is well tolerated in clinical practice, with SI and SAE being reported in 6% and 7% of patients, respectively. The strongest predictor of SI or SAE is the use of concomitant steroids or immunomodulators, with the greatest risk seen in patients on triple therapy with VDZ, steroids, and immunomodulator therapy. Further studies are needed to identify patients who will most benefit from concomitant immunosuppressive therapy use with VDZ to optimize the safety profile in clinical practice.Copyright © 2018 AGA Institute. All rights reserved.
