Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Objectives: Electronic medical record (EMR) datasets are increasingly being used to identify novel predictors of clinical outcomes, for example neutrophil and lymphocyte count have been shown to be associated with survival. However it is unclear if this association is causal or reflects bias or confounding. In this study we sought to explore associations between these lab tests and a variety of clinical and demographic characteristics. Method(s): Data were obtained from two EMRs: the United Kingdom Clinical Practice Research Datalink (CPRD) and the United States Optum database (Optum) for the time period of 2000 to 2017 (CPRD) and 2002 to 2017 (Optum). Individuals were 18 years or older at the time of the lab test with a minimum of 12 months of baseline data before the lab test to assess clinical characteristics. Lab test values were classified into high, normal and low according to standard cut-offs. Potential predictors of lab test results were tested with multivariable regression models. Result(s): We used data from 28,004,609 tests for neutrophils and 27,883,230 tests for lymphocytes combined across Optum and CPRD. Significant predictors of abnormal test results included patient age, gender, number of prior tests, geographical region, day and month of test, patient insurance type, smoking status, comorbidities (including chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, renal disorders, liver disease, autoimmune disorders, cancer, endocrine disorders, cardiovascular disease, infections) and treatments (including analgesics, anti-hyperlipidemics, anti-depressants, anti-hypertensives). Conclusion(s): We show that a lab test result can be associated with a number of variables, but these were not considered when investigating the association of these tests with survival. In the era of big data, researchers should consider the multitude of factors that may be associated with test results when conducting analyses focused on such variables, and adjust for these as necessary to provide true causal inference.Copyright © 2018
