T1  - Right heart failure before predicts right heart failure after LVAD implantation
T3  - European Society of Cardiology Congress, ESC 2018. Munich Germany.
A1  - Wagner T.
A1  - Schrage B.
A1  - Bernhardt A.
A1  - Reichenspurner H.
A1  - Blankenberg S.
A1  - Grahn H.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Purpose: Right heart failure (RHF) after continuous flow left ventricular assist device (cfLVAD) implantation has high impact on mortality and quality of life. Many parameters were challenged to predict RHF but so far, definition of RHF was not universal. Therefore, data are inconsistent, and accuracy of prediction remains low. This study assessed prevalence and determinants of RHF focusing on clinical manifestation according to INTERMACS definition [1]. Method(s): We performed a retrospective analysis of 112 patients undergoing LVAD (HVAD, Medtronic) implantation in our hospital. A broad set of parameters from echocardiography and right heart catheterization were included into analysis. All patients were screened for pre-operative RHF, the onset of early RHF (ERHF, on day 1, 7, 14) and late RHF (LRHF, after 1, 3, 6 and 12 months). Result(s): RHF was frequent on day 1, 7 and 14 (80%, 47% and 33%). Rates were lowering in long-term follow up, but combined outcome measure of persistent RHF and death from RHF (11% of patients after 1 year) showed a high prevalence after 3, 6 and 12 months (23%, 24% and 17%). Two thirds of patients had signs of RHF in the immediate pre-operative phase. Interestingly, LRHF results in half of the cases from a persistence of ERHF, but in the other half a new onset of LRHF was observed. Patients with ERHF had higher preoperative central venous pressures (p=0.04), a lower right ventricular Load Adaption Index (LAI, p=0.048), higher creatinine, urea and bilirubin (p<0.05 each), more frequent an infection (p=0.01) and an INTERMACS score <=3 (p<0.001). Patients with persistent LHF showed these associations to a high extent, but none of these determinants were found in cases of new onset of LRHF. Pre-existing RHF was only predictive for ERHF (OR=5.0, p=0.01) and persistent LRHF (OR=4.0, p=0.04) but not for new onset of LRHF (OR=0.7, p=0.52). Conclusion(s): RHF after cfLVAD implantation is highly prevalent and associated with worse outcome. Pre-existing RHF as a clinical manifestation enhances the risk of ERHF and persistent LRHF but is not associated with the new onset of LRHF. New onset of LRHF seems to be a separate entity which is poorly understood and should be addressed by a more careful follow up by echocardiography and periodic right heart catheterization. Furthermore, the clinical manifestation of RHF should be addressed by aggressive treatment before implantation.
