T1  - Non-communicable chronic diseases risk prediction based on deep recurrent neural network and attention mechanism
T3  - 2018 IERI International Conference on Medical Physics, Medical Engineering and Informatics, ICMMI 2018. Macau China.
A1  - Yang T.
A1  - Deng H.L.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Objectives: Due to the complexity, long incubation period, and no clear time for illness, non-communicable chronic diseases (NCD) usually have extremely high mortality and disability. Therefore, it is important to predict the onset risk of NCD in the early period, thereby prompting the patients to undergo early treatment. In recent years, benefiting from the large amount of medical data in electronic medical records, data-driven NCD risk prediction has achieved numerous achievements. However, due to the high-dimensionality, temporality, and sparsity of medical data, the prediction of NCD faces a lot of challenges. To overcome those challenges, this manuscript studies the use of deep recurrent neural networks and attention mechanism to construct a new risk prediction model of NCD. Method(s): We proposed an end-to-end model to predict the onset risk of NCD in patients from the clinical data of patients saved in electronic medical records. Firstly, our model learning the low-dimensional dense embedding of temporal clinical events based on temporal matrix representation, overcoming the high-dimensional and sparse representation in traditional methods. Then a deep recurrent neural network with two-layer long short-term memory (DLSTM) is adapted to capture the sequential representation of patient electronic medical records recurrently. And the attention mechanism is used as the temporal fusion strategy to generate the final feature representation of entirety of clinical events. The DLSTM combined with attention mechanism (DLSTM-AT) completes the encoding of the variable-length temporal clinical data of patients. Finally, the encoded representations are input into a softmax layer to evaluate the probability that the patient will face the risk of certain NCD. Result(s): We validated the effectiveness of our proposed model through performing the experiments on two specific diseases in the real world: Congestive heart failure (CHF) and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Data were from the electronic medical records of 324 260 patients in one of the top three hospitals over 5 years, which includes 1254 cases and 3985 controls, 565 cases and 2556 controls for cardiovascular and COPD patient cohort, respectively. For the two diseases, we both set the observation window to 12-month. In the experiments, we compared our model with the classical logistic regression (LR) and SVM methods, as well as the convolutional neural network (CNN) based model. The results are measured by the area under the ROC curve (AUC) over 10-fold cross-validation. The experimental results show that our model increases the prediction accuracy by 1.5% for CHF and 2% for COPD, compared to the best of the baseline models. Conclusion(s): Deep recurrent neural network combined with attention mechanism improve the performance of models for predicting the onset risk of NCD.
