T1  - The outcome of cardiac transplantation in patients with down syndrome
T3  - 38th Annual Meeting and Scientific Sessions of the International Society for Heart And Lung Transplantation, ISHLT 2018. Nice France.
A1  - Khoshbin E.
A1  - Reinhardt Z.
A1  - Kirk R.
A1  - Parry G.
A1  - Schueler S.
A1  - Hasan A.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Purpose: Rapid advances in cardiac surgery and a change in attitude towards patients with cognitive disability has meant that patients with Down Syndrome (DS) now live longer. In the majority of cases corrective cardiac surgery is possible but occasionally cardiac transplantation is the only solution. Method(s): This is a single institution case series of patients with end stage heart failure and DS who received heart transplantation. We categorized patients according to INTERMACS score and report the efficacy of treatment in terms of lives saved and complication rates. We reviewed the indication to transplantation, peri-operative challenges, transplant related complications, survival as the primary outcome and quality of life. Result(s): The patients were 3, 11, 20 and 30 years old at the time of heart transplant. Chemotherapy related cardiomyopathy was the aetiology in three patients. The remaining patient had idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy. All patients were gravely sick at the time of transplant three had an INTERMACS score of 1 and one scored 2. Two patients were bridged to transplant one using a combination of Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation and Bi-ventricular Assist Device and the other using an implantable Left Ventricular Assist Device (VAD). Two patients required re-sternotomy and explantation of the heart with the bridging device. One patient was found to have a persistent left Superior Vena Cava (SVC) and had to have reconstructive procedure. An extra anatomical reinforced Polytetrafluoroethylene graft was used to drain the left SVC to the right atrium. All four patients are alive after 173, 137, 15 and 8 months. Against strong hypothetical predictions we have observed no propensity for development of post-transplant infection or lymphoproliferative disorders. All patients and relatives reported good quality of life and return to full activity within months of their transplants. Conclusion(s): Patients with DS may be considered for heart transplantation. They can be successfully bridged using mechanical circulatory support. The outcome of transplantation has been satisfactory with no reported complications to date. These patients should be carefully assessed with respect to their level of cognition and particularly the presence of strong social support.
