T1  - ECMO outcome prediction scores for estimates of likelihood of survival in pediatric cardiac surgery patients
T3  - 6th Euro-ELSO Annual Congress. Maastricht Netherlands.
A1  - Badruddin S.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - World-wide, the combination of low weight, prematurity and congenital heart disease in the newborn is a challenge to the multidisciplinary team. Too early ECMO initiation may lead to uncontrolled use of ECMO, that increase hospital costs while exposing patients to unnecessary complications, however late ECMO initiation can be futile. ELSO registry showed only 23 percent of survival to hospital discharge with an indication for ECMO due to heart failure in pediatric patient. Whereas, in congenital heart surgery, ECMO has achieved 40 to 60 percent survival when commenced in a timely manner. Use of ecmo should be analyzed according to its risks. In one study, 303 infants with ECMO having heart failure showed out of 98, 46% of patients who died had stroke, pulmonary, disseminated intravascular coagulation, acute kidney injury, surgical site bleeding, and infections. Longer use of ECMO was associated with increase mortality, ventilator weaning time, ICU length of stay and hospital costs. Method(s): A systematic search was done in December 2016, to access research studies on outcome prediction scores for estimating the likelihood of survival in pediatric cardiac surgery patients with ECMO. Key words included: ECMO, prediction survival, outcome. Data was explored from CINAHL, PubMed, Sage and Science Direct to guide the search. The search ranged from 2006 to 2016. Only twenty articles relevant to the study purpose. The aim of the study was to identify and develop ECMO outcome prediction scores for estimating survival in pediatric cardiac surgery patients' course for ECMO initiation. Result(s): Recently several models have been created to assist clinicians/ECMO teams in their decision making process for ECMO. Different studies has used different scores to assess the degree of acute lung injury (Murray scores), risk assessment of pediatric mortality (PRISM score), and the degree of organ dysfunction in ICU (SOFA score), acute kidney injury (RIFLE score). ELSO registry has utilized the following scores PIPER, NEO-RESCUERS, PED-RESCUERS, P-PREP, RESP, SAVE to estimate the prediction of survival in patients with ECMO in different settings. The Aristotle score is a method to assess quality of life and ECMO outcome based on complexity. Save score is first reported in hospital survival predictionmodel for ecmo use in adult cardiogenic shock. Conclusion(s): The literature does not provide any specific/tested ECMO outcome prediction scores for estimates of likelihood of survival in pediatric cardiac patients. It is also suggested that serial blood lactate level may be an accurate rate predictor of death.
