N2  - Background: In patients with end-stage heart failure requiring heart transplantati end-stage disease of a second organ may require consideration of simultaneous du organ transplantation. Combined heart kidney transplantation (CHKT) has beco accepted as a viable therapeutic plan in this challenging clinical scenario and its pr alence has increased dramatically (147%) from 2000-2010, compared to only mod increase (3.6%) in isolated heart transplantation (OHT). However, little data has be published on the outcomes of CHKT. Method(s): Retrospective analysis was p formed on all patients between 2005-2015 treated with CHKT or OHT in a large volu academic center. We reviewed demographic as well as pre, peri and post-operative o comes. Result(s): A total of 20 patients treated with CHKT (75.0% male, mean a 53.25 +/- 9.5) and 250 patients treated with OHT (69.9% male, mean age 52.36 +/- 11 were included in this study. All 20 patients underwent successful CHKT, with dem graphic and pre-operative characteristics similar to previously published literature. Kapl Meier analysis demonstrated excellent 1 and 5-year survival in OHT compared w CHKT (90.2% vs. 85% at 1 year, P=.455; 67.1% vs. 55.0% at 5 years, P=.272) with statistically significant difference. Interestingly, rates of rejection were significantly creased amongst those received CHKT (58.5% vs. 30.0%, P =.01). Fungal infectio were almost five times more likely within CHKT (15.0% vs. 3.3%, P =.01), while mulative infection rates were decreased in CHKT, approaching significance betwe groups (30.0% vs. 51.6%, P =.06). Notably, new-onset malignancy was less comm among CHKT, without reaching statistical significance (10.0% vs. 27.6%, P=.08 Conclusion(s): This retrospective review is a large single-center series to evaluate co bined heart-kidney transplantation outcomes. While demonstrating equivalent survi at both 1 year and 5 years, future studies are needed to evaluate whether CHKT m confer improved morbidity in relation to infection, rejection and new onset maligna cy. Concomitant transplantation may provide immune-mediated projection from su highly morbid complications.
