N2  - Patients: We retrospectively studied 581 patients with typical SCID who received a hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT) at 32 PIDTC centers from 1982-2012. Known mutations (n =355) included 178 gC, 35 JAK3, 40 IL7R, 6 CD3, 1 CD45, 27 DCLRE1C, 28 RAG1/2 and 40 ADA. There were 160 B + NK-SCID (B cells > 400/mm3), 79 B + NK + SCID (NK cells > 100/mm3), 110 B-NK + SCID and 77 B-NK-SCID patients. Conditioning regimen (CR) groupings were none or immunosuppression only (No/IS) versus reduced intensity or myeloablative CR (bu-sulfan <12 or 12 mg/kg, respectively) (RIC/MAC). Table 1 shows donor type and CR distribution. Result(s): Overall survival (OS) was 64.6% at 20 years and was better for MSD versus all other donor types (Table 1). For non-MSD recipients, OS was better for gC/JAK3 versus ADA (P =.0198), or DCLRE1C (P =.0016) defects (Figure 1). As in our 2000-2009 cohort, non-MSD HCT recipients age <3.5 mo at HCT survived better than those >3.5 mo with active infection (P <.001); in this larger cohort, infection had a negative impact on survival regardless of phenotype. Graft failure (defined as need for 2nd HCT) was 16.4% and was higher for MMRD or URD versus MSD or MORD (P =.01). T cell recon-stitution (PHA proliferation, CD3, CD4, and CD8 counts) improved then reached a plateau at 1 year, while B cell function (off IVIG and normal IgA levels) plateaued at ~2 years. CD4 and CD4+CD45RA+ counts at 1 year were predictive of OS (Figure 2, landmark analysis, P<.001) and of long-term T cell reconstitution assessed at 2-5 y and at 6-10 y either by CD3 count >1000 or by a composite measure based on the achievement of at least 2 of 3 criteria: PHA > 50%, CD3 > 1000, or CD4 > 500 (Figure 3, predictive value of CD4 counts at 1 year on composite measure, Area under ROC curves =.894 and.794). In non-MSD HCT recipients, RIC/MAC was associated with superior T cell numbers (CD3, CD4 and CD45RA counts) and more robust B cell function through 11 years post-HCT. T cell reconstitution (PHA proliferation; CD3, CD4, CD8 and CD4+45RA+ counts) was faster in gC/JAK3 and slower in RAG patients while B cell function was faster and better in the long term for IL7/CD3/CD45 and ADA patients. RIC/MAC was associated with being off IVIG in B + NK-SCID (P <.001 from 1 y-11 y post-HCT) whereas no difference was observed in B + NK + SCID. Conclusion(s): Analysis of this large retrospective series of typical SCID patients suggests that HCT approach should be tailored to genotype/phenotype as well as infectious status for optimal survival and immune outcome. CD4 and CD4+CD45RA+ levels at 1 y had high predictive value for survival and long-term immune reconstitution and may identify at risk patients in need of further therapy.
