T1  - Screening criteria for community acquired sepsis prior to evidence of clinical suspicion of infection in the electronic health record
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2017. Washington, DC United States.
A1  - Seymour C.W.
A1  - Liu V.
A1  - Wulf D.A.
A1  - Angus D.C.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Background: Recent sepsis definitions focus on patients in whom infection is already suspected. How much time elapses before clinicians suspect, and start managing, infection is not well understood, complicating the evaluation of sepsis screening tools. In two independent cohorts, we examined the timing of community-acquired infection from emergency department (ED) arrival until digital evidence that the clinical team suspected infection and compared the performance sepsis screening tools during this time period. Method(s): We studied electronic health record encounters from 2010 to 2012 at 12 hospitals in southwestern Pennsylvania (Cohort-1) and identified adult encounters with and without Sepsis-3 criteria in the first 48h. Data were compared with 21 hospitals in northern California from 2009 to 2013 (Cohort-2) where a sepsis quality improvement program concluded in 2010. We measured the time to the digital signature of suspected infection (DSI) (first of either a body fluid culture or non-prophylactic antibiotic in the electronic health record). We measured systemic inflammatory response syndrome criteria (SIRS), quick sepsis-related organ failure assessment (qSOFA), and serum lactate in the pre-DSI time period and in no-DSI encounters over matching time at risk. We compared screening criteria by increment of area under the receiver operating characteristic curve to predict community sepsis over a baseline model using demographics and co-morbidity. Result(s): Cohort-1 and -2 included 534,015 and 755,592 encounters of whom 43,934 (8%) and 132,663 (18%) had community-acquired sepsis. The median time to clinically suspected infection was 5.8h [IQR: 2.3-18.0h] and 1.0h [IQR: 0.0-3.0h] (Figure 1). The majority of sepsis encounters (55% and 90%) had the DSI within 6h of arrival. Among 21,546 community sepsis encounters with DSI after 6h, 10,221 (47%) had >=2 SIRS, 5,200 (24%) had >=2 qSOFA, and 1,436 (7%) had lactate >=2mmol/l prior to the DSI. The best-performing screen (AUROC=0.73, 95%CI:0.71-0.74) combined all unique SIRS or qSOFA elements and serum lactate, and was superior to SIRS alone (AUROC=0.69, 95%CI:0.68-0.70, p<0.01), qSOFA alone (AUROC=0.70, 95%CI:0.69-0.71, p<0.01), or the baseline model alone (AUROC=0.63, 95%CI:0.62-0.64, p<0.01), although all performed modestly. Conclusion(s): Most community-acquired sepsis arises in patients recognized as infected within 6h of ED arrival. Quality improvement in this group should focus on recognition of organ dysfunction, such as with qSOFA or SOFA. For the remainder, where more prompt recognition of infection is still required, combination screens may help, but even best-fitting models that use traditional measures are not optimal. (Figure presented).
