T1  - Single vs multidrug prophylaxis for gustilo-anderson type II and IIIA open fractures
T3  - 2017 Annual Meeting of the Orthopaedic Research Society. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Araiza E.
A1  - Carlson B.
A1  - Johnson S.
A1  - Tilley M.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: Open fractures present unique challenges to soft tissue healing, bony union, and infection prevention. Gustilo-Anderson Type II/IIIA (See Table 1) open fractures are historically treated with multidrug antibiotic prophylaxis. In an effort to standardize open fracture care at KUMC, antibiotic prophylaxis guidelines were implemented in 2010. A multi-disciplinary panel examined open fracture infections data and bacterial antibiotic sensitivities in the Midwest region. This panel recommended single drug therapy for all Gustilo-Anderson injuries. Type I injuries receive cefazolin, Type II ampicillin/sulbactam, and Type III piperacillin/tazobactam. Additionally, 24 hour duration of antibiotic prophylaxis was established, which is in accordance with the EAST Guidelines. Our hypothesis is that there is no difference in the rate of deep infection between monotherapy and multidrug antibiotic prophylaxis. METHOD(S): A retrospective review of all open fractures treated at this Level I academic trauma center from 2010-2014 was performed to identify Type II/IIIA open fractures treated with antibiotic therapy within 24 hours of injury. The primary study outcome was deep infection. Patients included sustained open fractures since implementation of the 2010 guidelines with a minimum of 90 days of follow-up. Exclusion criteria were: patients transferred to the Level-I trauma center more than twenty-four hours after the time of injury or those that received primary orthopedic treatment elsewhere, patients receiving treatment for an acute or chronic infection of the injured limb, patients with previous malunion or nonunion of the injured limb, patients who did not receive antibiotic therapy for their open fracture, patients with follow-up of less than 90 days post-injury, and patients with residence outside of the catchment area of the trauma center. Differences in continuous variables were assessed with Student's independent t-test. Differences in categorical variables were assessed with Fisher's exact test. A binary logistic regression was performed with infection as the dependent variable and age, gender, duration of antibiotics, soft tissue coverage, tobacco use, diabetes, and external fixator use as independent predictor variables. Statistical significance was set at alpha=0.05. RESULTS SECTION: 286 patients with 340 fractures (Type II:170; Type IIIA:170) were studied. Mean age was 41 years (11- 96). Overall deep infection rate was 9.7% (II: 9.4%; IIIA 10%, P>0.05) at a mean of 63 days (2- 941) after injury. Using Fisher's exact test, there was a significant difference between frequency of infection between monotherapy (n=206, rate=6.5%) and multi-drug therapy (n=124, rate=15.3%), P=0.012. There were significant differences between infection groups for duration of antibiotic therapy (P=0.032) and tobacco use (P=0.025). There was no difference between groups for age (Student's t-test, P>0.05) or frequency of diabetes, external fixator use, or drug abuse between infection groups (Fisher's exact test, P>0.05). In binary logistic regression with infection as the dependent variable, tobacco use was the only significant independent predictor variable in the model, P=0.046. Duration of antibiotics (>24hrs vs. <=24hrs), gender, soft tissue coverage, diabetes and external fixator use were not significant in the model, P>0.05. DISCUSSION(S): As open fracture treatment algorithms developed, combination antibiotic therapy for infection prophylaxis became standard of care. Injury classification based on the Gustilo-Anderson open fracture classification system dictates antibiotic selection. Traditionally, cephalosporin antibiotic therapy provides antibiotic coverage for Type I and Type II injuries, while more severe Type III open fractures require aminoglycoside treatment, in addition to cephalosporin. Penicillin would be added if the injury occurred in an agricultural environment. Following our study, we find that antibiotic monotherapy prophylaxis in patients with Type II/IIIA open fractures prevents deep infection at rates similar to previously reported studies using multi-drug antibiotic therapy. Duration of antibiotic use >24hrs was not a significant predictor of infection. Tobacco users had significantly higher infection rates. (Figure Presented).
