A1  - Perng W.-C.
A1  - Wu C.-L.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Purpose: The aim of this study is to construct an easy scoring system for predicting outcomes in patients with complicated influenza admitted to ICU. Material(s) and Method(s): This is a retrospective observational study conducted by Taiwan Severe Influenza Research Consortium (TSIRC) which includes seven tertiary referral medical centers. Patients with virology-proven influenza infection admitted to intensive care unit between January and March 2016 were included for analysis. 177 patients with complete data for scoring were enrolled. Result(s): There were higher APACH II score and pneumonia severity index score in non-survival (n=45) group (APACH II mean(SD): 25.98(10.75) vs. 21.27(8.66), p=0.004; PSI: 141.16(50.06) vs. 110.69(40.31), p<0.01). Sepsis organ failure assessment score SOFA) were also higher in non-survival on day 1 (13.13(3.96) vs. 9.50(3.73), p<0.01), day 3, and day 7. Besides, patients in non-survival group had higher incidence of liver disease (26.7% vs. 11.4%, p=0.014) (Precipitation factor variable), influenza viral culture positive rate (51.6% vs. 31.1%, p=0.003), influenza PCR positive rate (93.3% vs. 84.8%, p=0.144) (Inflammation variable), and need for ECMO (20% vs. 8.3%, p=0.033) and hemodialysis (35.6% vs. 8.3%, p<0.001) due to this complicated influenza (host Response variable). One point was given for each feature present. Combined with first day SOFA score (Organ variable), redesigned for points (0 to 4), we constructed a complicated influenza PIRO score (range from 0 to 9 points) for stratifying mortality risk. The score can stratify hospital mortality risk (AUC: 0.78) (score 0-4 group: 20.6% Vs. score 5-9 group: 56%, p<0.001) and 28-d survival (Log Rank <0.001). Conclusion(s): Complicated Influenza PIRO score is an easy way to stratify mortality risk in patients with virology proven influenza admitted to ICU.
