T1  - Towards personalised real-time predictions of health trajectories and survival using the UK CF registry
T3  - 31st Annual North American Cystic Fibrosis Conference. Indianapolis, IN United States.
A1  - Keogh R.H.
A1  - Szczesniak R.D.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Objectives: For people with CF, there exists limited information on prognosis which takes into account their individual characteristics. The aim was to develop dynamic models which enable real-time prediction of survival based on an individual's current and historical clinical measures and other relevant factors. A further aim was to develop extensions to enable prediction of the occurrence of intermediate events of reaching a low lung function and receiving a transplant. Methods and Outcomes: Large observational patient databases, which provide longitudinal data on clinical measurements, present opportunities to develop "personalised" dynamic predictions of survival and occurrence of other events. Dynamic prediction models were developed using UK CF Registry data from 2006-2015. Longitudinal data from annual review visits were used in conjunction with data on mortality and transplants. The landmarking approach, combined with mixed modelling, was used to relate longitudinal clinical measures to survival, enabling real-time estimates of survivor curves conditional on survival to a current age. Models were also developed for estimating the likelihood of an individual taking a particular health trajectory in the future, based on their current and historical clinical data. Elements included in the health trajectories were reaching a low level of lung function and receiving a transplant. Health trajectories were studied using multi-state modelling. The dynamic prediction models were fitted using data on 11,215 individuals in the UK CF Registry. There were 1273 deaths and 331 transplants. The models used as predictors are baseline factors, including sex and genotype, and longitudinal measures, which comprise the health trajectories, including lung function, status of respiratory infections, diabetes, and BMI. The predictive performance of the models was assessed by executing the model development in training set and then applying the selected models to a validation set. Results show that the models have good prediction properties in terms of discrimination and calibration. We will illustrate the potential for using real-time dynamic prediction models in practice by presenting predicted health trajectories for example Registry participants. Conclusion(s): This work extends earlier survival models in CF by making full use of the longitudinal nature of the UK CF Registry data, therefore enabling dynamic predictions. Furthermore, we have incorporated predictions relating to health trajectories in addition to survival. The challenge remains as to how results on real-time personalised prognosis are most effectively communicated to clinicians and people with CF.
