T1  - The role of ascending ventral tegmental area projections in impulsivity and attention
T3  - ECNP Workshop for Junior Scientists in Europe 2016. Nice France.
A1  - Flores Dourojeanni J.
A1  - Adan R.
A1  - Vanderschuren L.
Y1  - 2016//
N2  - Impulsive behaviour can be defined as acting prematurely in response to an internal or external stimulus, with insufficient planning or recognition of the potential adverse consequences. It is a multifaceted phenomenon associated with drug addiction, pathological gambling, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder and mania [1]. To properly asses the best treatment options for these aforementioned psychosomatic and behavioural disorders; it is necessary to both gain a more lucid grasp on the neural circuitry governing impulsive behaviour, as well as widen our current scope of the circuit. Dopamine (DA) reuptake inhibitors are used to treat the impulsivity in these disorders and it is thought that these drugs act upon frontostriatal projections, including structures such as the ventral striatum and the medial prefrontal cortex [2]. Nonetheless, it remains elusive how DA activity precisely modulates impulse control within these areas. Therefore, we investigated the role of DA neuronal activity within frontostriatal circuits in impulsivity in the 5-choice serial reaction time task (5-CSRTT) using optogenetics in combination with viral vector technology. Wild-type Long Evans (WT) and TH-Cre rats were trained in the 5-CSRTT and an adenosine-associated viral vector (AVV) carrying a floxed channelrhodopsin [AAV5-EF1a- DIO-hChR2(H134R)-EYFP] was injected bilaterally into the ventral tegmental area (VTA). WT rats were further injected with a retrograde Cav2-Cre virus in either the nucleus accumbens (NAcc) or the infralimbic cortex (IL), to test the role of VTA-NAcc and VTA-IL projections in impulsivity. Chronic optic fibres where then bilaterally inserted (TH-Cre and WT) in the VTA as well as the NAcc or the IL. Optogenetic stimulation of the targeted pathways was then carried out during the 5-CSRTT to assess its influence on impulsivity as well as attention. Stimulation (20 Hz, average light intensity 12mW) took place either during the first 3 s or the last 3 s of the intertrial interval (ITI). DA-specific stimulation of the VTA and stimulation of the VTA-IL projection decreased accuracy whilst increasing the number of omissions, without affecting impulsivity (P>0.05). In contrast, DAspecific stimulation of the VTA-NAcc efferents increased accuracy, but only when stimulation took place close to stimulus presentation (i.e., the last 3 s of ITI; P>0.05). Non-DA specific stimulation of this same pathway in the WT rats, however, only resulted in an increase in accuracy when the animals were attentionally challenged with a short stimulus duration (P>0.05). Furthermore, VTA-NAcc projection stimulation increased premature responses when impulsive responding was provoked using a long ITI (P>0.05). These data are consistent with previous findings on the role of DA in impulsivity [3]. Moreover, they show that increased activity in VTA-Nacc efferents is sufficient to increase impulsivity. Last, these data demonstrate that time-locked activation of VTA-IL efferents reduces, whereas stimulation of VTA-NAcc DA efferents enhances, attention. Together, these data highlight the functional heterogeneity of ascending VTA projections in the modulation of cognition.
