T1  - Mews score as a tool for identifying patients with severe sepsis and adverse outcomes
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2015. Denver, CO United States.
A1  - Lockhart G.
A1  - Mehta P.
A1  - Exline M.C.
A1  - Kynyk J.
A1  - Ali N.A.
Y1  - 2015//
N2  - Introduction/Objective: Sepsis continues to be a major cause of morbidity in hospitalized patients, with mortality rates as high as 50% in those with septic shock. Guidelines are not clearly defined as to what specific set of physiologic markers warrant a sepsis screening. Our study aims to investigate the relationship between the Modified Early Warning Score (MEWS), the prevalence of sepsis, and correlation with adverse events . Method(s): We conducted a retrospective point prevalence study, gathering data via electronic chart review from patients hospitalized at a large academic medical center on a single day. Inclusion criteria was in-patient status on the index study day. Exclusion criteria were DNRCC status, age < 18, admission for Labor and Delivery Care or current occupancy in an ICU. A MEWS was generated for each patient based on routine variables. Data for SIRS criteria and suspicion for infection were manually abstracted. The same initial patient population was then followed through the next seven days to evaluate for subsequent adverse events. [rapid response team (RRT) activation, mechanical ventilation, and hospital mortality] Results: 596 Patients met enrollment criteria. Elevated MEWS scores were infrequent in this cohort. (MEWS of 1 or less, 72.5%; 2, 18.6%; 3, 6.7%; 4, 2.2%) As expected, the prevalence of SIRS positive status was directly related to MEWS score (12.3%, 27.0%, 52.5%, 76.9%). In addition Sepsis (5.6%, 18.0%, 35.0%, 53.8%), Severe sepsis (4.4%, 15.3%, 25.0%, 53.8%) and Septic shock (0.6%, 0%, 5.0% and 15.4%) prevalence were all highly correlated with MEWS score category (p<0.01, for each) A total of 15/596 (2.5%) cases went on to have adverse outcome over the course of 7 days from original time point. Stratified by MEWS, there were 7/432 (1.6%) cases with adverse outcome for MEWS 1, 4/111 (3.6%) cases for MEWS 2, 2/40 (5.0%) cases for MEWS 3, and 2/13 (15.4%) cases for MEWS 4. Both cases in the MEWS 4 group consisted of respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation. Hazard ratio of combined adverse outcome at MEWS 4 was 6.27 (95% CI 1.29 - 30.4). Discussion(s): These results provide a justification for utilizing a MEWS score to identify groups in need of screening for severe sepsis interventions and of predicting those patients at risk for future adverse outcome.
