T1  - A pilot project with multiple IAEA studies exploring a Big Clinical Data strategy for research and to improve quality
T3  - 2014 Annual Conference of the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology, ESTRO 33. Vienna Austria.
A1  - Jones G.
A1  - Menon T.
A1  - Rosenblatt E.
A1  - Zubizarreta E.
A1  - Fidarova E.
A1  - Pynda Y.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Purpose/Objective: Clinical policies are based typically on higher quality studies focused on narrow questions with limited data. A vague concept of 'big data'has emerged for internet informatics and marketing studies, and it has produced a few famous epidemiological studies (e.g. predicting epidemics). With the advent of health care informatics in large departments and larger hospital organizations, with regionalising connectivity, we now envision bigger clinical data (BCD) resources with electronic access for multiple cross-cutting analyses. What challenges are there of a BCD environment, and what can one learn from the data in it? Pilot projects are needed to empirically explore such questions as there is no clear theory or set of principles for guidance. We report the first such pilot project. Material(s) and Method(s): Our immediate context is multi-centre, low-tomiddle income country (LMIC), international studies including trials in oncology and radiation medicine. For three dozen studies we use a novel application of PDF forms with custom-built e-form manager to capture, assemble and report data. Some studies include images and objects, and results of their further processing. A very novel extension is to use NoSQL to allow combining of data from research studies into one data-set. Result(s): Characteristics of BCD differ from other, related strategies: registries append more patients with few variables; widening data-sets increase variables per case with greater complexity; while BCD add more cases and more variables and much greater complexity. Aggregating data from research studies produces challenges and opportunities. Challenges include a geometric increase in complexity of data and meta-data interrelationships. These may be managed inside the BCD environment, or can be reduced through adapting incoming studies (in their initial design) to prepare for BCD integration. Security of aggregated patient-specific data and ethical use of such data is an issue. Potential opportunities are assessing for associations between data, images, objects, audio and meta-data regarding epidemiological questions (e.g. health determinants),service questions (e.g. waiting times, indicators of followup patterns) and clinical questions (e.g. chemo-radiation toxicities by socio-ethnic characteristics across trials) across many countries and continents. Further, a'common' model or index for determining investigator and study performance could emerge within such a BCD environment. Conclusion(s): This is the first attempt using a novel PDF-e-form-manager- NoSQL solution to aggregate higher quality study data into a BCD-like structure for initial exploration. It provides an early window into challenges and opportunities of this emerging area, with the goals of proving, improving and transforming the quality of care using BCD strategies.
