T1  - Characteristics of Efavirenz drug induced liver injury-a cohort analysis
T3  - 65th Annual Meeting of the American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases: The Liver Meeting 2014. Boston, MA United States.
A1  - Sonderup M.W.
A1  - Wainwright H.
A1  - Setshedi M.
A1  - Spearman C.W.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Drug induced liver injury (DILI) complicates antiretroviral therapy (ART). Efavirenz (EFV), a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, is used in combination in the management of HIV. More than 2 million South Africans are now on ART and DILI poses a clinical challenge. Minimal data exists on the characteristics of EFV DILI in a high HIV/AIDS burden setting. Method(s):After ART initiation, patients with suspected DILI who had a liver biopsy and had complete clinical/demographic data, were selected. EFV was assessed as the cause of the DILI after a clinico-pathological assessment consisting of a temporal relationship between exposure and the injury, excluding acute viral hepatitis, alcohol, herbal toxins and biliary obstruction and observing the effects of de-challenge. Histological patterns suggestive of a DILI were jointly assessed by an experienced liver histopathologist and hepatologists. Result(s):50 patients,31 female and 19 male, were evaluated, median age 34 [IQR 30-40] years did not differ between genders. 84% were Black African, 14% Mixed Ancestry and 2% White. Median CD4 count at time of ART initiation was 210 [IQR 71-415]; females having a significantly higher CD4 than males, 291 [8-1434]; 157 [3-955], p=0.017, respectively. The median duration of ART was 16 [IQR 8-20] weeks. 3 histological patterns of injury were identified - submassive necrosis (SMN) 32% (16), nonspecific hepatitis (NSH) 32% (16) and mixed cholestasis-hepatitis (MCH) 36% (18). The table notes the liver enzymes in the 3 histological sub-types. Prolonged INR, was more likely in the SMN group vs. the other 2 groups, 1.68 [IQR 1.32- 2.6]; 1.16 [IQR 0.98-1.28], p<0.0001. With multivariate analysis, SMN was significantly associated with a CD4>200, odds ratio (95%CI) [11.6 (2.1-37.2), p=0.007] and younger age [0.89 (0.81-0.99), p=0.04]; whilst the MCH group was associated with a lower CD4 count (<200), [5.2(1.3-20.2), p=0.01]. No associations were noted in the NSH group. The median duration of hospitalization was 21 [IQR 7-33] days. During follow up, 3 (6%) patients died. Corticosteroids were used in all patients with SMN with no mortality in this group. Conclusion(s):EFV related DILI occurs more frequently in woman whilst CD4 count predicted for the histological pattern of injury. Morbidity is marked with prolonged hospital stay necessitated. Corticosteroids were used with severe liver injury with no in hospital mortality. (Table presented) .
