T1  - Standardized data collection (SDC) for rectal cancer: Towards personalized medicine
T3  - 2014 Annual Conference of the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology, ESTRO 33. Vienna Austria.
A1  - Meldolesi E.
A1  - Van Soest J.
A1  - Damiani A.
A1  - Dekker A.
A1  - Gambacorta M.A.
A1  - Valentini V.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Purpose/Objective: The necessity of tailored treatment has become more important in the last decades as the oncology profession moves toward individualized medicine. Aggregating the enormous quantity of variables (clinical, imaging, treatments e.g.) that describe human and tumor characteristics routinely collected and innovative 'rapidlearning'research techniques allow extraction of knowledge of the masses for the benefit of the individual. The strategy to collect data in a standard and consistent manner and to analyze them properly for decision support system is named 'umbrella protocol', that includes an ontology of the variables that need to be collected. Our aim is to build an ontology for rectal cancer that formally represents a sort of dictionary where all information are specified and organized in a welldefined data collection model. Material(s) and Method(s): The base of any ontology is to describe the domain. More than 200 features related with rectal cancer patients, have been selected. They were organized according three different tiers, with increasing density of data, to obtain a well defined data collection model. The first and most general level, the Registry level, includes all the minimal information (age, gender, ethnicity e.g.) used for epidemiological analysis only. The second level, the Procedures one, includes treatment information and related toxicities and the evaluation of outcome in term of disease free survival and acute and late toxicities. The last tier, the Research level, includes clinical and imaging information used for in-depth, advanced research projects only. The formal ontology describes each of these concepts in a consistent manner and with a unique reference, preferably mapped to a published coding system (e.g. NCI Thesaurus, CTCAE, SNOMED) representing the 'semantic' step of the umbrella protocol. Result(s): An 'umbrella protocol' strategy has been used to standardize data and procedure to create a consistent dataset useful to obtain a trustful analysis for decision support system. An ontology, a well-defined data collection model, able to collect, standardize and organize features related with rectal cancer patients, has been created. The three datastoring levels have been used to classify all the information to easily address the query depending on the different analysis requested (epidemiological, toxicity, outcomes e.g.). Conclusion(s): In the era of individualized medicine it can be expected that more personalized treatments will not only save patients from unnecessary toxicity, but will also facilitate the choice of the most appropriate treatment. Structural collaboration between different groups is crucial to generate enough large databases realized by sharing and combining multiple dataset. The 'umbrella protocol' strategy and the building of a formal ontology lead to obtain a standardized and wellorganized dataset useful to create predictive models which complement existing consensus or guidelines moving from prescription by consensus to prescription by numbers.
