T1  - Impact of infection control guidelines on infection rates
T3  - 28th Annual North American Cystic Fibrosis Conference. Atlanta, GA United States.
A1  - Savant A.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Infections can be detrimental to cystic fibrosis (CF) patients' health and quality of life. It is known that certain respiratory infections lead to declines in health for patients with CF. In the early 1980s the "cepacia syndrome," caused by patient-to-patient transmission of Burkholderia cepacia, led to death in 62-100% of patients (1-3). P. aeruginosa infection can lead to worse lung function, lower chest x-ray (CXR) scores, poor growth, increased need for antibiotics, greater likelihood of hospitalization and increased mortality (4,5). Methicillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) has been found to be associated with increased courses of IV antibiotics, worse baseline CXRs, decline in lung function and decreased survival (4,6-8). CF patients with either MRSA or P. aeruginosa were found to have a higher risk of death (6,9). Mycobacterium abscessus subsp massiliense, possibly spread from patient to patient, led to the death of 3 of 5 patients (10). In the last 30 years, knowledge of new detrimental infections has been discovered. Unfortunately for our patients, we cannot predict which infection may be the next epidemic. Infection control measures can decrease or halt the spread of epidemic infections. Strict segregation of patients led to decreased transmission of B. cepacia, specifically in summer camps; those not attending camp had no incidence of B. cepacia, while those who attended had a 6.1% incidence of sputum conversion (11). MRSA incidence and prevalence was maintained at 3% at one center through infection control, consisting of segregation and barrier nursing coupled with an eradication policy (12). P. aeruginosa epidemics, such as the Liverpool Epidemic Strain (LES) were reduced through a strict isolation control policy, specifically, home intravenous antibiotics and discouraging social mixing, leading to infected patients declining from 55 to only 9 patients (13,14). Australian Epidemic Strain-1 (AES-1) of P. aeruginosa was shown to decrease from 21% to 6% after implementation of cohort segregation and reinforcement of standard infection control measures (15). The number of patients infected with the Houston-1 endemic strain of P. aeruginosa declined from 56% to 36% after the following infection control measures were instituted: contact precautions for all inpatients until final culture results, immediate placement in clinic rooms and greater accessibility to gloves and gowns for healthcare workers, among other processes (16). The M. abscessus subsp massiliense outbreak was halted through an infection control regimen involving airborne isolation and clinic and equipment surfaces being cleaned twice between patients (10). Another center found M. abscessus in 47% of patients, however, through use of strict adherence to infection control procedures, including gowning and gloving for all inpatient and outpatient encounters and dedicated pulmonary function and bronchoscopy equipment, there has only been one new acquisition of M. abscessus (17). It is unclear if there is one specific infection control intervention that led to the decline in infection rates in these examples, since all the centers implemented a combination of measures. Waiting for an epidemic infection is too late to make an intervention. Applying infection control guidelines will improve patient safety by minimizing the risk of patient-to-patient transmission of infections. In 2007, the Ann & Robert H. Lurie Children's Hospital of Chicago did not have a sentinel event or an epidemic infection. Our desire was to create improved patient safety through reduced risk of patient-to-patient transmission of infections as a quality improvement (QI) effort. We chose to "assume" that all patients had a potentially detrimental infection that could be spread to other patients, since standard assessment of respiratory tract pathogens takes up to 7 days to become final and even when final, may not adequately reflect all pathogens in the patient's airways. We implemented contact precautions for all patients in all locations by all providers in order to improve patient safety through reduced risk of patient-to-patient transmission of infections (18). The most recent infection control and prevention guidelines from this year now recommend doing this for all patients. Our team spent over 9 months preparing to implement changes. There were numerous structural components needed, such as adequate supply of gowns and gloves and lidded trashcans to contain the protective gear. We developed new process flows for many areas of the clinic, from check-in to the pulmonary function lab to the clinic area and beyond. Finally, education to the healthcare workers and the patients and families had to be developed and provided. Only after all these aspects were in place could we begin to implement our new infection control process. In order to track the effect of implementation of contact precautions for all, we evaluated our infection rates for numerous bacteria, including P. aeruginosa and MRSA. Tracking consisted of surveillance of the number of patients with specific bacteria on culture. We recognized there were multiple confounding factors that could have led to a decreased number of infections; however, we did have significant declines. Our P. aeruginosa rate decreased from a mean of 30% of patients to 22% and our MRSA rate decreased from 11% to 9%. We have continued to monitor our staff for adherence to our processes, a critical component to ensure the safety of our patients. Our center is just one example of infection control QI efforts changing the outcome. The center at Houston also implemented a QI process after the Houston-1 P. aeruginosa epidemic led to decline in new acquisition of this pathogen (16). Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center also implemented improved infection control as part of QI that likely contributed to improved outcomes in BMI and FEV1 (19). Many other centers have similarly improved the safety of the care delivered to their patients with CF and helped improve the outcomes for all.
