N2  - Background and aim: Triple therapy for HCV-1 patients has boosted sustained virological response (SVR) as 63-75% but eligibility for treatment should be weighted up against both safety and cost of the new regimens. Conventional dual therapy with Peg-interferon (Peg-IFN) and ribavirin remains efficacious for about 40-50% who have never been treated before. Accordingly, a current challenge to identify those patients who may obtain SVR with standard therapy, we have developed a model to estimate the patient's a priori probability to experience favorable or unfavorable outcomes after administration of Peg-IFN/RBV. Material(s) and Method(s): Significant predictors of RVR and SVR were identified in 1259 naive HCV-1 infected patients by multiple logistic analysis of baseline and on-treatment features. Pertinent predictive models (Model A for RVR, and Model B for SVR, respectively) were generated based on respective regression coefficients of the independent predictors. Model A and B were then combined to generate a comprehensive model to predict SVR at presentation, whose accuracy was compared with the registered SVR rate after completion of therapy. Result(s): Age, IL28B CC type, fibrosis <=3, normal gGT, and low viremia were identified as independent predictors of both RVR and SVR. In addition, RVR was found to be a strong predictor of SVR. Model A and B showed ROC values of 0.75 and 0.82. The a priori probability of SVR was estimated as follows = (Probability of SVR if RVR) x (Probability of RVR) + (Probability of SVR without RVR) x (Probability of no RVR). At presentation, 426 patients were predicted to have a <25% SVR probability: in 358 (84%) failure to attain SVR was correctly classified. Conversely, at week 4, 188 patients were predicted to have an SVR >80%: in 167 (88.2%%) SVR was correctly classified. Conclusion(s): The proposed model allows a precise individual estimate of the probability to attain SVR when conventional dual therapy will be administered to patients with HCV-1 infection at presentation, providing an objective tool to facilitate physicians and patients in decision making on whether adopt triple therapies, or defer treatment for pending options.
