T1  - Multivariate analysis of the risk factors predicting the outcome of dorsal buccal mucosa graft urethroplasty
T3  - 66th Annual Scientific Meeting of the Urological Society of Australia and New Zealand. Melbourne, VIC Australia.
A1  - Mulawkar P.
A1  - Mulawkar U.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Introduction and Objectives : Inflammatory conditions of urethra, iatrogenic due to urethral instrumentation or lichen sclerosis - balanitis xerotica obliterans (BXO) are common causes of the long segment male urethral stricture disease. Dorsal Buccal Mucosa Graft Urethroplasty (DBMU) is a successful but challenging procedure for the treatment of these strictures. We studied the risk factors that promote failure of dorsal buccal mucosa graft urethroplasty. Material and Methods : We reviewed the records of patients who underwent urethroplasty between Jan 2001 till Oct 2012. Following risk factors were analysed: Host factors: Age (continuous variable), smoking (yes/ no), tobacco chewing (yes/no), past pulmonary Kochs (yes/no), Diabetes yes/ no), Hypertension (yes/no), Ischaemic heart disease (yes/no), Chronic kidney disease (Serum creatinine greater than 1.4 mg/dl at presentation: yes/no), Creatinine at presentation (continuous variable), Stricture Factors : A etiology of stricture: inflammatory (cause like gonorrhoea, other inflammations and catheter induced strictures grouped together as inflammatory) and BXO. duration of symptoms (continuous variable), urethral stricture length (continuous variable), location of the stricture (meatal, submeatal, penile, penobulbar, bulbar, panurethral) T reatment Factors: p rior urethral dilatation (yes/no), prior visual internal urethrotomy (VIU) (yes/no), number of VIUs (continuous variable), prior open surgery for stricture (like meatotomy, meatoplasty, urethroplasty: yes/no), grade of mouth opening, number of graft s needed, quality of the graft s (surgeon ' s assessment as thick or thin graft ), wound infection (yes/no), epididymo-orchitis (yes/ no), urethra-cutaneous fistula (yes/no). The patients were followed periodically. Any obstructive symptom in the patient was further investigated by uroflometry, urethral calibration, retrograde urethrogram and urethrocystoscopy. Any of these either alone or in combination showing re-stricture was considered as treatment failure and all other were considered as successful outcome till the length of follow-up. The outcome was analyzed using the multiple logistic regression. Backward selection method with a likely hood ratio was used for variable entry and removal. Stricture free survival was plotted using Kaplan Meir log rank test curve. We also analysed the association between each risk factor with outcome using fisher exact test. All statistical tests are performed at 5% level of significance. Results : B etween 2001 to 2012, 95 patients underwent dorsal buccal mucosa graft urethroplasty (DBMU). Age: 12 to 70 (Mean: 42.5 yr). Median follow-up was 26 months (range 1 month to 11 years). 56 were inflammatory strictures and 39 were BXO strictures. Stricture recurred in 22 patients (23.2%). The stricture recurrence was seen as early as 2 months and as late as 6 years. Aft er multivariate analysis tobacco chewing, hypertension and prior VIU were predictive of treatment failure. On univariate analysis prior dilatation, number of VIU procedures, quality of graft and Epididymo-orchitis were also seen to be significant predictors of failure using fisher exact test at 5% level of significance. Conclusions : T obacco chewing, hypertension and prior VIU are predictive of failure aft er DBMU. Number of VIU procedures, quality of graft s and epididymo-orchitis may also predict failure aft er DBMU.
