T1  - Ventilator associated pneumonia: A lean six sigma tiger team approach to the collection of high-quality respiratory culture samples
T3  - 40th Annual Conference, APIC 2013. Ft Lauderdale, FL United States.
A1  - Adamson C.A.
A1  - Cooke L.J.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - ISSUE: Ventilator Associated Pneumonia (VAP) is the leading cause of death from hospital-acquired infections and can result in increased mortality, prolonged stays in the intensive care unit, and up to $40,000 in increased cost per occurrence. At our 631-bed university hospital, a VAP reduction program was initiated in 2010. Although an initial decrease in VAP was noted, concerns remained regarding the accuracy of upper versus lower airway culture specimens. Endotracheal (upper airway) aspirates are analyzed semi-quantitatively, and can represent tube colonization. Bronchoscopic or non-bronchoscopic alveolar lavage (lower airway: BAL or mini-BAL) are analyzed quantitatively. The question became, are endotracheal aspirate specimens leading to false positive culture results and negatively impacting our VAP rate? (Figure presented) PROJECT: With the goal of sending higher-quality respiratory culture specimens, the Tiger Team approach from Lean Six Sigma methodology was utilized to change practice. Tiger Teams are multidisciplinary groups of subject-matter experts focused on rapid cycles of improvement. We began with a literature review, and created an adult ICU-wide standardized respiratory culture procedure, including a respiratory culture algorithm (Fig. 1). In the algorithm, providers were prompted to order lower airway samples unless contraindicated. Education was provided to nursing, respiratory care, physicians, and laboratory. Culturing more often than every 72 hourswas discouraged. Barriers to the utilization of BAL or mini-BAL were identified and addressed. Links to policy and algorithms were built into the Electronic Order Entry process (Fig.2). We predicted these changes would lead to a lower, more accurate VAP rate. (Figure presented) RESULTS: Three quarters of data were collected (Fig. 3). Quarter 1 was pre-intervention. Quarter 2 was our transitional quarter, as the practice changes were launched in late April. An aggregate decrease in VAP emerged. Quarter 3 shows continued VAP reduction. LESSON LEARNED: Collecting higher-quality, less frequent respiratory samples has led to a lower VAP rate in six out of seven ICUs. Initial results are encouraging and data collection is ongoing. There was a secondary benefit in that 24% fewer specimens were sent, resulting in further cost savings. The intensive multidisciplinary teamwork required to achieve this clinical practice change yielded an unexpected outcome: the breaking down of silos. Silos occur when organizational departments become isolated from one another, leading to lack of teamwork and communication. This project was exciting in the sense that departments met together at the table with the common goal of putting procedures in place to do the right thing for our patients and for our institution.
