T1  - Are overall adiposity and abdominal adiposity separate or redundant determinants of blood viscosity?
T3  - 17th Conference of the European Society for Clinical Hemorheology and Microcirculation, ESCHM 2013. Pecs Hungary.
A1  - Brun J.F.
A1  - Varlet-Marie E.
A1  - Mauverger E.R.
A1  - Fedou C.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Recent epidemiological evidence leads to think that both general adiposity and abdominal adiposity are associated with the risk of death. We reported in a sample of 430 patients that general adiposity measured by the body mass index BMI) and abdominal adiposity (measured with the waist-to hip ratio WHR) are independent predictors of blood viscosity, related to different determinants of viscosity: for BMI it was explained by its correlation with plasma viscosity and red blood cells (RBC) aggregation; for WHR it was explained by its correlation with hematocrit, while hematocrit was not correlated with BMI. This finding was discussed by the team of A. Vaya (2013 in press) who concluded that abdominal adiposity (as measured with the waist circumference WC and not the WHR) is the only independent determinant while overall adiposity was a dependent variable excluded by the multivariate analysis. To address this issue we re-assessed on our previous database of 43 patients the correlations among viscosity factors, BMI, WHR and WC. Blood viscosity was correlated to BMI (r = 0.155; p = 0.004), WHR (r = 0.364; p = 0.027) and WC (r = 0.094; p = 0.05). Hematocrit was correlated to WHR (r = 0,524) but neither to BMI (r =-0.021) nor waist circumference (r = 0.053). WC was correlated with plasma viscosity (r = 0.154; p = 0.002) while WHR was not (r =-0.0102 NS). A stepwise regression analysis selected two determinants of whole blood viscosity at high shear rate: BMI (p = 0.0167) and WC (p = 0.0003) excluding WHR. Therefore, in this sample, abdominal fatness expressed by WC and whole body adiposity remain independent determinants of blood viscosity, in contrast to Vaya's findings. It is interesting to notice that WHR and WC have not the same meaning, WC measuring the size of abdominal fat while WHR measuring the shape of body distribution regardless the degree of fat excess. Interestingly, hematocrit is rather related to shape (even within a normal range of body size) than the extent of abdominal fatness, and is not related to whole body adiposity. Further studies using more precise measurements of abdominal fat rather that those simple surrogates are needed to clarify these issues.
