T1  - Study of new algorithms for non-invasive staging of liver fibrosis in chronic viral hepatitis (CH) and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)
T3  - 46th Annual Meeting of the Italian Association for the Study of the Liver, A.I.S.F. 2013. Rome Italy.
A1  - Campion D.
A1  - Gaia S.
A1  - Spandre M.
A1  - Brunello F.
A1  - Evangelista A.
A1  - Cosso L.
A1  - Bugianesi E.
A1  - Carenzi S.
A1  - Carucci P.
A1  - Cantamessa A.
A1  - Ciccone G.
A1  - Rizzetto M.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Introduction: Non-invasive assessment of hepatic fibrosis is increasingly challenging liver biopsy (LB). Aim(s): To stage fibrosis in CH and NAFLD by using biochemical, elastographic and ultrasonographic (US) data combined into 2 algorithms. Method(s): overall 123 patients undergoing LB were enrolled (72 CH, 51 NAFLD). Platelet count, AST/ALT, serum CK-18 M30 fragment (available in 36 NAFLD patients), transient elastography (TE) and 17 US liver parameters (by 2 blinded operators) were evaluated within 6 months from LB. Data showing major correlation to histologic stage and best inter-observer agreement were combined into 2 different algorithms, for CH and NAFLD. Collected data were processed in the algorithms allowing fibrosis prediction according to 3 stages: F0-1, F2, F3-4. Predicted stages were compared to Metavir/Brunt stages obtained by LB. CH algorithm was developed on 51 patients, then validated on an independent sample of 21 patients. NAFLD algorithm was developed on 36 patients with available CK-18 measurement using first-operator's US data and validated using second-operator's data. Result(s): The 2 resulting algorithms can be processed by a calculator or displayed in a practical score chart including platelet count, 4 US parameters, TE measurement (and CK-18 in NAFLD). CH algorithm, elaborated on 2 operators' data, correctly staged 61-72% of F0-1 cases, 40-66% of F2, 83-88% of F3-4 in development phase (AUROC 0.81-0.86, 0.77-0.88, 0.88-0.97 respectively). In validation phase it correctly predicted 80%, 33%, 60% of F0-1, F2, F3-4 respectively (AUROC 0.76, 0.43, 0.67). NAFLD algorithm, applied on the 2 operators, correctly staged 81%-87.5%, 50%-67%, 62.5%-75% of F0-1, F2, F3-4 respectively (AUROC 0.87-0.89, 0.72-0.85, 0.77-0.92). Conclusion(s): The 2 proposed algorithms exploit simple and user-friendly data to predict liver fibrosis in CH and NAFLD and show to be promising strategies for patients' clinical management. However, while accuracy is high in detecting absent-mild or advanced fibrosis, it remains lower in discriminating intermediate stages.
