A1  - Pfaller M.A.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - There are five commercial antifungal susceptibility testing systems that are available in Europe for MIC testing of Candida spp. against the systemically active antifungal agents: the Vitek 2 yeast susceptibility test (bioMerieux), the Sensititre YeastOne colorimetric panel (ThermoFisher), the Etest gradient diffusion method (bioMerieux), Micronaut microdilution panel for spectrophotometer reading (Micronaut) and Neosensitabs diffusion method (Rosco). The first three of these are also available in the United States (U.S.) where they account for 40%, 32%, and 12%, respectively, of the antifungal susceptibility testing methods in use in the U.S. in 2013. In Europe data on the proportional use of these systems is not available but Etest and Vitek YeastOne appear to be most commonly used followed by Sensititre. These three systems are also the ones that have been most thoroughly compared to the CLSI and EUCAST reference methods and have shown very good essential agreement (EA; +/-2 log dilutions) when tested against Candida spp. for both amphotericin B, triazoles and echinocandins. However, the performance regarding correct categorization of susceptibility (S, I and R) when the CLSI or EUCAST breakpoints are adopted for endpoint interpretation is less well understood. The development of EUCAST breakpoints and significant changes in the clinical CLSI breakpoints (BPs) for fluconazole, voriconazole and the echinocandins may impact the commercially available MIC systems, all of which were developed at a time when EUCAST breakpoints were not available and CLSI breakpoints were higher. A reanalysis of previously published data using the current breakpoints or epidemiological cut off values for compounds without breakpoints show that both Etest, Vitek and Sensititre methods may still predict susceptibility for most species and antifungal agents but that the testing of some drug bug combinations are associated with a significant risk of misclassification. Therefore, additional challenges using well characterized wild-type (WT) and non-WT strains are necessary. Another issue relates to the fact that at the present time these systems have not been adequately validated with respect to their ability to detect acquired resistance and thus their use in the present era of emerging resistance to both triazoles and echinocandins for the purpose of directing patient therapy is questionable. Strengths and pitfalls for a selected number of these systems, compounds and Candida spp. will be reviewed.
