T1  - Support structure impacts outcomes after VAD implantation
T3  - 32nd Annual Meeting and Scientific Sessions of the International Society for Heart and Lung Transplantation. Prague Czechia.
A1  - Entwistle J.W.
A1  - Cassano C.
A1  - Rowe T.
A1  - Feitell S.C.
A1  - Boateng P.
A1  - Hankins S.R.
A1  - Eisen H.J.
A1  - Petrucci R.J.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Purpose: Determine the impact of support structure on outcomes after VAD implantation. Methods and Materials: All 30-day continuous flow pump survivors (n=32) were retrospectively scored on their ability for independence (AI) and on the level of social support (SS). Pre-AI and SS scores were based on perceived support before implantation, and post-AI and SS scores after implantation once the true AI/SS was evident. Scores were combined to achieve total support (TS). Outcomes were analyzed in relation to AI, SS and TS to look for an association. Causes for readmission were categorized as possibly or unlikely compliance-related. Result(s): There was no association between pre-AI, pre-SS or pre-TS and survival. Only poor post-SS was associated with death on the VAD. No association existed between any measure of support and VAD replacement or VAD infection. Poor scores in each category were associated with increased all-cause readmission days. However, only pre-AI, pre-TS, post-SS and post-TS were associated with readmission for compliancerelated issues. There were significant differences between pre- and post-SS (10 declined, 0 improved) and pre- and post-TS (10 declined, 3 improved), but no difference between pre- and post-AI (6 declined, 3 improved). Conclusion(s): Each patient underwent pre-implant screening similar to that for transplantation. Patients at very high risk for poor compliance or support were denied implantation. In this group of screened patients who underwent implantation, poor support structure was strongly associated with higher readmission rates. Post-SS was associated with a higher risk of death on VAD. The ability of the team to predict strength of SS and TS before implantation was poor. Since outcomes after VAD implantation depend on strength of the social support structure, careful preoperative screening is important and warning signs should not be ignored. (Table presented).
