T1  - The ANDANTE project: Refining risk estimates from neutrons
T3  - 2012 Annual Conference of the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology, ESTRO 31. Barcelona Spain.
A1  - Ottolenghi A.
A1  - Smyth V.
A1  - Trott K.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Neutrons are an unwanted by-product of the use of radiation in many essential activities. Examples are the nuclear power industry and in the nuclear fuels processing and transportation industries, exposure to neutrons in the upper atmosphere during air travel, and neutron exposure of radiotherapy patients undergoing treatment with high energy photons from medical linear accelerators or with hadrons (protons, carbon ions). At present the harmful effects on humans from neutrons are still not well understood. Because the secondary charged particles generated by interactions with energetic neutrons have a higher linear energy transfer (LET) than photons or electrons, the micro- or nanoscopic clusters of ionisation produced within tissue, and particularly within cells, can instigate quite different damage processes, leading to different effects and risks per absorbed dose. The ANDANTE project is funded by Euratom under FP7, and will be carried out by a consortium consisting of 7 European partners and one partner from the USA. It started on 1 January 2012, and will run for 4 years. The purpose of the project is to investigate the relative risk of induction of cancer from exposure to neutrons compared to photons. The project will focus on three specific cancers that may occur as second malignant neoplasms following paediatric photon radiotherapy: salivary gland, thyroid gland, and breast tissue. Stem cells from each of the types of tissue will be exposed to well characterised beams of neutrons, and biological markers of possible tumorigenesis will be used to develop neutron response models. In parallel a track structure model will be developed to simulate the exact experimental conditions and to explore the relationships between exposure parameters and response. The combination of radiobiology and biophysics/systems biology will generate a strongly directed epidemiological investigation to validate the neutron response model, using clinical data from paediatric proton therapy patients. The epidemiological approach will be strengthened by using the discipline for model validation rather than model generation. A prospective multi-centre study will be designed in order to accumulate sufficient statistical power. The overarching objective of the project is to determine values of RBE for neutrons, for specific tissues and neutron energies, which can then be validated using paediatric proton therapy data. Each task within each workpackage of the project forms an essential component part of this objective. The interrelationship is shown in the diagram. The three research workpackages have the following objectives: WP2: Physical characterisation, using measurements and modelling, of the neutron fields of various energy spectra used for experimental irradiation of cells, and generated during paediatric proton radiotherapy for the purpose of accurate dosimetry and track structure simulation and model development of the damage response; WP3: Investigation of the damage induction in stem cells as an indicator of the relative carcinogenic effectiveness of low and intermediate doses from neutrons compared to photons; use of the results to generate provisional values for RBE and explore the dependence on neutron energy and dose; WP4: Integration of functional relationships from biophysical simulations and radiobiological stem cell experiments to develop predictive neutron risk models for second primary malignancies following paediatric proton therapy that can be validated using clinical treatment and follow-up data: proof-of-principle pilot study leading to design of a multi-centre prospective study. The ANDANTE project is expected to produce results that will have an impact on any facet of radiation protection where neutrons are a significant factor. The re-evaluation of RBE for neutrons will provide information of fundamental importance to the ICRP formalism of radiation protection. Information will be posted on the website http://www.andanteproject.eu/(Figure presented).
