T1  - Clinical challenge in chronic hepatitis C topic: Hemoglobinopathy
T3  - Asian Pacific Digestive Week 2012. Bangkok Thailand.
A1  - Pramoolsinsap C.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - The most frequent hemoglobinopathy associated with chronic hepatitis C is thalassemia major and sickle cell disease (SCD), which requires frequent blood transfusions. Among thalassemic patients and SCD, prevalence of HCV infection was 4.4-85.4% and 18.2%, respectively. Either HCV or iron over load (IOL) are independent risk factors for the progression of liver fibrosis in thalassemic patients. Male sex, high ALT, positive HCV-RNA, and high IOL were associated with severe fibrosis, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. SCD differs from thalassemia in terms of interaction between IOL and HCV. In thalassemic patients using Interferon (IFN) monotherapy for 6-15 months induces SVR 40-50%. Ribavirin induce hemolysis and increase the need for transfusions by 30-60%, thus worsening IOL. On ongoing debate regarding the use of combination of IFN (or Peg-IFN) plus ribavirin. Complete SVR has been reported up to 62% in combination therapy vs. 30% using IFN monotherapy. Metaanalysis, Peg-IFN monotherapy induce SVR 28%, while Peg-IFN and low dose ribavirin elicits SVR 44%. Multivariate regression, OR of ribavirin for prediction of SVR was 2.2. SVR was significantly higher in ribavirin group in patients with age > 24 years, elevated ALT, ferritin < 2006 ng/ mL, genotype 1, prior splenectomy, fibrosis score (0-4 HAI) and viral load < 600,000 IU/mL. The rate of hemoglobin decline to below 8 g/dL was comparable between two groups (43% in ribavirin vs. 37% in non-ribavirin group). Current treatment guidelines recommend Peg-IFN and ribavirin therapy for 48 weeks in genotype 1 or 4, and for 24 weeks to in genotype 2 or 3. Patients with SCD and HCV can be treated with Peg-IFN and ribavirin at the usual recommended dose, even in those who are not receiving hydroxyurea.
