T1  - Hepatitis C - New treatment
T3  - Hepatitis C - Neue behandlungen
A1  - Herber A.
A1  - Berg T.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - The results of the Phase III trials evaluating the HCV-specific protease inhibitors telaprevir andboceprevir in combination with peg-interferon- alpha plus ribavirin in patients with HCV type 1 infection are very promising. An increase in sustained virologic response rate of approximaltely 30% was observed in comparison to standard of care. Most important, this increase was achieved although in the majority of telaprevir-treated patients the duration of triple therapy was shortened to 24 weeks. The approval of the new compounds is expected in May 2011 in the United States and in Germany most likely at the end of 2011. A rapid virologic response at week 4 will become the main response criterion during protease inhibitor based regimen and is associated with a chance for cure of approximately 90%. In contrast, patients who at week 4 still have a significant viraemia are at high risk for treatment failure and the development of drug resistance. Treatment algorithm will differ between a boceprevir-based (lead-in and continuous application of the protease inhibitor), and a telaprevir-based regimen (12-week triple followed by 12 week standard of care with no lead-in). Adherence to a every 8 hour regimen will be a further challenge in daily practice, as will be the management of additional side effects, especially anemia (boceprevir, telaprevir) and skin reactions (telaprevir). For non-sustained responder patients, the protease- inhibitors represent a major step forward as well. The individual virologic response pattern to the previous course of standard of care determines the outcome of the proteaseinhibitor based re-treatment which is highest in relapse and lowest in null responder patients. Advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis were also negative predictors for treatment response. Both protease inhibitors do not show significant antiviral efficacy in HCV type 3 (and 4)-infection but for telaprevir a significant antiviral effect was documented in HCV type 2. © Schattauer 2011.
