T1  - Reasons for non-completion of latent tuberculosis infection (LTBI) treatment; a prospective cohort study in the u.s. and canada
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2011. Denver, CO United States.
A1  - Hirsch-Moverman Y.
A1  - Shrestha-Kuwahara R.
A1  - Bethel J.
A1  - Blumberg H.M.
A1  - Munguia G.
A1  - Venkatappa T.
A1  - Vempaty P.
A1  - Colson P.W.
A1  - Horsburgh C.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Rationale: Approximately 200-300,000 individuals are treated for LTBI in the U.S. annually, with reported LTBI treatment completion rates in the U.S. ranging from 20-65% for a 6-month course of self-administered isoniazid (INH) treatment. The objective of our study was to identify modifiable factors associated with completion or non-completion of LTBI therapy. Method(s): This prospective cohort study assessed factors associated with completion of LTBI treatment through interviews with persons who were offered and initiated LTBI treatment. The study was conducted at 12 CDC-funded TB Epidemiologic Studies Consortium study sites. Study definition of treatment completion was based on the INH-prescribed regimen, unless the patient was switched to a different regimen. Interviews were conducted at treatment initiation and again at treatment completion/cessation. Participants received usual care according to each clinic's procedure. Multivariate logistic regression models were constructed based on a manual, stepwise assessment of potential predictors and hypothesized interactions. Result(s): 1,515 participants initiated treatment of LTBI and 1,299 had treatment completion information available in medical charts. Of the 1299, 664 (51%) completed LTBI treatment. In multivariate analysis, predictors of completion of LTBI therapy included having health insurance, male gender, and perceived doctors' competence in treating TB (Table). Unstable housing and drug use were predictors of treatment non-completion; however, drug users with unstable housing were more likely to complete than other drug users or other persons in unstable housing. Expecting to stop LTBI medicines if they made them sick and anticipating problems with the medicines were additional predictors of treatment non-completion. Interviews with non-completers revealed that common reasons for treatment non-completion were patient concerns about tolerability/toxicity, appointment conflicts, low prioritization of TB, and forgetfulness. Conclusion(s): Our study represents a large prospective multi-site study using general clinic populations. Information about the variety of personal, clinical, and societal factors which facilitate or discourage LTBI treatment completion is essential if current goals for the reduction of TB in the US and Canada are to be realized. In our study, half of those who initiated treatment for LTBI completed a course of therapy. Unstable housing, current use of drugs, and fear of toxicity were among factors that predict treatment non-completion. It may be that drug use combined with unstable housing was a predictor of treatment completion because the study included participants who were moved to substance abuse rehabilitation programs after starting LTBI treatment; residence in such programs may have a positive effect on treatment completion.
