T1  - Genotype and duration of therapy are crucial for sustained virological response in chronic hepatitis C patients retreated after relapse following a previous course of pegylated interferon plus ribavirin
T3  - 60th Annual Meeting of the American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases: The Liver Meeting 2009. Boston, MA United States.
A1  - Stern C.
A1  - Martinot-Peignoux M.
A1  - Ripault M.-P.
A1  - Moucari R.
A1  - Asselah T.
A1  - Cardoso A.-C.
A1  - Boyer N.
A1  - Bedossa P.
A1  - Marcellin P.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - BACKGROUND/AIMS: Management of chronic hepatitis C (CHC) patients with failure to antiviral therapy is a challenge nowadays. Reports of retreatment have included nonresponders and relapsers mostly previously treated with the combination of conventional IFN and ribavirin (RBV). The aim of this study was to evaluate the efficacy and factors related to sustained virological response (SVR) in CHC patients who have relapsed after a prior treatment with PEG-IFN in combination with RBV. METHOD(S): Out of 1,228 CHC patients treated with PEG-IFN/RBV, 165 patients had a relapse. Among these, 52 patients were retreated between April 2003 and March 2008 with PEG-IFN alpha-2b or-2a and RBV at a dose of 800 to 1200 mg/day. The duration of retreatment was based on genotype, duration of previous therapy, virological response and tolerance to treatment. Clinical, biological, virological and histological data were collected. Type of PEG-IFN, initial doses and modifications of therapy were analyzed. The efficacy of retreatment was evaluated with a sensitive qualitative HCV RNA assay (<15 IU/mL). Factors associated with SVR were analyzed. RESULT(S): The 52 patients studied had the following baseline characteristics: male gender 73%, mean age 52+/-9 years, mean BMI 26+/-4, genotype 1 48%, HCV RNA higher than 400,000 IU/mL in 49%, METAVIR Activity >=2 in 44%, Fibrosis >=3 in 60% and marked steatosis (>30%) in 40%. 77% of patients received PEG-IFN alpha-2a. Median duration of therapy was 48 weeks (16-120 weeks). Retreatment was at least 24 weeks longer than the previous course in 57%. SVR and relapse were observed in 29% and 46% of patients, respectively. Factors associated with SVR were genotype 2 or 3 (p=0.012), treatment at least 24 weeks longer (p=0.02) and presence of rapid virological response (RVR) (p=0.016). 91% of patients with no RVR did not achieve SVR. Response to treatment was not related to gender (p=0.19), age (p=0.18), BMI (p=0.41), high viral load (p= 0.44) and abnormal ALT at baseline (p=0.24). Necro-inflammatory activity (p=0.32), fibrosis (p=0.53) and steatosis (p=0.50) were not associated with SVR. In addition, type of PEG-IFN and dose modification were not associated with response. In the logistic regression, factors associated with SVR were genotype 2 or 3 (p=0.008) and lengthening of therapy at least 24 weeks (p=0.034). CONCLUSION(S): Retreatment of CHC patients with a relapse after prior PEG-IFN plus RBV is effective in patients with genotype 2 (60% of SVR) or 3 (42%) and little effective in those with genotype 1 (16%). At least 24 weeks longer duration is important to obtain SVR when a retreatment is prescribed. Absence of RVR is a strong predictor of non response.
