T1  - Idiopathic TTP: Predictors of relapse and mortality from the serf(surveillance, epidemiology and risk factors)-TTP group
T3  - 2009 Annual Scientific Symposium of the Hemophilia and Thrombosis Research Society, Inc., HTRS. Chicago, IL United States.
A1  - Zakarija A.
A1  - Yarnold P.
A1  - Kwaan H.
A1  - Luu T.H.
A1  - Pandey D.
A1  - Cursio J.
A1  - Bandarenko N.
A1  - Richey E.A.
A1  - Weiss I.
A1  - Bennett C.L.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Objective: Thrombotic thrombocytopenia purpura (TTP) is a rare microvascular occlusive disorder characterized by systemic or renal platelet aggregates, thrombocytopenia and microangiopathic hemolytic anemia. Although survival in patients with TTP has improved with therapeutic plasma exchange (TPE), early mortality and relapses occur. This study sought to identify variables associated with TTP relapse and mortality. Sample: A total of 92 patients were recruited prospectively through the national SERFTTP consortium from 2002 to 2007. Patients were enrolled at time of first episode of idiopathic TTP. Exclusion criteria included history of solid organ transplant, cancer, or use of chemotherapy or calcineurin inhibitors. Method(s): Clinical, laboratory and treatment data were collected at presentation and follow-up. ADAMTS13 activity and inhibitor were measured on plasma samples collected at presentation and remission. Outcomes evaluated were 30 day relapse, longterm relapse and 30 day mortality. Hierarchically optimal classification tree analysis (CTA) was used to construct a non-linear multi-attribute 'tree' model for predicting outcome variables. Result(s): A total of 92 cases of idiopathic TTP, had mean age of 42, and 78% were female. ADAMTS13 deficiency (<15%) was present in 52% of cases. Relapses in the first 30 days occurred in 21% of patients. Higher relapse rates were seen in patients with speech abnormalities at presentation or if adverse reactions to TPE occurred. Long-term relapse occurred in 25% (14/56) of patients, and was associated with severe ADAMTS13 deficiency, high-titer functional ADAMTS13 inhibitor, severe thrombocytopenia (platelet: <27 000), normal creatinine and neurologic abnormalities. No patients with a platelet count >27 000 lL-1 relapsed. 30-day mortality for the entire cohort was 10%. CTA model for mortality identified cognitive deficit as the root attribute. Thirty day mortality was 31% (4/13) in patients who presented with a cognitive deficit (P<0.008). If cognitive deficit was not present then mortality was 50% (1/2) when Rose-Eldor (RE) score was >5 (P<0.048) but 0% (0/38) if RE score was <5. Conclusion(s): Cognitive deficits at presentation with idiopathic TTP are associated with higher mortality, and early and late relapses. Critically assessing patient's neurologic status is important in TTP as it may have prognostic significance.
