T1  - Simple physiological measurements as predictors of outcome in experimental intraabdominal infection
A1  - Jonsson B.
A1  - Berglund J.
A1  - Skau T.
A1  - Nystrom P.O.
Y1  - 1990//
N2  - In a porcine model, peritonitis was induced by intraperitoneal injection of E. coli and B. fragilis or by bacteria combined with sterile faeces. From eight variables (rectal temperature, mean blood pressure, pulse rate, arterial PO2 and pH, hematocrit, white blood count and urine production) a score system was construed to quantify physiological alterations during severe bacterial peritonitis. We also aimed at obtaining prior information about outcome. Measurements were made between 4 and 6 hours. The experiments were terminated after 24 hours. In the infected surviving animals the mean score was 5.5 (s.d. +/- 4.0) while in the non-surviving group the mean was 14.6 (s.d. +/- 5.1, p < 0.01). The score divided the animals into those with a score >= 10, where 9 out of 12 died, while in animals with less than 10 only one out of 14 died. No correlation could be seen between score level and the capacity of clearing bacteria from the peritoneal cavity, nor could any significant relationship between infecting dose and score level be demonstrated. It was therefore concluded that the quantification of the physiological response carried more information about outcome, and that it adds an explanation to outcome that is not entirely related to the dosage of the challenging agent.
