T1  - Prognosis after splenectomy. A review of 322 cases
A1  - Macpherson A.I.S.
Y1  - 1981//
N2  - The results of splenectomy are best when it removes a local source of blood cell destruction or sequestration and when the primary disease is thereby rendered less hurtful. This combination occurs in congenital spherocytosis in which splenectomy is almost certain to be followed by relief of the haemolytic anaemia and by normal red blood cell survival, though the defect in erythropoiesis is unaffected. In autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, even in cases carefully selected on clinical grounds and after radioisotope tests, the results are less predictable, but the chances of improvement at least to the extent of reducing the dose of corticosteroids to an acceptable 'non-toxic' level are more than 60%. Once hypersplenism becomes manifest, it persists and can be relieved only by splenectomy. The prognosis for survival varies with the underlying disease and may not be greatly influenced by splenectomy, but the relief of the clinical handicaps associated with the hypersplenism unquestionably improves the quality of that survival. Myelofibrosis and Felty's syndrome present difficult problems of assessment. In Felty's syndrome relief of hypersplenism is usually unequivocal but the changes in the immunoglobulins associated with the rheumatoid state and possibly following the splenectomy can be associated with unexpectedly severe and sometimes lethal infection in the months immediately after operation. The outlook in myelofibrosis is gloomy however it is viewed, and for the reasons I have given surgical intervention is not sought until the disease is getting out of medical control. In a few cases splenectomy hastens a fatal outcome by removing a major source of granulopoiesis and thrombocytopoiesis and these are at present unpredictable, but in more than half in our experience the requirements for blood transfusion have been reduced and the patient's life has been made more comfortable, though seldom for long. In ITP symptomatic improvement can be confidently expected though a return of the platelet count permanently to normal occurs in only some 75% of all cases. Recurrent thrombocytopenia is much less likely if only a short interval is allowed to elapse between the onset of symptoms and the performance of splenectomy. That there are risks associated with the operation of splenectomy is undeniable. Some of these are inherent in the primary disease for which the operation is done but careful selection and preparation of patients and avoidance of splenectomy in the very young will go a long way to reducing the risks, particularly that of infection. A cooperating and mutually understanding team of physicians, surgeons and clinical scientists can restore a happy life and sometimes also a longer life to many patients.
